          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p_{XYZ}$ be a joint probability vector on finite alphabets and write $p_Z(z)$ for its marginal. It is the law of finite coordinate random elements on the canonical finite probability space from [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]] and [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]. For each $z$ with $p_Z(z)>0$, the conditional law $p_{XY|z}$ and its marginals $p_{X|z},p_{Y|z}$ are probability vectors by [[lem-it-finite-marginals-and-conditionals-are-pmfs]]. Define conditional mutual information in nats by\n\n$$I(X;Y|Z):=\\sum_{z:p_Z(z)>0}p_Z(z)D(p_{XY|z}\\|p_{X|z}\\otimes p_{Y|z}),$$\n\nusing the finite KL convention of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]], where $(p_{X|z}\\otimes p_{Y|z})(x,y):=p_{X|z}(x)p_{Y|z}(y)$. A positive conditional joint mass forces both conditional marginals to be positive, so each displayed KL term is finite. Zero-mass conditioning values contribute no term. The base-$b$ version replaces $\\ln$ by $\\log_b$ inside the conditional KL sums.",
