          "claim": "Suppose $P\\ll Q$, let $r=dP/dQ$, and set $\\ell=\\log r$ on $\\{r>0\\}$, with $\\ell=-\\infty$ on $\\{r=0\\}$; the latter set is P-null. Define $f_n=\\max(-n,\\min(n,\\ell))$, taking $f_n=-n$ on $\\{r=0\\}$. The negative part of $\\ell$ is integrable by [F1]; its clipped negative parts converge by [F4], while the clipped positive parts increase to $\\ell^+$ and converge in integral by [F4]. Hence $\\int f_n\\,dP\\to D(P\\|Q)$, including value $+\\infty$. Pointwise, $e^{f_n}\\to r$ Q-a.e. and $0\\le e^{f_n}\\le r+1$, so [F4] gives $\\int e^{f_n}dQ\\to\\int r\\,dQ=1$. Therefore $\\Phi_{P,Q}(f_n)\\to D(P\\|Q)$ and the bounded-function supremum is at least the divergence.",
