          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-kullback-leibler-is-nonnegative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC and use the directional convention of\n[[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]]. For probability measures $P,Q$ on one\nmeasurable space, $D(P\\|Q)\\ge0$, and $D(P\\|Q)=0$ if and only if $P=Q$.\n\nThere is also the following normalized-density identity. Let\n$(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space: it is a set\nwith a sigma-algebra and measure ([[def-measure-space]]), and there is a\ncountable measurable cover by sets of finite $\\mu$-measure\n([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]). Let\n$\\gamma:\\mathsf X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a finite-valued measurable function with\n$Z:=\\int\\gamma\\,d\\mu\\in(0,\\infty)$, and put $\\pi=\\gamma/Z$. Let\n$q:\\mathsf X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a finite-valued measurable probability density,\nso $\\int q\\,d\\mu=1$, and suppose $q=0$ $\\mu$-almost everywhere on\n$\\{\\gamma=0\\}$. Assume\n$$\\int q\\bigl(|\\log\\gamma|+|\\log q|\\bigr)\\,d\\mu<\\infty,$$\nwhere each term with $q=0$ is defined to be zero. Then\n$$\\log Z =\\int q(\\log\\gamma-\\log q)\\,d\\mu +D(q\\,\\mu\\|\\pi\\,\\mu).$$\nIn particular, the first integral is at most $\\log Z$. This identity concerns\nnormalized densities only; it does not assert posterior existence for an\nunspecified statistical model.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-measure-theoretic-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(E,\\mathcal E)$ and $(F,\\mathcal F)$ be measurable spaces and let $P$ be a probability measure on $(E\\times F,\\mathcal E\\otimes\\mathcal F)$. Its marginals are $P_E(A)=P(A\\times F)$ and $P_F(B)=P(E\\times B)$. The product probability $P_E\\otimes P_F$ exists by the finite product-measure theorem. Define the **measure-theoretic mutual information** of the joint law $P$ by\n$$I(P):=D(P\\|P_E\\otimes P_F)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor random elements $X,Y$ with joint law $P_{XY}$, write $I(X;Y):=I(P_{XY})$. The logarithm convention is the natural logarithm, so finite values are in nats as fixed by [[def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base]]. The divergence is directional in its two law arguments and is $+\\infty$ when $P_{XY}\\not\\ll P_X\\otimes P_Y$.\n\nThis definition uses only the joint law, not a choice of density or a difference of entropy quantities.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-standard-borel-conditional-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let X,Y,Z be random elements with standard-Borel value spaces and joint law $P_{XYZ}$. Let $K(z,\\cdot)$ and $L(z,\\cdot)$ be regular conditional laws of X and Y given Z=z. Let R be the probability law on the product space whose rectangle values are\n$$R(C\\times A\\times B)=\\int_C K(z,A)L(z,B)\\,P_Z(dz).$$\nIts existence, uniqueness, and independence of the versions K,L are established in [[lem-same-marginal-conditional-product-law-well-defined]]. Define the **conditional mutual information** by\n$$I(X;Y\\mid Z):=D(P_{XYZ}\\|R)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nThe reference R has the same Z marginal as the joint law and makes X,Y independent conditionally on Z. The logarithm is natural, so finite values are measured in nats under [[def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base]]. The definition is a divergence between full probability laws; it does not subtract two possibly infinite entropies.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-conditional-independence-given-a-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the axiom of choice so that the library's conditional-expectation\nclasses are available. Let $Y$ and $Z$ be random elements and let\n$\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ be a sigma-algebra. We say that $Y$ and $Z$ are\n**conditionally independent given $\\mathcal G$**, and write\n$Y\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Z\\mid\\mathcal G$, if for every pair of bounded measurable\nreal functions $f,g$,\n$$ \\mathbb E[f(Y)g(Z)\\mid\\mathcal G] =\\mathbb E[f(Y)\\mid\\mathcal G]\\, \\mathbb E[g(Z)\\mid\\mathcal G]\\quad\\text{a.s.} $$\nThis is an equality of almost-everywhere classes and hence does not depend on\nrepresentatives.\n\nFor sigma-algebras $\\mathcal H_1,\\mathcal H_2\\subseteq\\mathcal F$, the notation\n$\\mathcal H_1\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp\\mathcal H_2\\mid\\mathcal G$ means the same identity\nfor every bounded $\\mathcal H_1$-measurable $U$ and bounded\n$\\mathcal H_2$-measurable $V$.\n\nThe definition is symmetric. If $\\mathcal G=\\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\}$ modulo\nnull sets, it reduces to ordinary independence. If one side is\n$\\mathcal G$-measurable, conditional independence is automatic because that\nfactor is already known when conditioning. The choices of the zero function or\nthe constant-one function cause no exceptional case.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-conditional-expectation-given-a-sigma-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ be a probability space, $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$ a sub-sigma-algebra, and $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ integrable. A **conditional-expectation version of $X$ given $\\mathcal G$** is a real, $\\mathcal G$-measurable, integrable function $Y$ such that $\\int_A Y\\,dP=\\int_A X\\,dP$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal G$. No completeness of $\\mathcal G$ is assumed.\n\nExpectation means the integral of [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]]. Integrable representatives use [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]; quotient notation is introduced after uniqueness.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-conditional-fatou-and-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. For nonnegative measurable $X_n$, $E[\\liminf_n X_n\\mid\\mathcal G]\\le\\liminf_n E[X_n\\mid\\mathcal G]$ almost surely, in the extended sense. If real-valued measurable $X_n$ and $X$ satisfy $X_n\\to X$ almost surely and $|X_n|\\le W$ almost surely for one nonnegative $W\\in L^1(P)$, then $E[X_n\\mid\\mathcal G]\\to E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]$ almost surely and in $L^1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B)$ be measurable spaces. Their\n**product sigma-algebra** is\n\n$$\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B := \\sigma(\\{A \\times B : A \\in \\mathcal A,\\ B \\in \\mathcal B\\}),$$\n\nthe sigma-algebra on $X \\times Y$ generated by the measurable rectangles of\n[[def-measurable-rectangle]].\n\nFor the empty family, put $\\prod_{i<0}X_i:=\\{()\\}$ and define its **empty\nproduct sigma-algebra** by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<0}\\mathcal A_i:=\\{\\varnothing,\\{()\\}\\}.$$\n\nFor a nonempty finite family of measurable spaces $(X_i,\\mathcal A_i)$ with\n$i<n$, the **finite product sigma-algebra** is defined recursively by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<1}\\mathcal A_i := \\mathcal A_0,\\qquad \\bigotimes_{i<n+1}\\mathcal A_i := \\left(\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathcal A_i\\right)\\otimes\\mathcal A_n.$$\n\nThis recursive definition is the finite base case used later when the library\npasses to countable product constructions.",
