          "claim": "Let $(\\mathcal G_n)$ be any increasing sequence of sub-sigma-algebras generating $\\mathcal G$, and let $\\rho=P+Q$ for probability measures on $\\mathcal G$. The union $\\mathcal U=\\bigcup_n\\mathcal G_n$ is an algebra. The class of $A\\in\\mathcal G$ approximable in $\\rho$-measure by a set in $\\mathcal U$ is a sigma-algebra: complements preserve the symmetric-difference measure, finite unions preserve approximation, and for a countable union one first truncates it using continuity from below [F5] of the finite measure $\\rho$, then approximates the finite union. Hence every set in $\\mathcal G$ is approximable. Approximating bounded measurable functions first by simple functions and then their level sets gives bounded $\\mathcal U$-measurable functions converging in $L^1(\\rho)$, with one $\\mathcal G_n$ containing each finite simple approximation. The approximants may be clipped to the same bound M. On that bound, [F2]'s variational functional is continuous under $L^1(\\rho)$ approximation: $e^t$ is Lipschitz on $[-M,M]$, and the exponential integral is bounded below by $e^{-M}$. By [F2] applied to $P,Q$ and their restrictions to each $\\mathcal G_n$, the supremum over all bounded $\\mathcal G_n$-measurable functions is $D(P|_{\\mathcal G_n}\\|Q|_{\\mathcal G_n})$. These test classes increase, so the restricted divergences increase; the approximation shows their supremum equals the unrestricted variational supremum $D(P\\|Q)$. Hence $D(P\\|Q)=\\lim_n D(P|_{\\mathcal G_n}\\|Q|_{\\mathcal G_n})$.",
