          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-measure-theoretic-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(E,\\mathcal E)$ and $(F,\\mathcal F)$ be measurable spaces and let $P$ be a probability measure on $(E\\times F,\\mathcal E\\otimes\\mathcal F)$. Its marginals are $P_E(A)=P(A\\times F)$ and $P_F(B)=P(E\\times B)$. The product probability $P_E\\otimes P_F$ exists by the finite product-measure theorem. Define the **measure-theoretic mutual information** of the joint law $P$ by\n$$I(P):=D(P\\|P_E\\otimes P_F)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor random elements $X,Y$ with joint law $P_{XY}$, write $I(X;Y):=I(P_{XY})$. The logarithm convention is the natural logarithm, so finite values are in nats as fixed by [[def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base]]. The divergence is directional in its two law arguments and is $+\\infty$ when $P_{XY}\\not\\ll P_X\\otimes P_Y$.\n\nThis definition uses only the joint law, not a choice of density or a difference of entropy quantities.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-it-finite-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p_{XY}$ be a joint probability vector on finite alphabets $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$, with marginals $p_X(x):=\\sum_y p_{XY}(x,y)$ and $p_Y(y):=\\sum_xp_{XY}(x,y)$. The marginals are probability vectors by [[lem-it-finite-marginals-and-conditionals-are-pmfs]]. The joint vector is the outcome-weight function of the canonical finite probability space from [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]; its coordinate maps are finite random elements in the sense of [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]. Define mutual information in nats by\n\n$$I(X;Y):=D(p_{XY}\\|p_X\\otimes p_Y)=\\sum_{(x,y):p_{XY}(x,y)>0}p_{XY}(x,y)\\ln\\frac{p_{XY}(x,y)}{p_X(x)p_Y(y)},$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite KL convention of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]] and $(p_X\\otimes p_Y)(x,y):=p_X(x)p_Y(y)$. Every ratio in the displayed sum is positive and finite: if $p_{XY}(x,y)>0$, then each marginal sum is at least that positive entry. The sum is over finite support, so $I(X;Y)$ is finite. The base-$b$ version replaces $\\ln$ by $\\log_b$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-it-finite-kl-agrees-with-measurable-kl",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC only for the interface to the published general definition [[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]]. Let $p,q$ be probability vectors on a finite set $S$, and let $P(A)=\\sum_{i\\in A}p_i$, $Q(A)=\\sum_{i\\in A}q_i$ on $\\mathcal P(S)$. Then the measurable-space divergence $D(P\\|Q)$ equals the finite KL sum $\\sum_{i:p_i>0}p_i\\ln(p_i/q_i)$, with value $+\\infty$ exactly when some $p_i>0=q_i$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Write $\\ln$ for the natural logarithm $\\log$ of [[def-natural-logarithm]]. Unless a base is displayed, every information quantity on this page uses $\\ln$ and is measured in **nats**. For a base $b>1$ and $t>0$, define\n$$\\log_b t:=\\frac{\\ln t}{\\ln b}.$$\nThe denominator is positive: $b>1$, $\\ln$ is strictly increasing, and $\\ln 1=0$ by [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]. A base-$b$ entropy or divergence is obtained by replacing each $\\ln$ in the finite formulas below by $\\log_b$; use $0\\log_b0:=0$ and assign $+\\infty$ to a positive numerator over a zero denominator. The case $b=2$ measures **bits**; $b=e$ gives nats.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite nonempty set identified with $[k]=\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$, a **probability vector** is $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^k$ with $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$; it is the outcome-weight vector of a finite probability space ([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]). Its Shannon entropy in natural units is\n$$H(p):=-\\sum_{i=1}^k p_i\\ln p_i,$$\nwith $0\\ln0:=0$. For probability vectors $p,q$ on the same set $[k]$, the relative entropy is\n$$D(p\\|q):=\\sum_{i:p_i>0}p_i\\ln(p_i/q_i),$$\nwith value $+\\infty$ if $p_i>0=q_i$ for some $i$. A matrix $A\\in[0,1]^{N\\times m}$ is **row-stochastic** when each row sums to one. For a probability row vector $p$ on $[m]$ and a row vector $X\\in[0,1]^m$, the dot product $pX^{\\mathsf T}=\\sum_{i=1}^m p_iX_i$ is the acceptance probability under distribution $p$.\n\nThese definitions use finite sets only; entropy is between $0$ and $\\ln k$, as proved where needed below.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
