          "claim": "On $\\{0,1\\}$, define $d(x,y)=0$ if $x=y$ and $d(x,y)=1$ otherwise; distinct endpoints force at least one of the two intermediate distances to be 1, so the triangle inequality holds. For a Cauchy sequence, tolerance $1/2$ forces all sufficiently late terms to be equal, so the metric is complete. The finite set itself is a finite dense subset, and radius-$1/2$ balls are singletons, so the topology is discrete. By the definitions in [F4], this is a Polish presentation of the discrete sigma-algebra, which is therefore standard Borel. If $p=0$ or $p=1$, X and Y are the same constant almost surely; their joint law and the product of their marginals are the same point mass. Thus [F1] gives a finite-alphabet mutual information, [F2] gives $I(X;Y)=0$, and [F3] gives the same entropy.",
