        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "If $t=1$, then $\\ln t=t-1=0$. Otherwise let $a=\\min\\{1,t\\}$ and $b=\\max\\{1,t\\}$. By [F2] and [F3], $\\ln$ is continuous on $[a,b]$; by [F5], it is differentiable on $(a,b)$ with derivative $1/u$. Applying [F6] gives $c\\in(a,b)$ with $\\ln t-\\ln1=(t-1)/c$, so $\\ln t=(t-1)/c$ by [F4]. If $t>1$, then $c>1$; if $0<t<1$, then $0<c<1$ and $t-1<0$. In either case $(t-1)/c<t-1$. Thus $\\ln t\\le t-1$, with equality only at $t=1$.",
