    "thm-mcmillan-inequality-for-finite-binary-codes": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-binary-source-code-and-extension",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **binary word** is a finite sequence of symbols from $\\{0,1\\}$. The set $\\{0,1\\}^*$ includes the empty word $\\varepsilon$, and $\\{0,1\\}^+$ is the set of nonempty binary words. A **binary source code** for the finite source $\\mathcal X=[m]$ is a function $C:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}^+$. Its codeword length is $\\ell_i:=|C(i)|\\ge1$.\n\nThe **extension** $C^*:\\mathcal X^*\\to\\{0,1\\}^*$ maps the empty source word to $\\varepsilon$ and maps $i_1\\cdots i_n$ to $C(i_1)\\cdots C(i_n)$. The code is **uniquely decodable** if $C^*$ is injective, including comparisons between source words of different lengths. Its expected length for source law $p$ is\n$$L_p(C):=\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i\\ell_i.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-integer-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$m \\;\\le\\; x \\;<\\; m + 1 .$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $K:=\\sum_{i=1}^m2^{-\\ell_i}$ and $\\lambda:=\\max_i\\ell_i$. For each integer $k\\ge1$, expand $K^k=\\sum_{(i_1,\\ldots,i_k)\\in[m]^k}2^{-(\\ell_{i_1}+\\cdots+\\ell_{i_k})}$. For each total length $s$, let $N_{k,s}$ count the tuples with $\\ell_{i_1}+\\cdots+\\ell_{i_k}=s$. Their concatenated codewords are distinct by [F1] and each is a binary word of length $s$, of which there are exactly $2^s$ because each of the $s$ positions has two choices; hence $N_{k,s}\\le2^s$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Since every $\\ell_i\\ge1$, the only possible totals satisfy $k\\le s\\le k\\lambda$, so there are $k(\\lambda-1)+1$ possible integer values of $s$. Grouping step 1.1 by $s$ gives $K^k=\\sum_{s=k}^{k\\lambda}N_{k,s}2^{-s}\\le k(\\lambda-1)+1$. Therefore $K\\le(k(\\lambda-1)+1)^{1/k}$ for every $k$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $K>1$, set $\\epsilon=K-1>0$, and put $a=\\lambda-1\\ge0$. By [F2], the integer $k:=\\lfloor1+2a/\\epsilon^2\\rfloor+1$ satisfies $k>1+2a/\\epsilon^2$ and $k\\ge2$, so $k(k-1)\\epsilon^2/2>ak$. The binomial theorem gives $K^k=(1+\\epsilon)^k\\ge1+k\\epsilon+\\binom{k}{2}\\epsilon^2>1+ak$, contradicting step 2.1, which gives $K^k\\le ak+1$. Hence $K\\le1$.",
