    "ex-uniquely-decodable-code-not-prefix": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-binary-source-code-and-extension",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **binary word** is a finite sequence of symbols from $\\{0,1\\}$. The set $\\{0,1\\}^*$ includes the empty word $\\varepsilon$, and $\\{0,1\\}^+$ is the set of nonempty binary words. A **binary source code** for the finite source $\\mathcal X=[m]$ is a function $C:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}^+$. Its codeword length is $\\ell_i:=|C(i)|\\ge1$.\n\nThe **extension** $C^*:\\mathcal X^*\\to\\{0,1\\}^*$ maps the empty source word to $\\varepsilon$ and maps $i_1\\cdots i_n$ to $C(i_1)\\cdots C(i_n)$. The code is **uniquely decodable** if $C^*$ is injective, including comparisons between source words of different lengths. Its expected length for source law $p$ is\n$$L_p(C):=\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i\\ell_i.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-binary-prefix-code",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A binary code $C$ is a **prefix code** if it is nonsingular and no codeword $C(i)$ is a proper prefix of a different codeword $C(j)$. A word $u$ is a proper prefix of $v$ when $v=uw$ for some nonempty binary word $w$. Nonsingular means $C(i)\\ne C(j)$ whenever $i\\ne j$.\n\nThe **code tree** is the finite rooted trie whose vertices are all prefixes of codewords, whose root is $\\varepsilon$, and whose edges append one bit, labeled $0$ or $1$. Since the code is prefix-free, its codewords are exactly the leaves; unused branches are absent. A binary word path from the root records its successive edge labels.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-mcmillan-inequality-for-finite-binary-codes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C$ be a finite binary uniquely decodable code with lengths $\\ell_i$. Then\n$$\\sum_{i=1}^m2^{-\\ell_i}\\le1.$$",
          "uses": [
