        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "alg-binary-huffman-coding",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Input:** A finite full-support source $(m,p)$ as in [[def-finite-discrete-source-and-blocks]], with the symbols ordered $1,\\ldots,m$.\n\n**Output:** A binary prefix code $C_H$ on these symbols.\n\nIf $m=1$, return the one-bit codeword $C_H(1)=0$.\n\nIf $m\\ge2$, make one active leaf for each source symbol $i$, with weight $p_i$ and serial number $i$. Repeatedly do the following while more than one node is active:\n\n1. Order active nodes lexicographically by (weight, serial number), using the ordinary order on real weights and then integer serials.\n2. Remove the first two nodes. Make a new parent whose left child is the first removed node and whose right child is the second, whose weight is their sum, and whose serial is the next unused integer.\n3. Insert this parent among the active nodes.\n\nWhen one root remains, label every left edge $0$ and every right edge $1$. The codeword for symbol $i$ is the edge-label sequence on the path from the root to its original leaf. The order by serial number fixes all ties and fixes the bit orientation. The same merge rule applies to any finite active list of positive weights carrying distinct serial numbers; the source input initializes the serials as $1,\\ldots,m$, and each new parent receives a fresh serial.\n\nFor each $m\\ge2$, the algorithm executes exactly $m-1$ merges because each merge decreases the number of active nodes by one. The returned tree is finite. The next theorem proves the code is prefix-free and optimal; no complexity bound is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-binary-source-code-and-extension",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **binary word** is a finite sequence of symbols from $\\{0,1\\}$. The set $\\{0,1\\}^*$ includes the empty word $\\varepsilon$, and $\\{0,1\\}^+$ is the set of nonempty binary words. A **binary source code** for the finite source $\\mathcal X=[m]$ is a function $C:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}^+$. Its codeword length is $\\ell_i:=|C(i)|\\ge1$.\n\nThe **extension** $C^*:\\mathcal X^*\\to\\{0,1\\}^*$ maps the empty source word to $\\varepsilon$ and maps $i_1\\cdots i_n$ to $C(i_1)\\cdots C(i_n)$. The code is **uniquely decodable** if $C^*$ is injective, including comparisons between source words of different lengths. Its expected length for source law $p$ is\n$$L_p(C):=\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i\\ell_i.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-base-two-entropy-of-a-finite-source",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite source $p$ of [[def-finite-discrete-source-and-blocks]], define its **base-two Shannon entropy**, measured in bits, by\n$$H_2(p):=\\frac{H(p)}{\\ln 2}=-\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i\\log_2 p_i.$$\nHere $H(p)=-\\sum_i p_i\\ln p_i$ is the finite Shannon entropy in natural units from [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]]. By [[def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base]], information quantities use natural logarithms by default and $\\log_2 t=\\ln(t)/\\ln 2$. Substituting this identity termwise and using $\\sum_i p_i=1$ gives the displayed equality. Every $p_i$ is positive by the source convention, so no zero-term convention is needed here.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-huffman-code-lengths-are-within-one-bit-of-entropy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $C_H$ be the code returned by [[alg-binary-huffman-coding]] for a finite full-support source $p$. If $m\\ge2$, then\n$$H_2(p)\\le L_p(C_H)<H_2(p)+1.$$\nIf $m=1$, then $H_2(p)=0$ and $L_p(C_H)=1$.",
