        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-finite-support-binary-prefix-code",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a finite nonempty message set. A **binary prefix code on $M$** is an injective assignment $c:M\\to\\{0,1\\}^*$ such that no assigned string is a proper initial segment of another assigned string. Write $\\ell(m):=|c(m)|$ for its codeword lengths. For a probability vector $p$ on $M$, its expected length is\n$$L_p(c):=\\sum_{m\\in M}p(m)\\ell(m).$$\n\nThe empty string is permitted only when $M$ is a singleton. In that case it describes one known source block, or a known number of copies of the sole message; the message count is part of the framing convention. For arbitrary concatenations whose message count is not supplied, require every codeword to be nonempty. Thus a singleton source can retain a zero-bit code for one known block without claiming unknown-count decoding.",
