          "claim": "The product of the source system and the iid marker-seed shift is ergodic. To verify this, take two bounded real-valued rectangle cylinder functions $f_1(X)g_1(B,U)$ and $f_2(X)g_2(B,U)$. For all sufficiently large shifts $n$, the iid-coordinate windows of $g_1$ and $g_2\\circ S^n$ are disjoint, so their expectation factors as $\\mathbb E g_1\\,\\mathbb E g_2$. For the source factor, [F7] gives almost-sure convergence of $N^{-1}\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}f_2(T^nX)$ to a finite invariant function $f_2^*$. Ergodicity makes $f_2^*$ constant almost surely: for each rational $a$, the limsup orbit set $\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}T^{-n}\\{f_2^*>a\\}$ is strictly invariant and differs from $\\{f_2^*>a\\}$ by a null set, so it has probability zero or one; the rational level sets then force a constant value. Since $f_2$ is bounded, dominated convergence and preservation of the source law show this constant is $\\mathbb E f_2$. Thus the time averages of $f_2$ converge almost surely to $\\mathbb E f_2$, and bounded convergence gives $$ \\frac1N\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\\mathbb E[f_1(X)f_2(T^nX)]\\longrightarrow\\mathbb E f_1\\,\\mathbb E f_2. $$ The Cesàro correlations of the rectangle functions therefore converge to the product of their means. Their finite linear combinations are dense in real product $L^2$: indicators of measurable rectangles form a generating $\\pi$-system, and the class of sets whose indicators lie in the $L^2$-closure of their span is a Dynkin class, since increasing unions converge in $L^2$ on a probability space. The $\\pi$-$\\lambda$ theorem and density of simple functions give the claim. Cauchy–Schwarz and shift invariance extend the correlation limit to every real $L^2$ function. If $A$ is invariant, its indicator has correlation $\\mathbb P(A)$ at every shift, while the limit is $\\mathbb P(A)^2$; hence $\\mathbb P(A)\\in\\{0,1\\}$.",
