    "lem-discrete-memoryless-channel-capacity-is-attained": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-discrete-memoryless-channel-information-capacity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an input probability vector $P_X$ on $\\mathcal X$ ([[def-finite-probability-vector]]), let $P_{XY}(x,y):=P_X(x)W(y\\mid x)$ and $Q_{P_X}(y):=\\sum_xP_X(x)W(y\\mid x)$. Write $I(P_X;W):=I(X;Y)$ for this joint law, using [[def-it-finite-mutual-information]]. The **single-letter information capacity** is\n$$C(W):=\\sup_{P_X} I(P_X;W),$$\nmeasured in nats per channel use. The finite-dimensional maximum is attained, as proved in [[lem-discrete-memoryless-channel-capacity-is-attained]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-it-finite-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p_{XY}$ be a joint probability vector on finite alphabets $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$, with marginals $p_X(x):=\\sum_y p_{XY}(x,y)$ and $p_Y(y):=\\sum_xp_{XY}(x,y)$. The marginals are probability vectors by [[lem-it-finite-marginals-and-conditionals-are-pmfs]]. The joint vector is the outcome-weight function of the canonical finite probability space from [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]; its coordinate maps are finite random elements in the sense of [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]. Define mutual information in nats by\n\n$$I(X;Y):=D(p_{XY}\\|p_X\\otimes p_Y)=\\sum_{(x,y):p_{XY}(x,y)>0}p_{XY}(x,y)\\ln\\frac{p_{XY}(x,y)}{p_X(x)p_Y(y)},$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite KL convention of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]] and $(p_X\\otimes p_Y)(x,y):=p_X(x)p_Y(y)$. Every ratio in the displayed sum is positive and finite: if $p_{XY}(x,y)>0$, then each marginal sum is at least that positive entry. The sum is over finite support, so $I(X;Y)$ is finite. The base-$b$ version replaces $\\ln$ by $\\log_b$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-it-finite-entropy-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For finite $X,Y,Z$ with a joint pmf,\n\n$$H(X,Y|Z)=H(X|Z)+H(Y|X,Z),$$\n\nand\n\n$$I(X;Y,Z)=I(X;Z)+I(X;Y|Z).$$\n\nIn particular, $I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X|Y)=H(Y)-H(Y|X)$. All terms are finite.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-it-finite-conditional-entropy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p_{XY}$ be a joint probability vector on finite alphabets $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$. It is the weight function of the canonical finite probability space from [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]; its coordinate maps are finite random elements in the sense of [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]. Write $p_Y(y)=\\sum_xp_{XY}(x,y)$. For each $y$ with $p_Y(y)>0$, set $p_{X|y}(x)=p_{XY}(x,y)/p_Y(y)$; this is a probability vector by [[lem-it-finite-marginals-and-conditionals-are-pmfs]]. Define the conditional entropy in nats by\n\n$$H(X|Y):=\\sum_{y:p_Y(y)>0}p_Y(y)H(p_{X|y}),$$\n\nwhere $H(p)=-\\sum_xp(x)\\ln p(x)$ is the finite entropy of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]]. Terms for zero-mass $y$ are omitted. The base-$b$ version replaces $\\ln$ by $\\log_b$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite nonempty set identified with $[k]=\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$, a **probability vector** is $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^k$ with $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$; it is the outcome-weight vector of a finite probability space ([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]). Its Shannon entropy in natural units is\n$$H(p):=-\\sum_{i=1}^k p_i\\ln p_i,$$\nwith $0\\ln0:=0$. For probability vectors $p,q$ on the same set $[k]$, the relative entropy is\n$$D(p\\|q):=\\sum_{i:p_i>0}p_i\\ln(p_i/q_i),$$\nwith value $+\\infty$ if $p_i>0=q_i$ for some $i$. A matrix $A\\in[0,1]^{N\\times m}$ is **row-stochastic** when each row sums to one. For a probability row vector $p$ on $[m]$ and a row vector $X\\in[0,1]^m$, the dot product $pX^{\\mathsf T}=\\sum_{i=1}^m p_iX_i$ is the acceptance probability under distribution $p$.\n\nThese definitions use finite sets only; entropy is between $0$ and $\\ln k$, as proved where needed below.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-slower-than-every-positive-power",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $\\alpha>0$,\n$$\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}\\frac{\\log x}{x^\\alpha}=0.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-heine-borel-pseudocompactness-and-extreme-values",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$ and let $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$ be nonempty. The following are equivalent.\n\n1. $A$ is compact.\n2. $A$ is closed and bounded.\n3. $A$ is pseudocompact.\n4. Every continuous $f:A\\to\\mathbb{R}$ attains a maximum and a minimum on $A$.\n\nThis theorem is a ZF statement. The nonemptiness hypothesis is necessary for condition 4, because the empty image has neither a maximum nor a minimum.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
