        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-parallel-gaussian-channel-with-power-constraint",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. Fix an integer $d\\ge1$ and a real symmetric positive-definite matrix $K\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$. The **$d$-dimensional additive Gaussian channel with power budget $P\\ge0$** has input and output spaces $\\mathbb R^d$ and single-use law\n$$Y=X+Z,\\qquad Z\\sim N_d(0,K),\\qquad Z\\perp X.$$\nAt each use a fresh independent copy of $Z$ is added, so the channel is memoryless. The input cost is $c(x)=\\|x\\|_2^2$. A length-$n$ deterministic code with $M$ equiprobable messages meets the **average block power constraint** $P$ when\n$$\\frac1{nM}\\sum_{m=1}^M\\sum_{t=1}^n\\|x_t(m)\\|_2^2\\le P.$$\nRates are measured in nats per vector use. The noise law is [[def-multivariate-normal-law]]; positive definiteness of $K$ gives the Lebesgue density stated in [[thm-nondegenerate-multivariate-normal-law-has-density]]. The scalar case $d=1$ is the real AWGN channel. Rates use the natural logarithm [[def-natural-logarithm]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-parallel-awgn-feedback-code",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC [[def-axiom-of-choice]] and use the channel in [[def-parallel-gaussian-channel-with-power-constraint]] with dimension $d$ and budget $P$. A length-$n$ code with $M\\ge1$ equiprobable messages is a tuple of Borel encoders\n$$f_t:[M]\\times(\\mathbb R^d)^{t-1}\\to\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le n,$$\nand a Borel decoder $g:(\\mathbb R^d)^n\\to[M]$. At time $t$ the input is $X_t=f_t(M,Y^{t-1})$ and the output is $Y_t=X_t+Z_t$, with the fresh independent Gaussian noise of the channel definition. The code meets the expected average-power constraint when\n$$\\frac1n\\mathbb E\\left[\\sum_{t=1}^n\\|X_t\\|_2^2\\right]\\le P.$$\nIts average error is $\\Pr[g(Y^n)\\ne M]$ and its rate is $n^{-1}\\ln M$ nats per vector use. If every $f_t$ is independent of $Y^{t-1}$, the code does not use feedback. Define $C_{\\rm fb}(P)$ as the supremum of rates $R$ for which feasible feedback codes for all sufficiently large $n$ have average error tending to zero and $\\liminf n^{-1}\\ln M_n\\ge R$. Define $C_{\\rm nf}(P)$ by restricting to codes that do not use feedback. Both capacities use natural-log nats per vector use [[def-natural-logarithm]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-gaussian-noise-orthogonalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $K\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ be symmetric positive definite and let $Z\\sim N_d(0,K)$ be the additive noise in [[def-parallel-gaussian-channel-with-power-constraint]]. Then there are an orthogonal matrix $U$ and positive numbers $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_d$ such that $K=U^T\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_d)U$ and the coordinates of $UZ$ are independent with laws $N(0,\\lambda_j)$. For every input $X$, $\\|UX\\|_2=\\|X\\|_2$ and $U(X+Z)=UX+UZ$, so this change of coordinates preserves the power constraint and the channel law.",
          "uses": [
