          "source": "lem-cost-pruned-random-code",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix a blocklength $n$, a message count $M\\ge2$, a codebook ensemble with exchangeable message codewords, and a decoder for each sampled codebook. Let $B_n$ be the random fraction of codewords whose normalized cost exceeds $\\Gamma$, and let $\\bar\\varepsilon_n$ be the ensemble codebook's average error. If $q_n:=\\mathbb E B_n$ and $a_n:=\\mathbb E\\bar\\varepsilon_n$ satisfy $q_n+a_n<1$, then some deterministic subcode has every retained codeword of normalized cost at most $\\Gamma$, average error at most $\\sqrt{q_n+a_n}/(1-\\sqrt{q_n+a_n})$, and at least $M(1-\\sqrt{q_n+a_n})$ messages.\n\nIf this construction is available for each sufficiently large $n$, with $q_n+a_n\\to0$, then the retained code's rate differs from $n^{-1}\\ln M_n$ by a quantity tending to zero.",
