          "source": "lem-density-channel-threshold-random-coding-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $P_X$ be an input law, let $\\nu$ be a sigma-finite reference measure on the output space, and let $w(y\\mid x)$ be a measurable single-use channel density satisfying $\\int w(y\\mid x)\\,d\\nu(y)=1$. Let $q(y)=\\int w(y\\mid x)\\,P_X(dx)$ be the induced output density and assume $q>0$ on its probability-one support. For independent uses define\n$$\\imath(x^n;y^n):=\\sum_{t=1}^n\\ln\\frac{w(y_t\\mid x_t)}{q(y_t)}$$\non the joint support. For every $n\\ge1$, $M\\ge2$, and $\\tau>0$, the IID random-code ensemble's expected average error is at most\n$$\\Pr\\!\\left[\\imath(X^n;Y^n)\\le\\ln(M-1)+\\tau\\right]+e^{-\\tau},$$\nwhere $(X^n,Y^n)$ has input law $P_X^{\\otimes n}$ followed by the product channel. Consequently, some deterministic length-$n$ code with $M$ messages and the threshold decoder has average error no greater than this bound.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
