          "source": "thm-chebyshev-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every finite real random variable $X$ and every $t>0$,\n$$\\mathbb P(|X-\\mathbb E[X]|\\ge t)\\le\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}{t^2}.$$\nEquivalently, if $\\sigma_X>0$ and $c>0$, then $\\mathbb P(|X-\\mathbb E[X]|\\ge c\\sigma_X)\\le1/c^2$. If $\\sigma_X=0$, the first form remains valid for every $t>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-discrete-memoryless-channel-information-capacity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an input probability vector $P_X$ on $\\mathcal X$ ([[def-finite-probability-vector]]), let $P_{XY}(x,y):=P_X(x)W(y\\mid x)$ and $Q_{P_X}(y):=\\sum_xP_X(x)W(y\\mid x)$. Write $I(P_X;W):=I(X;Y)$ for this joint law, using [[def-it-finite-mutual-information]]. The **single-letter information capacity** is\n$$C(W):=\\sup_{P_X} I(P_X;W),$$\nmeasured in nats per channel use. The finite-dimensional maximum is attained, as proved in [[lem-discrete-memoryless-channel-capacity-is-attained]].",
          "uses": [
