        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-it-empirical-type-and-type-class",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal A$ be a finite nonempty alphabet and let $n\\ge1$. A word of length $n$ is $x^n=(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\in\\mathcal A^n$. For $a\\in\\mathcal A$, define its count and empirical type by\n$$N(a\\mid x^n):=\\#\\{i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}:x_i=a\\},\\qquad \\widehat P_{x^n}(a):=\\frac{N(a\\mid x^n)}n.$$\nEvery position contributes to exactly one count, so the coordinates of $\\widehat P_{x^n}$ are nonnegative and sum to one; it is a probability vector in the sense of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]]. The **set of $n$-types** is $\\mathcal P_n(\\mathcal A):=\\{\\widehat P_{x^n}:x^n\\in\\mathcal A^n\\}$. Thus $q\\in\\mathcal P_n(\\mathcal A)$ exactly when every $nq(a)$ is a nonnegative integer and $\\sum_a nq(a)=n$: the converse is obtained by arranging the prescribed finite number of copies of each letter. For $q\\in\\mathcal P_n(\\mathcal A)$, its **type class** is\n$$T_q:=\\{x^n\\in\\mathcal A^n:\\widehat P_{x^n}=q\\}.$$\nThe map $x^n\\mapsto\\widehat P_{x^n}$ has these type classes as its fibers.",
