          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-finite-alphabet-iid-information-aep",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\ge1}$ be a finite-alphabet IID source with one-letter law $p$,\nentropy $H(p)$, and surprisal variance $V(p)$ as defined above. For\n$I_n(X^n)=-n^{-1}\\ln p^n(X^n)$, defined almost surely on $A_+^n$, every\n$n\\ge1$ and $\\varepsilon>0$ satisfy\n$$\\mathbb P(|I_n(X^n)-H(p)|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\frac{V(p)}{n\\varepsilon^2}.$$\nConsequently $I_n(X^n)\\to H(p)$ in probability.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-it-finite-conditional-entropy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p_{XY}$ be a joint probability vector on finite alphabets $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$. It is the weight function of the canonical finite probability space from [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]; its coordinate maps are finite random elements in the sense of [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]. Write $p_Y(y)=\\sum_xp_{XY}(x,y)$. For each $y$ with $p_Y(y)>0$, set $p_{X|y}(x)=p_{XY}(x,y)/p_Y(y)$; this is a probability vector by [[lem-it-finite-marginals-and-conditionals-are-pmfs]]. Define the conditional entropy in nats by\n\n$$H(X|Y):=\\sum_{y:p_Y(y)>0}p_Y(y)H(p_{X|y}),$$\n\nwhere $H(p)=-\\sum_xp(x)\\ln p(x)$ is the finite entropy of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]]. Terms for zero-mass $y$ are omitted. The base-$b$ version replaces $\\ln$ by $\\log_b$.",
