          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\rho>0$ be any desired relative accuracy for a constructed joint type. For each positive-mass $x$, let $n_x$ be its count in $x^n$. Choose $\\delta<1/2$; then $n_x/n$ is within relative error $\\delta$ of $P_X(x)$, so for all sufficiently large $n$ every such $n_x$ is positive and grows linearly in $n$, uniformly over $x^n\\in T_\\delta^{(n)}(P_X)$. For each row, take floors of $n_xP_{U|X}(u|x)$ on its positive conditional support and distribute the fewer than $|\\mathcal U|$ remaining counts among those entries; set zero-conditional-probability counts to zero. Every resulting count differs from its target by at most $|\\mathcal U|$. As there are finitely many positive joint atoms, first choose $\\delta$ small and then $n$ large so that the joint type $q_{XU}(x,u)=(n_x/n)q_{U|X}(u|x)$ has relative error at most $\\rho$ on every positive atom of $P_{XU}$. Zero joint atoms receive zero counts. Thus the associated conditional type class is nonempty and its joint type lies in $T_\\rho^{(n)}(P_{XU})$.",
          "step": "1.1",
