      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-it-finite-wyner-ziv-code",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_t,Y_t)_{t\\ge1}$ be an IID pair with joint pmf $P_{XY}$ on finite nonempty alphabets $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$, as the $k=2$ instance of [[def-it-finite-iid-multisource]]. Let $\\widehat{\\mathcal X}$ be finite nonempty and let $d:\\mathcal X\\times\\widehat{\\mathcal X}\\to[0,d_{\\max}]$ be the finite bounded single-letter distortion from [[def-it-single-letter-distortion-and-block-distortion]]. For $x^n=(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $\\hat x^n=(\\hat x_1,\\ldots,\\hat x_n)$, define\n\n$$d_n(x^n,\\hat x^n):=\\frac1n\\sum_{t=1}^n d(x_t,\\hat x_t).$$\n\nA length-$n$, $M$-message Wyner-Ziv code consists of a deterministic encoder $f_n:\\mathcal X^n\\to[M]$ and deterministic decoder $g_n:[M]\\times\\mathcal Y^n\\to\\widehat{\\mathcal X}^n$. The encoder observes only $X^n$; the decoder observes both the message and $Y^n$. Its rate is $n^{-1}\\ln M$ nats per source symbol, and its expected distortion is $\\mathbb E[d_n(X^n,g_n(f_n(X^n),Y^n))]$.\n\nA pair $(R,D)$ is operationally achievable if there are increasing blocklengths $n_j\\to\\infty$ and such codes with $\\limsup_j n_j^{-1}\\ln M_j\\le R$ and $\\limsup_j\\mathbb E[d_{n_j}]\\le D$. Define $R_{\\rm WZ}^{\\rm op}(D)$ as the infimum of achievable rates.\n\nFor a finite auxiliary alphabet $\\mathcal U$, a test channel is a stochastic kernel $P_{U|X}$ together with a deterministic map $g:\\mathcal Y\\times\\mathcal U\\to\\widehat{\\mathcal X}$, under the joint law $P_{XYU}=P_{XY}P_{U|X}$. It has $U-X-Y$, expected distortion $\\mathbb E[d(X,g(Y,U))]$, and conditional mutual information $I(X;U|Y)$ from [[def-it-finite-conditional-mutual-information]]. Define the single-letter candidate\n$$R_{\\rm WZ}(D):=\\inf_{\\substack{\\mathcal U\\ {\\rm finite},\\,P_{U|X},\\,g\\\\\\mathbb E[d(X,g(Y,U))]\\le D}} I(X;U|Y),$$\nwith value $+\\infty$ if the feasible set is empty. This definition alone makes no operational equality claim; the following theorem proves it and the cardinality lemma makes the finite minimum explicit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-it-finite-entropy-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For finite $X,Y,Z$ with a joint pmf,\n\n$$H(X,Y|Z)=H(X|Z)+H(Y|X,Z),$$\n\nand\n\n$$I(X;Y,Z)=I(X;Z)+I(X;Y|Z).$$\n\nIn particular, $I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X|Y)=H(Y)-H(Y|X)$. All terms are finite.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite nonempty set identified with $[k]=\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$, a **probability vector** is $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^k$ with $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$; it is the outcome-weight vector of a finite probability space ([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]). Its Shannon entropy in natural units is\n$$H(p):=-\\sum_{i=1}^k p_i\\ln p_i,$$\nwith $0\\ln0:=0$. For probability vectors $p,q$ on the same set $[k]$, the relative entropy is\n$$D(p\\|q):=\\sum_{i:p_i>0}p_i\\ln(p_i/q_i),$$\nwith value $+\\infty$ if $p_i>0=q_i$ for some $i$. A matrix $A\\in[0,1]^{N\\times m}$ is **row-stochastic** when each row sums to one. For a probability row vector $p$ on $[m]$ and a row vector $X\\in[0,1]^m$, the dot product $pX^{\\mathsf T}=\\sum_{i=1}^m p_iX_i$ is the acceptance probability under distribution $p$.\n\nThese definitions use finite sets only; entropy is between $0$ and $\\ln k$, as proved where needed below.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]],\n[[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous\n([[def-metric-continuity]]), $\\mathbb{R}$ carrying its usual metric\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]). Then the\nimage $f[X]$ is bounded above and below ([[def-bounded-set]]), and it has a\nmaximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]): there are points\n$x_{\\max}, x_{\\min} \\in X$ with\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; f(x_{\\max}) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in X,$$\n\nand then $f(x_{\\max}) = \\sup f[X]$ and $f(x_{\\min}) = \\inf f[X]$\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNonemptiness of $X$ is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for $X = \\emptyset$ the\nimage is empty and has neither a supremum nor a maximum. No choice principle is\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
