          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Choose $K$ with $p_0:=\\Pr(\\|X\\|\\le K)>0$, which exists because the increasing union of the finite-radius balls is $\\mathbb R^d$. For every $y$, $f_Y(y)\\le(2\\pi N)^{-d/2}$ and $f_Y(y)\\ge p_0(2\\pi N)^{-d/2}e^{-(\\|y\\|+K)^2/(2N)}$. Thus $|\\ln f_Y(y)|\\le C(1+\\|y\\|^2)$ for a finite constant $C$; [F1] and step 1.1 give $\\int f_Y|\\ln f_Y|<\\infty$. The same argument applies to every coordinate marginal and, for almost every $s$, to the conditional law $P_{X|S=s}$ using a radius $K_s$ with positive conditional mass.",
