          "source": "def-it-two-user-real-awgn-mac-feedback-code",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the channel, independent messages, noise law, average joint error, powers $P_1,P_2$ and nats/use convention of [[def-it-two-user-real-awgn-mac-code]]. A length-$n$ perfect-feedback code has a Borel-measurable joint decoder $g:\\mathbb R^n\\to[M_1]\\times[M_2]$. Its user-$j$ encoder is a deterministic Borel-measurable family $f_{ji}:[M_j]\\times\\mathbb R^{i-1}\\to\\mathbb R$, $1\\le i\\le n$, with $X_{ji}=f_{ji}(M_j,Y^{i-1})$; at $i=1$ the history is the singleton empty tuple. Both senders receive the same noiseless past output, and there is no message sharing or preshared common randomness. The same expected block-power constraint is imposed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $K=\\begin{pmatrix}P_1&c\\\\c&P_2\\end{pmatrix}$ with $c=\\rho\\sqrt{P_1P_2}$, $a=(1,1)^T$, and $D=N+a^TKa$. Set $R=X-(Ka/D)Y$. Then $\\operatorname{Cov}(R,Y)=Ka-(Ka/D)D=0$. Since $(R,Y)$ is jointly Gaussian, this block-diagonal covariance makes its joint characteristic function factor, so $R$ and $Y$ are independent. Thus $\\mathbb E[X\\mid Y]=KaY/D$ and the residual covariance is $\\operatorname{Cov}(R)=K-Kaa^TK/D$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Since $Ka=(P_1+c,P_2+c)^T$ and $N+a^TKa=D$, subtracting the rank-one matrix gives diagonal entries $P_1[N+P_2(1-\\rho^2)]/D$, $P_2[N+P_1(1-\\rho^2)]/D$, and off-diagonal entry $[cN+c^2-P_1P_2]/D$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
