    "lem-it-fano-for-finite-message-density-observations": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a finite nonempty set identified with $[k]=\\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$, a **probability vector** is $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^k$ with $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$; it is the outcome-weight vector of a finite probability space ([[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]). Its Shannon entropy in natural units is\n$$H(p):=-\\sum_{i=1}^k p_i\\ln p_i,$$\nwith $0\\ln0:=0$. For probability vectors $p,q$ on the same set $[k]$, the relative entropy is\n$$D(p\\|q):=\\sum_{i:p_i>0}p_i\\ln(p_i/q_i),$$\nwith value $+\\infty$ if $p_i>0=q_i$ for some $i$. A matrix $A\\in[0,1]^{N\\times m}$ is **row-stochastic** when each row sums to one. For a probability row vector $p$ on $[m]$ and a row vector $X\\in[0,1]^m$, the dot product $pX^{\\mathsf T}=\\sum_{i=1}^m p_iX_i$ is the acceptance probability under distribution $p$.\n\nThese definitions use finite sets only; entropy is between $0$ and $\\ln k$, as proved where needed below.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-it-finite-kl-nonnegative-equality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For finite probability vectors $p,q$ on the same alphabet, $D(p\\|q)\\ge0$, and $D(p\\|q)=0$ if and only if $p=q$. If $p_i>0=q_i$ for some coordinate, then $D(p\\|q)=+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "If $\\pi_s(m)=\\Pr[M=m\\mid S=s]$ and $f_{m,s}$ is the conditional density of $Y$, then $f_s(y)=\\sum_m\\pi_s(m)f_{m,s}(y)$ and $p_{M|y,s}(m)=\\pi_s(m)f_{m,s}(y)/f_s(y)$ whenever $f_s(y)>0$. This is a finite posterior probability vector, so [F1] makes its entropy well-defined and bounded by $\\ln m$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Put $E=\\mathbf1\\{\\widehat M\\ne M\\}$. For each fixed $(y,s)$, $E$ is a function of $M$; expanding the finite entropy sum first over $M$ and then over $E$ gives $H(M\\mid y,s)=H(E\\mid y,s)+H(M\\mid E,y,s)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $q(y,s)=\\Pr[E=1\\mid Y=y,S=s]$. If $0<\\epsilon<1$, [F2] applied to the two-point laws $(q,1-q)$ and $(\\epsilon,1-\\epsilon)$ gives $q\\ln(q/\\epsilon)+(1-q)\\ln((1-q)/(1-\\epsilon))\\ge0$. Rearranging and averaging, using $\\mathbb E q=\\epsilon$, yields $\\mathbb E h_b(q)\\le h_b(\\epsilon)$. If $\\epsilon=0$ or $1$, then $q=\\epsilon$ almost surely and both binary entropies are zero.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra",
            "cases"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "On $E=0$, $M=\\phi(Y,S)$ and so its conditional entropy is zero; on $E=1$ and fixed $(Y,S)$, at most $m-1$ message values remain, so [F2] bounds that entropy by $\\ln(m-1)$. Therefore $H(M\\mid E,Y,S)\\le\\epsilon\\ln(m-1)$.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Averaging the identity of step 2.1 and applying steps 3.1 and 3.2 proves the sharp bound. Since $h_b(\\epsilon)\\le\\ln2$ and $\\ln(m-1)\\le\\ln m$, the weaker displayed bound follows. If $m=1$, $M$ is constant.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 3.2",
            "algebra",
            "cases"
          ]
        }
