          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix powers $P_1,P_2\\ge0$ and noise variance $N>0$. At use $i$ the real channel is\n$$Y_i=X_{1i}+X_{2i}+Z_i,$$\nwhere the noises are independent of the messages and of one another, each with Lebesgue density $\\phi_N(z)=(2\\pi N)^{-1/2}e^{-z^2/(2N)}$. The normalization is proved in step 1.1 below. A length-$n$ no-feedback code has independent uniform messages $M_j\\in[M_j]$, separate deterministic encoders $f_j:[M_j]\\to\\mathbb R^n$, and a Borel-measurable joint decoder $g:\\mathbb R^n\\to[M_1]\\times[M_2]$, where the finite message sets have their discrete sigma-algebras. The encoders are measurable because their domains are finite. Write $X_j^n=f_j(M_j)$. Its average joint error is $P_e=\\Pr[g(Y^n)\\ne(M_1,M_2)]$ and its expected block-power constraints are $n^{-1}\\sum_i\\mathbb E X_{ji}^2\\le P_j$, separately for $j=1,2$. The rate is $R_j=n^{-1}\\ln M_j$ nats/use. Achievability means a sequence with error tending to zero and limiting lower rates at least the specified pair; the capacity region is the closure of all achievable pairs.",
