          "claim": "Choose any $R_0$ with $0<R_0<C(P_0/N)$ and set $L_0=\\lceil e^{nR_0}\\rceil$. Generate an independent private codebook $A^n(m_0)$, $m_0\\in[L_0]$, with IID $N(0,P_0)$ coordinates. User 1 splits its message into $m_0$ and a feedback-layer message; it adds $A_i(m_0)$ to its feedback-layer input on each recursive use. User 2 uses the feedback layer with residual power $P_2^\\circ$. On the three startup uses send only the feedback layer, using the message-point/noise construction of the sum-corner scheme. The startup noises have variance $N$, but this construction only needs the resulting initial Gaussian pair: choose the shared-noise coefficient and the two scales so its variances are $P_1'',P_2^\\circ$ and its correlation is $\\rho_f$. On recursive uses, the codebook-ensemble private symbols are IID $N(0,P_0)$, independent of the feedback messages, past outputs and fresh channel noise before each current use. Thus they are an additional independent Gaussian noise of variance $P_0$, and the recursive feedback layer sees variance $N'$. The causal feedback-layer maps are affine in the observed past outputs and message points, and adding the fixed codeword is Borel-measurable, so they satisfy the feedback-code interface. The covariance update [F4] and the calculation in [F3] apply to this initial pair and recursive noise, giving feedback-layer rates arbitrarily close to $$R_{1f}=C\\!\\left(\\frac{P_1''(1-\\rho_f^2)}{N'}\\right),\\qquad R_2=C\\!\\left(\\frac{P_2^\\circ(1-\\rho_f^2)}{N'}\\right).$$",
