    "ex-it-binary-xor-dm-mac-product-input-region": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-it-two-user-discrete-memoryless-mac-code",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a DM-MAC as in [[def-it-two-user-discrete-memoryless-mac]], a length-$n$ no-feedback code consists of independent uniform private messages $M_j\\in[M_j]$, separate deterministic message-only encoders $f_j:[M_j]\\to\\mathcal X_j^n$, and one joint decoder $g:\\mathcal Y^n\\to[M_1]\\times[M_2]$. The average joint error is $\\Pr[g(Y^n)\\ne(M_1,M_2)]$; $R_j=n^{-1}\\ln M_j$ nats/use. A pair is achievable if a sequence has error tending to zero and limiting lower rates at least the pair; the capacity region is the closure. In particular, independent messages and separate encoders give $X_{1i}\\perp X_{2i}$ at every coordinate.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-it-two-user-discrete-memoryless-mac-capacity-region",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the average-joint-error no-feedback code of [[def-it-two-user-discrete-memoryless-mac-code]] over the finite DM-MAC, the capacity region is the closure of the union over all finite $Q$ and laws $P_QP_{X_1|Q}P_{X_2|Q}W$ of\n$$R_1\\le I(X_1;Y\\mid X_2,Q),\\quad R_2\\le I(X_2;Y\\mid X_1,Q),\\quad R_1+R_2\\le I(X_1,X_2;Y\\mid Q).$$\nHere $Q$ is a public time-sharing variable independent of both private messages; conditional on each $Q=q$, the two input laws are a product. This result does not characterize maximal-error capacity.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-it-finite-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p_{XY}$ be a joint probability vector on finite alphabets $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$, with marginals $p_X(x):=\\sum_y p_{XY}(x,y)$ and $p_Y(y):=\\sum_xp_{XY}(x,y)$. The marginals are probability vectors by [[lem-it-finite-marginals-and-conditionals-are-pmfs]]. The joint vector is the outcome-weight function of the canonical finite probability space from [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]; its coordinate maps are finite random elements in the sense of [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]. Define mutual information in nats by\n\n$$I(X;Y):=D(p_{XY}\\|p_X\\otimes p_Y)=\\sum_{(x,y):p_{XY}(x,y)>0}p_{XY}(x,y)\\ln\\frac{p_{XY}(x,y)}{p_X(x)p_Y(y)},$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite KL convention of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]] and $(p_X\\otimes p_Y)(x,y):=p_X(x)p_Y(y)$. Every ratio in the displayed sum is positive and finite: if $p_{XY}(x,y)>0$, then each marginal sum is at least that positive entry. The sum is over finite support, so $I(X;Y)$ is finite. The base-$b$ version replaces $\\ln$ by $\\log_b$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-it-finite-conditional-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p_{XYZ}$ be a joint probability vector on finite alphabets and write $p_Z(z)$ for its marginal. It is the law of finite coordinate random elements on the canonical finite probability space from [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]] and [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]. For each $z$ with $p_Z(z)>0$, the conditional law $p_{XY|z}$ and its marginals $p_{X|z},p_{Y|z}$ are probability vectors by [[lem-it-finite-marginals-and-conditionals-are-pmfs]]. Define conditional mutual information in nats by\n\n$$I(X;Y|Z):=\\sum_{z:p_Z(z)>0}p_Z(z)D(p_{XY|z}\\|p_{X|z}\\otimes p_{Y|z}),$$\n\nusing the finite KL convention of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]], where $(p_{X|z}\\otimes p_{Y|z})(x,y):=p_{X|z}(x)p_{Y|z}(y)$. A positive conditional joint mass forces both conditional marginals to be positive, so each displayed KL term is finite. Zero-mass conditioning values contribute no term. The base-$b$ version replaces $\\ln$ by $\\log_b$ inside the conditional KL sums.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-it-finite-conditional-mutual-information-identities-and-equality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For finite $X,Y,Z$,\n\n$$I(X;Y|Z)=H(X|Z)+H(Y|Z)-H(X,Y|Z)\\ge0.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if, for every $z$ with $p_Z(z)>0$, the conditional joint pmf factors as $p_{XY|z}=p_{X|z}p_{Y|z}$; this is conditional independence given $Z$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-it-finite-entropy-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For finite $X,Y,Z$ with a joint pmf,\n\n$$H(X,Y|Z)=H(X|Z)+H(Y|X,Z),$$\n\nand\n\n$$I(X;Y,Z)=I(X;Z)+I(X;Y|Z).$$\n\nIn particular, $I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X|Y)=H(Y)-H(Y|X)$. All terms are finite.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For independent Bernoulli inputs with parameters $p,q$, given $X_2$ the XOR is a bijective relabeling of $X_1$, and given $(X_1,X_2)$ the output is fixed. Thus [F4]–[F5] give $I(X_1;Y\\mid X_2)=H(X_1\\mid X_2)=h_b(p)$; interchanging users gives $h_b(q)$. The deterministic output and [F3], [F6] give $I(X_1,X_2;Y)=H(Y)=h_b(p+q-2pq)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
