            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The diagonal joint law has probability $1/2$ on $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$, so it is not a product law: its marginals are uniform but their product puts positive mass on $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$. More generally, suppose a finite mixture $P_QP_{X_1|Q}P_{X_2|Q}$ equals this diagonal law. For every positive-mass $q$, nonnegativity and zero total off-diagonal mass force $P(X_1=0|q)P(X_2=1|q)=P(X_1=1|q)P(X_2=0|q)=0$. If $X_1$ had positive conditional mass on both bits, these equalities would force $P(X_2=0|q)=P(X_2=1|q)=0$, impossible; hence $X_1$ is deterministic given $q$, and the two equalities then force $X_2$ to be the same deterministic bit. Thus any such public time-sharing representation uses deterministic diagonal product components. With $Q$ selecting $(0,0)$ or $(1,1)$, [F5] gives $I(X_1;Y\\mid X_2,Q)=I(X_2;Y\\mid X_1,Q)=0$; the mixture adds no private-message rate.",
