      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $g$ be the everywhere-positive Gaussian density in [F1] with mean $m$ and covariance $K$. Since $f$ is a Lebesgue density and $g>0$ everywhere, the law $P_f$ is absolutely continuous with respect to $P_g$. The log ratio is integrable: $\\int f\\ln f$ is finite by hypothesis, while $\\ln g$ is a constant plus a quadratic form and $X$ has finite second moments. Hence $$D(P_f\\|P_g)=\\int f\\ln(f/g)=-h(X)+\\frac d2\\ln(2\\pi)+\\frac12\\ln\\det K+\\frac12\\mathbb E[(X-m)^TK^{-1}(X-m)].$$ The last expectation is $\\operatorname{tr}(K^{-1}K)=d$, by expanding the finite quadratic form and using the covariance entries.",
