        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-it-real-differential-entropy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "If $X$ has Lebesgue density $f$ on $\\mathbb R^d$ and $\\int f\\ln f$ is a finite real number, its differential entropy in nats is $h(X):=-\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}f(x)\\ln f(x)\\,dx$. If $(X,S)$ has conditional densities $f_{X|S=s}$ for almost every $s$ and the indicated integral is finite, set $h(X|S):=\\int h(f_{X|S=s})\\,dP_S(s)$. We use only these finite-integral cases; differential entropy is not defined here by a formal subtraction of possibly infinite quantities.",
          "uses": [
