          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $q(x)=(2\\pi)^{-d/2}e^{-|x|^2/2}$ and $M=\\max\\{1,\\|f\\|_\\infty\\}$. On $\\{f>1\\}$, $f\\ln f\\le f\\ln M$, so the positive part is integrable. For $t\\ge0$, $t\\ln(1/t)\\le1/e$; with $t=f/q$, this gives $f\\ln(q/f)\\le q/e$. Thus on $\\{f\\le1\\}$, $$-f\\ln f=f\\ln(q/f)-f\\ln q\\le q/e+f|\\ln q|.$$ The right side is integrable because $q$ is a probability density and $|\\ln q|$ is bounded by a constant plus $|x|^2/2$. Hence the negative part of $f\\ln f$ is integrable.",
