            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2>0$ be the eigenvalues of $K$. Then $\\det K=\\lambda_1\\lambda_2\\le((\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)/2)^2=(\\sigma^2/2)^2$, because $(\\lambda_1-\\lambda_2)^2\\ge0$. Substitution into step 1.1 gives $h(Z)\\le\\ln(\\pi e\\sigma^2)$. Equality requires equality in [F1] and $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2$, so $Z$ is Gaussian with real covariance $(\\sigma^2/2)I_2$, exactly $\\mathcal{CN}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$. Conversely, substituting that circular Gaussian density gives entropy $\\ln(\\pi e\\sigma^2)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
