          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-it-finite-strongly-typical-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal A$ be a finite nonempty alphabet, let $P$ be a probability vector on $\\mathcal A$ in the sense of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]], and let $n\\ge1$. Write $\\operatorname{supp}(P):=\\{a\\in\\mathcal A:P(a)>0\\}$. For $a^n=(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)\\in\\mathcal A^n$, its empirical distribution is $\\widehat P_{a^n}(a):=n^{-1}|\\{t:a_t=a\\}|$. For $\\delta>0$, define the support-restricted strongly typical set by\n$$T_\\delta^{(n)}(P):=\\{a^n\\in\\operatorname{supp}(P)^n:|\\widehat P_{a^n}(a)-P(a)|\\le\\delta P(a)\\text{ for every }a\\text{ with }P(a)>0\\}.$$\nFor a finite joint law $P_{XA}$ and a fixed $x^n$, write $T_\\delta^{(n)}(P_{A|X}\\mid x^n):=\\{a^n:(x^n,a^n)\\in T_\\delta^{(n)}(P_{XA})\\}$. This conditional set is empty if $x^n$ is outside the support of $P_X$. Zero-probability letters are excluded exactly, so no logarithm of zero occurs in likelihood calculations on these sets.",
