          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-real-gaussian-multicast-relay-network",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **real scalar Gaussian relay network** has node set $[N]$ for an integer $N\\ge1$, source set $\\mathcal S\\subseteq[N]$, destination set $\\mathcal D\\subseteq[N]$, one real input $X_i$ and output $Y_i$ at each node, and channel equations\n$$Y_j=\\sum_{i=1}^N g_{ji}X_i+Z_j,\\qquad j\\in[N],$$\nwhere the gains $g_{ji}\\in\\mathbb R$ are fixed and the noises $Z_j$ are independent $N(0,1)$ random variables, independent across channel uses and of all source messages. At each use the current noise vector is independent of all past observations and the current inputs; future inputs may depend on past noises through feedback. Each node has a nonnegative power budget $P_i\\ge0$ and average power constraint $\\frac1n\\sum_{t=1}^n\\mathbb E[X_{i,t}^2]\\le P_i$. Each source $i\\in\\mathcal S$ has an independent uniform message $M_i$ in a finite nonempty set $\\mathcal M_i$. A length-$n$ causal code uses measurable encoders $X_{i,t}=f_{i,t}(M_i,Y_i^{t-1})$ and destination decoders based on $(Y_d^n,M_d)$, where $M_d$ is its own source message when $d\\in\\mathcal S$ and a singleton otherwise; multicast means every destination must decode every other source message, while its own message is already known. The average error probability is the probability that any required destination estimate is wrong. Write $G_{B,A}$ for the matrix with rows indexed by $B$ and columns by $A$ whose entries are $g_{ji}$.\n\nRates in this page's Gaussian formulas are measured in bits per use, $R_i=n^{-1}\\log_2|\\mathcal M_i|$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-finite-or-euclidean-likelihood-ratio-network-coding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "All logarithms, mutual informations and rates in this lemma are in nats. Consider a memoryless network with finite node set $[N]$, source set $\\mathcal S$, destination set $\\mathcal D$, and independent uniform source messages, each required by every destination. Alphabets are either all finite, as in [[def-finite-discrete-memoryless-network-and-code]], or finite-dimensional Euclidean spaces with constant coordinates allowed. Encoders are measurable functions of a node's own message and its past outputs; a destination knows its own message. Choose a finite variable $Q$ and measurable kernels with joint law\n$$P_Q\\prod_k P_{X_k|Q}\\,W(dy_{[N]}|x_{[N]})\\prod_k P_{\\hat Y_k|X_k,Y_k,Q}.$$\nAssume AC in the Euclidean case. For each positive-probability $q$, require a measurable version of the local log likelihood\n$$\\iota_{k,q}=\\log\\frac{dP_{\\hat Y_k|X_k,Y_k,q}}{dP_{\\hat Y_k|X_k,q}}(\\hat Y_k)$$\nto exist and be square-integrable under the target law. For a destination $d$, nonempty $A\\subseteq[N]\\setminus\\{d\\}$, and $U\\subseteq A^c\\setminus\\{d\\}$, put $C_0=(A\\cup U)^c$, let $P_{d,q}$ be the target marginal law of $(X_{[N]},\\hat Y_{[N]},Y_d)$ given $q$, and define\n$$Q_{d,A,U,q}=P_{X_{A^c},\\hat Y_{C_0},Y_d|q}\\prod_{k\\in A}P_{X_k|q}\\prod_{k\\in A\\cup U}P_{\\hat Y_k|X_k,q}.$$\nRequire $P_{d,q}\\ll Q_{d,A,U,q}$ with a measurable square-integrable log likelihood under $P_{d,q}$. Require all mutual informations in the following cut expression to be finite:\n$$F_d(A)=I(X_A;Y_d,\\hat Y_{A^c}|X_{A^c},Q)-I(Y_A;\\hat Y_A|X_{[N]},Y_d,\\hat Y_{A^c},Q).$$\nThese likelihood and finiteness hypotheses hold automatically for finite alphabets; zero-mass target atoms are omitted. At any chosen constrained node let $c_k\\ge0$ be a measurable input cost. Require either $\\mathbb E[c_k(X_k)^2]<\\infty$ and $\\mathbb E[c_k(X_k)]<\\Gamma_k$, or a deterministic input whose cost is at most $\\Gamma_k$. The latter case includes an identically zero input under a zero budget. Unconstrained nodes and constant coordinates are permitted.\n\nEvery nonnegative rate tuple satisfying\n$$\\sum_{k\\in A\\cap\\mathcal S}r_k<F_d(A)$$\nfor every $d\\in\\mathcal D$ and nonempty $A$ with $d\\notin A$ and $A\\cap\\mathcal S\\ne\\varnothing$ is achievable by deterministic measurable causal codes. Each constrained node can satisfy $\\sum_{t=1}^m c_k(X_{k,t})\\le m\\Gamma_k$ on every realized transmitted input sequence of code length $m$, and hence also its expected average-cost constraint. The closure of the union of these regions over admissible laws is achievable. Constant reconstructions are permitted. The separate all-zero tuple is achievable whenever one can choose fixed inputs obeying any imposed costs, regardless of negative cut expressions.",
          "uses": [
