          "quote": "A **finite two-user interference channel** is the two-sender, two-receiver memoryless channel with finite nonempty input alphabets $\\mathcal X_1,\\mathcal X_2$, finite nonempty output alphabets $\\mathcal Y_1,\\mathcal Y_2$, and transition law $W(y_1,y_2|x_1,x_2)$. Independent uniform messages $M_1,M_2$ on finite nonempty sets are encoded separately as $X_1^n=f_1(M_1)$ and $X_2^n=f_2(M_2)$; receiver $i$ estimates $M_i$ from $Y_i^n$. The **strong-interference regime** means that, for every product law $P_{X_1}P_{X_2}$,\n$$I(X_1;Y_1|X_2)\\le I(X_1;Y_2|X_2),\\qquad I(X_2;Y_2|X_1)\\le I(X_2;Y_1|X_1).$$\nThe inequalities are evaluated under the stated product law and the channel. They are universal hypotheses over input laws, not just inequalities at a chosen optimizing distribution.",
