          "quote": "A **finite discrete memoryless network (DMN)** has a node set $[N]=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ for an integer $N\\ge1$, finite nonempty input alphabets $\\mathcal X_k$ and output alphabets $\\mathcal Y_k$, and one transition probability vector $W(y_1,\\ldots,y_N\\mid x_1,\\ldots,x_N)$ for each input tuple. At each use, conditional on the current input tuple, the output tuple is drawn from this vector independently of earlier uses.\n\nNode $k$ may have an independent uniform message $M_k$ in a finite nonempty set $\\mathcal M_k$ and a destination set $D_k\\subseteq[N]$. A length-$n$ **causal code** consists of encoders $X_{k,i}=f_{k,i}(M_k,Y_k^{i-1})$ and, for each destination $d\\in D_k$, a decoder $\\widehat M_{kd}=g_{kd}(Y_d^n,M_d)$; here $Y_k^{i-1}=(Y_{k,1},\\ldots,Y_{k,i-1})$, with the empty tuple at $i=1$. The average error is the probability that any required estimate differs from its message. A rate tuple is achievable if a sequence of such codes has error tending to zero and message-set rates tending to at least that tuple; the capacity region is the closure of achievable tuples.",
