        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-standard-borel-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A measurable space $(E,\\mathcal E)$ is **standard Borel** if there are a Polish space $P$ and a bijection $h:E\\to P$ such that $A\\in\\mathcal E$ exactly when $h[A]$ is Borel in $P$. Thus the measurable structure is part of the datum; no particular compatible Polish topology on $E$ is selected.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-finite-products-of-standard-borel-spaces-are-standard-borel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every finite product of standard Borel spaces, with its finite product sigma-algebra, is standard Borel.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-mutual-information-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For standard-Borel random elements X,Y,Z,\n$$I((Z,X);Y)=I(Z;Y)+I(X;Y\\mid Z).$$\nThe right side is a sum in $[0,+\\infty]$. This chain rule is asserted as an equality of extended nonnegative quantities; no difference of possibly infinite entropies or divergences occurs.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-standard-borel-conditional-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let X,Y,Z be random elements with standard-Borel value spaces and joint law $P_{XYZ}$. Let $K(z,\\cdot)$ and $L(z,\\cdot)$ be regular conditional laws of X and Y given Z=z. Let R be the probability law on the product space whose rectangle values are\n$$R(C\\times A\\times B)=\\int_C K(z,A)L(z,B)\\,P_Z(dz).$$\nIts existence, uniqueness, and independence of the versions K,L are established in [[lem-same-marginal-conditional-product-law-well-defined]]. Define the **conditional mutual information** by\n$$I(X;Y\\mid Z):=D(P_{XYZ}\\|R)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nThe reference R has the same Z marginal as the joint law and makes X,Y independent conditionally on Z. The logarithm is natural, so finite values are measured in nats under [[def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base]]. The definition is a divergence between full probability laws; it does not subtract two possibly infinite entropies.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-kullback-leibler-is-nonnegative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC and use the directional convention of\n[[def-kullback-leibler-divergence]]. For probability measures $P,Q$ on one\nmeasurable space, $D(P\\|Q)\\ge0$, and $D(P\\|Q)=0$ if and only if $P=Q$.\n\nThere is also the following normalized-density identity. Let\n$(\\mathsf X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space: it is a set\nwith a sigma-algebra and measure ([[def-measure-space]]), and there is a\ncountable measurable cover by sets of finite $\\mu$-measure\n([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]). Let\n$\\gamma:\\mathsf X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a finite-valued measurable function with\n$Z:=\\int\\gamma\\,d\\mu\\in(0,\\infty)$, and put $\\pi=\\gamma/Z$. Let\n$q:\\mathsf X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a finite-valued measurable probability density,\nso $\\int q\\,d\\mu=1$, and suppose $q=0$ $\\mu$-almost everywhere on\n$\\{\\gamma=0\\}$. Assume\n$$\\int q\\bigl(|\\log\\gamma|+|\\log q|\\bigr)\\,d\\mu<\\infty,$$\nwhere each term with $q=0$ is defined to be zero. Then\n$$\\log Z =\\int q(\\log\\gamma-\\log q)\\,d\\mu +D(q\\,\\mu\\|\\pi\\,\\mu).$$\nIn particular, the first integral is at most $\\log Z$. This identity concerns\nnormalized densities only; it does not assert posterior existence for an\nunspecified statistical model.",
          "uses": [
