          "quote": "A **finite discrete memoryless relay channel** is the three-node instance of [[def-finite-discrete-memoryless-network-and-code]] with source input $X\\in\\mathcal X$, relay input $X_r\\in\\mathcal X_r$, relay observation $Y_r\\in\\mathcal Y_r$, destination observation $Y\\in\\mathcal Y$, and transition law $W(y_r,y\\mid x,x_r)$. For a positive integer message-set size $M$, the one-way message is uniform on $[M]$ and the destination estimates it from $Y^n$.\n\nIn the **relay-only causal model**, $X_i=f_i(M)$ and $X_{r,i}=g_i(Y_r^{i-1})$. In the **full output-feedback model**, both encoders know the past pair $(Y^{i-1},Y_r^{i-1})$, so $X_i=f_i(M,Y^{i-1},Y_r^{i-1})$ and $X_{r,i}=g_i(Y^{i-1},Y_r^{i-1})$. In either model the channel outputs at time $i$ are drawn from $W(\\cdot,\\cdot\\mid X_i,X_{r,i})$; no encoder sees a current-time output before choosing its current input. The rate is $n^{-1}\\ln M$, and achievability uses average error under the uniform message law.",
