        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-two-user-interference-channel-and-strong-regime",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite two-user interference channel** is the two-sender, two-receiver memoryless channel with finite nonempty input alphabets $\\mathcal X_1,\\mathcal X_2$, finite nonempty output alphabets $\\mathcal Y_1,\\mathcal Y_2$, and transition law $W(y_1,y_2|x_1,x_2)$. Independent uniform messages $M_1,M_2$ on finite nonempty sets are encoded separately as $X_1^n=f_1(M_1)$ and $X_2^n=f_2(M_2)$; receiver $i$ estimates $M_i$ from $Y_i^n$. The **strong-interference regime** means that, for every product law $P_{X_1}P_{X_2}$,\n$$I(X_1;Y_1|X_2)\\le I(X_1;Y_2|X_2),\\qquad I(X_2;Y_2|X_1)\\le I(X_2;Y_1|X_1).$$\nThe inequalities are evaluated under the stated product law and the channel. They are universal hypotheses over input laws, not just inequalities at a chosen optimizing distribution.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-finite-strong-interference-block-lift",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the strong-interference inequalities of [[def-finite-two-user-interference-channel-and-strong-regime]]. For independent random vectors $X_1^n,X_2^n$, pass each coordinate through independent uses of the channel. Then\n$$I(X_1^n;Y_1^n|X_2^n)\\le I(X_1^n;Y_2^n|X_2^n),\\qquad I(X_2^n;Y_2^n|X_1^n)\\le I(X_2^n;Y_1^n|X_1^n).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-it-fano-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ take values in an alphabet $\\mathcal X$ of size $m\\ge2$, let $Y$ be finite, and let $\\widehat X$ be an estimator in the same alphabet generated from $Y$, so the joint pmf factors as $p_{XY\\widehat X}=p_{XY}p_{\\widehat X|Y}$. Put $P_e=\\mathbb P[\\widehat X\\ne X]$ and define\n\n$$h_2(t):=-t\\ln t-(1-t)\\ln(1-t),\\qquad 0\\ln0:=0.$$\n\nThen\n\n$$H(X|Y)\\le h_2(P_e)+P_e\\ln(m-1)\\le \\ln2+P_e\\ln m.$$\n\nFor $m=1$, $X$ is constant and $H(X|Y)=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-it-two-user-discrete-memoryless-mac-capacity-region",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the average-joint-error no-feedback code of [[def-it-two-user-discrete-memoryless-mac-code]] over the finite DM-MAC, the capacity region is the closure of the union over all finite $Q$ and laws $P_QP_{X_1|Q}P_{X_2|Q}W$ of\n$$R_1\\le I(X_1;Y\\mid X_2,Q),\\quad R_2\\le I(X_2;Y\\mid X_1,Q),\\quad R_1+R_2\\le I(X_1,X_2;Y\\mid Q).$$\nHere $Q$ is a public time-sharing variable independent of both private messages; conditional on each $Q=q$, the two input laws are a product. This result does not characterize maximal-error capacity.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-it-finite-entropy-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For finite $X,Y,Z$ with a joint pmf,\n\n$$H(X,Y|Z)=H(X|Z)+H(Y|X,Z),$$\n\nand\n\n$$I(X;Y,Z)=I(X;Z)+I(X;Y|Z).$$\n\nIn particular, $I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X|Y)=H(Y)-H(Y|X)$. All terms are finite.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
