    "lem-it-finite-dmc-information-density-concentration": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-discrete-memoryless-channel",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **finite discrete memoryless channel (DMC)** is the channel sequence [[def-channel-sequence]] with fixed finite nonempty input and output alphabets $\\mathcal X,\\mathcal Y$ (with every subset measurable) and one stochastic kernel $W(y\\mid x)$ from $\\mathcal X$ to $\\mathcal Y$ ([[def-finite-stochastic-kernel]]). At every blocklength its input and output alphabets are $\\mathcal X^n,\\mathcal Y^n$, and its block kernel is\n$$W^n(y^n\\mid x^n):=\\prod_{i=1}^n W(y_i\\mid x_i).$$\nThus the same single-use transition law is applied independently at each coordinate, conditional on the input word. This page imposes no feedback or input-cost constraint.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-it-finite-mutual-information",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p_{XY}$ be a joint probability vector on finite alphabets $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$, with marginals $p_X(x):=\\sum_y p_{XY}(x,y)$ and $p_Y(y):=\\sum_xp_{XY}(x,y)$. The marginals are probability vectors by [[lem-it-finite-marginals-and-conditionals-are-pmfs]]. The joint vector is the outcome-weight function of the canonical finite probability space from [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]; its coordinate maps are finite random elements in the sense of [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]. Define mutual information in nats by\n\n$$I(X;Y):=D(p_{XY}\\|p_X\\otimes p_Y)=\\sum_{(x,y):p_{XY}(x,y)>0}p_{XY}(x,y)\\ln\\frac{p_{XY}(x,y)}{p_X(x)p_Y(y)},$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite KL convention of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]] and $(p_X\\otimes p_Y)(x,y):=p_X(x)p_Y(y)$. Every ratio in the displayed sum is positive and finite: if $p_{XY}(x,y)>0$, then each marginal sum is at least that positive entry. The sum is over finite support, so $I(X;Y)$ is finite. The base-$b$ version replaces $\\ln$ by $\\log_b$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Write $\\ln$ for the natural logarithm $\\log$ of [[def-natural-logarithm]]. Unless a base is displayed, every information quantity on this page uses $\\ln$ and is measured in **nats**. For a base $b>1$ and $t>0$, define\n$$\\log_b t:=\\frac{\\ln t}{\\ln b}.$$\nThe denominator is positive: $b>1$, $\\ln$ is strictly increasing, and $\\ln 1=0$ by [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]. A base-$b$ entropy or divergence is obtained by replacing each $\\ln$ in the finite formulas below by $\\log_b$; use $0\\log_b0:=0$ and assign $+\\infty$ to a positive numerator over a zero denominator. The case $b=2$ measures **bits**; $b=e$ gives nats.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
