        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-it-wiretap-secrecy-capacities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix an error criterion $e\\in\\{\\mathrm{av},\\mathrm{max}\\}$ and a secrecy criterion $s\\in\\{\\mathrm{weak},\\mathrm{strong}\\}$. For each code let $P_{\\rm err}^{(n)}=P_{e,\\rm av}^{(n)}$ when $e=\\mathrm{av}$ and $P_{\\rm err}^{(n)}=P_{e,\\rm max}^{(n)}$ when $e=\\mathrm{max}$. A rate $R\\ge0$ in nats per channel use is $(e,s)$-achievable for a finite wiretap channel [[def-it-wiretap-channel]] if there is a sequence of wiretap codes, one at each blocklength $n\\ge1$, such that\n\n$$\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac1n\\ln|\\mathcal M_n|\\ge R,\\qquad P_{\\rm err}^{(n)}\\longrightarrow0,$$\n\nand the sequence satisfies secrecy criterion $s$ from [[def-it-weak-and-strong-secrecy]], with leakage measured by [[def-it-information-leakage-and-equivocation]]. Define $C_{e,s}$ as the supremum of all such rates. Thus this definition gives four operational capacities, distinguished by average or maximal error and weak or strong secrecy.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-it-information-leakage-and-equivocation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For finite random variables $M,Z$, information leakage is the finite mutual information $I(M;Z)$ in nats, as defined in [[def-it-finite-mutual-information]], and equivocation is the finite conditional entropy $H(M\\mid Z)$, as defined in [[def-it-finite-conditional-entropy]]. For an $n$-use code with Eve's observation $Z^n$, its normalized leakage is $n^{-1}I(M;Z^n)$. When $M$ is uniform on a finite message set $\\mathcal M$, its entropy is $H(M)=\\ln|\\mathcal M|$. Zero-probability symbols are handled by the finite entropy and KL conventions cited above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.4",
