            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-it-weak-and-strong-secrecy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Consider a sequence of finite wiretap codes from [[def-it-wiretap-channel]], with uniform message $M_n$ and Eve's observation $Z^n$. Call it reliable when its average error tends to zero; maximal reliability is the stronger condition that its maximal error tends to zero. Among reliable sequences, **weak secrecy** means\n\n$$\\frac1n I(M_n;Z^n)\\longrightarrow 0,$$\n\nand **strong secrecy** means\n\n$$I(M_n;Z^n)\\longrightarrow 0,$$\n\nwhere leakage is the unnormalized finite mutual information of [[def-it-information-leakage-and-equivocation]] and all quantities are in nats. Exact independence at every blocklength, total-variation secrecy, and per-message secrecy are separate conditions and are not identified with either limit here.",
          "uses": [
