          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $p_{XY}$ be a joint probability vector on finite alphabets $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$, with marginals $p_X(x):=\\sum_y p_{XY}(x,y)$ and $p_Y(y):=\\sum_xp_{XY}(x,y)$. The marginals are probability vectors by [[lem-it-finite-marginals-and-conditionals-are-pmfs]]. The joint vector is the outcome-weight function of the canonical finite probability space from [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]; its coordinate maps are finite random elements in the sense of [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]. Define mutual information in nats by\n\n$$I(X;Y):=D(p_{XY}\\|p_X\\otimes p_Y)=\\sum_{(x,y):p_{XY}(x,y)>0}p_{XY}(x,y)\\ln\\frac{p_{XY}(x,y)}{p_X(x)p_Y(y)},$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite KL convention of [[def-qa-35-finite-probability-entropy-and-stochastic-matrices]] and $(p_X\\otimes p_Y)(x,y):=p_X(x)p_Y(y)$. Every ratio in the displayed sum is positive and finite: if $p_{XY}(x,y)>0$, then each marginal sum is at least that positive entry. The sum is over finite support, so $I(X;Y)$ is finite. The base-$b$ version replaces $\\ln$ by $\\log_b$.",
          "uses": [
