    "lem-it-partial-traces-of-a-state-are-states": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qc-density-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be a nonzero finite-dimensional complex inner-product space, with the root library's convention that the inner product is linear in its first argument. A **density operator** on $H$ is a non-negative endomorphism $\\rho:H\\to H$ with $\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho)=1$; non-negative and trace have the meanings of [[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]] and [[def-trace-of-an-endomorphism]]. The zero-dimensional space is excluded: its only endomorphism has trace $0$.\n\nHere $\\ker\\rho=\\{x\\in H:\\rho x=0\\}$, using the kernel of a linear map from [[def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map]]. The **support** of $\\rho$ is the orthogonal complement of its kernel in the root convention:\n\n$$\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho):=\\{x\\in H: \\langle x,y\\rangle=0\\text{ for every }y\\in\\ker\\rho\\}.$$\n\nFor a subspace $S\\subseteq H$, $\\rho$ is **faithful on $S$** when $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)=S$ and the restriction $\\rho|_S:S\\to S$ is positive definite in the sense of [[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]]. This restriction is an endomorphism: if $x\\in S$ and $y\\in\\ker\\rho$, self-adjointness gives $\\langle\\rho x,y\\rangle=\\langle x,\\rho y\\rangle=0$, so $\\rho x\\in(\\ker\\rho)^\\perp=S$.",
          "uses": [
