        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-qc-density-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be a nonzero finite-dimensional complex inner-product space, with the root library's convention that the inner product is linear in its first argument. A **density operator** on $H$ is a non-negative endomorphism $\\rho:H\\to H$ with $\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho)=1$; non-negative and trace have the meanings of [[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]] and [[def-trace-of-an-endomorphism]]. The zero-dimensional space is excluded: its only endomorphism has trace $0$.\n\nHere $\\ker\\rho=\\{x\\in H:\\rho x=0\\}$, using the kernel of a linear map from [[def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map]]. The **support** of $\\rho$ is the orthogonal complement of its kernel in the root convention:\n\n$$\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho):=\\{x\\in H: \\langle x,y\\rangle=0\\text{ for every }y\\in\\ker\\rho\\}.$$\n\nFor a subspace $S\\subseteq H$, $\\rho$ is **faithful on $S$** when $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)=S$ and the restriction $\\rho|_S:S\\to S$ is positive definite in the sense of [[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]]. This restriction is an endomorphism: if $x\\in S$ and $y\\in\\ker\\rho$, self-adjointness gives $\\langle\\rho x,y\\rangle=\\langle x,\\rho y\\rangle=0$, so $\\rho x\\in(\\ker\\rho)^\\perp=S$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-qc-partial-trace",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a complex vector space $H$, write $\\operatorname{End}(H)$ for the set of\nlinear maps $H\\to H$ ([[def-linear-map]]).\n\nLet $H_A,H_B$ be finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces with fixed\nordered orthonormal bases $(e_i)_{i<a}$ and $(f_j)_{j<b}$, respectively. The\nordered product basis of $H_A\\otimes H_B$ has the $A$ index first. For\n$R\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A\\otimes H_B)$ define\n$\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A)$ by\n\n$$\\bigl(\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\bigr)_{i i'}:=\\sum_{j<b}R_{(i,j),(i',j)}.$$\n\nRows are output coordinates and columns are input coordinates, as in\n[[def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map]]. If $b=0$, the sum is\nempty and equals $0$. The notation $\\operatorname{Tr}_B$ means that the second\nfactor is contracted; the first factor and its order are retained.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-qc-tensor-register-and-permutation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **ordered tensor register** consists of a finite ordered list of\nfinite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces $H_0,\\ldots,H_{n-1}$, each\nequipped with a specified ordered orthonormal basis. Its space is\n$H_0\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes H_{n-1}$, with factors retained in the displayed\norder. The product basis is ordered lexicographically by its tuple of factor\nindices. The empty tensor product is $\\mathbb C$ with basis $\\{1\\}$. A\nzero-dimensional factor is allowed in this mathematical construction and makes\nthe tensor space zero; it is not a register under\n[[def-qc-register-and-computational-basis]].\n\nThe inner product is linear in the first argument and conjugate-linear in the\nsecond, and on elementary tensors it is\n\n$$\\left\\langle\\bigotimes_{k<n}x_k,\\bigotimes_{k<n}y_k\\right\\rangle_{\\otimes}:=\\prod_{k<n}\\langle x_k,y_k\\rangle_{{\\rm root},k}.$$ \n\nFor the empty tensor, the right side is the empty product $1$, giving the\nstandard inner product on $\\mathbb C$.\n\nFor a permutation $\\sigma$ of the $n$ factor positions, the **wire permutation**\n$P_\\sigma$ maps the product basis vector indexed by\n$(i_0,\\ldots,i_{n-1})$ to the product basis vector whose output factor order is\n$(\\sigma(0),\\ldots,\\sigma(n-1))$. It is extended linearly; for $n=0$ it is the\nidentity on $\\mathbb C$. The extension is a linear map in the sense of\n[[def-linear-map]].",
