        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-qc-purification-existence-and-unitary-equivalence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every density operator $\\rho$ on a nonzero finite-dimensional complex-inner-product space $H$ has a purification on some finite-dimensional auxiliary space. More precisely, if $r=\\operatorname{rank}(\\rho)$, it has one on an auxiliary space of dimension $r$. If $\\Psi_i\\in H\\otimes K_i$ ($i=1,2$) are any two purifications of the same $\\rho$, then for every integer $m\\ge\\max(\\dim K_1,\\dim K_2)$ and every pair of linear isometric embeddings $\\iota_i:K_i\\to\\mathbb C^m$, where $\\mathbb C^m$ has its standard complex inner product, there is a unitary $U$ on $\\mathbb C^m$ such that\n\n$$ (I_H\\otimes U)(I_H\\otimes\\iota_2)\\Psi_2=(I_H\\otimes\\iota_1)\\Psi_1. $$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-qc-schmidt-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $H,K$ be finite-dimensional complex-inner-product spaces with the tensor-product convention of [[def-qc-tensor-register-and-permutation]]. For every $\\Psi\\in H\\otimes K$ there are an integer $r\\ge0$, positive numbers $s_1,\\ldots,s_r$, and orthonormal families $u_1,\\ldots,u_r\\in H$ and $v_1,\\ldots,v_r\\in K$ such that\n\n$$\\Psi=\\sum_{j=1}^r s_j u_j\\otimes v_j,\\qquad \\|\\Psi\\|^2=\\sum_{j=1}^r s_j^2.$$\n\nFor $\\Psi=0$ take $r=0$ and the empty sum. For any chosen ordered pair of orthonormal bases, the construction below obtains the $s_j$ as the positive singular values of the coefficient matrix of $\\Psi$ in their ordered product basis.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-qc-finite-stinespring-dilation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $H,K$ be finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces and let\n$\\Phi:\\mathcal L(H)\\to\\mathcal L(K)$ be a quantum channel. It has a finite\nKraus representation, and for any chosen such representation with $N$\noperators $K_a:H\\to K$ there are an $N$-dimensional environment $E$ and a\nlinear isometry $V:H\\to K\\otimes E$ such that\n$$\\Phi(X)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E(VXV^\\dagger)\\quad\\text{for every }X\\in\\mathcal L(H).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-it-von-neumann-entropy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be a nonzero finite-dimensional complex inner-product space, with the root convention that its inner product is linear in the first argument, and let $\\rho$ be a density operator on $H$ ([[def-qc-density-operator]]). If $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_d$ are its eigenvalues counted with multiplicity, define its **von Neumann entropy** by\n\n$$S(\\rho):=-\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho\\ln\\rho):=-\\sum_{j:\\lambda_j>0}\\lambda_j\\ln\\lambda_j.$$\n\nThe trace is the finite-dimensional endomorphism trace ([[def-trace-of-an-endomorphism]]), and the spectral definition makes the expression independent of the eigenbasis. The term for a zero eigenvalue is defined to be $0\\ln0:=0$. The default unit is nats, using the natural logarithm ([[def-natural-logarithm]]); the entropy in bits is $S_2(\\rho):=S(\\rho)/\\ln2$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-qc-partial-trace",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a complex vector space $H$, write $\\operatorname{End}(H)$ for the set of\nlinear maps $H\\to H$ ([[def-linear-map]]).\n\nLet $H_A,H_B$ be finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces with fixed\nordered orthonormal bases $(e_i)_{i<a}$ and $(f_j)_{j<b}$, respectively. The\nordered product basis of $H_A\\otimes H_B$ has the $A$ index first. For\n$R\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A\\otimes H_B)$ define\n$\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A)$ by\n\n$$\\bigl(\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\bigr)_{i i'}:=\\sum_{j<b}R_{(i,j),(i',j)}.$$\n\nRows are output coordinates and columns are input coordinates, as in\n[[def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map]]. If $b=0$, the sum is\nempty and equals $0$. The notation $\\operatorname{Tr}_B$ means that the second\nfactor is contracted; the first factor and its order are retained.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
