          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-it-qudit-erasure-channel-complement-and-degradability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $\\mathcal E_{d,\\epsilon}$, choose the Stinespring isometry in [[thm-it-qudit-erasure-channel-is-cptp]], with environment $E=A\\oplus\\mathbb C e_E$. Its complement is\n\n$$\\mathcal E^c_{d,\\epsilon}(X)=\\epsilon J_EXJ_E^*+(1-\\epsilon)\\operatorname{tr}(X)|e_E\\rangle\\langle e_E|.$$\n\nThe channel is degradable for $0\\le\\epsilon\\le\\tfrac12$ and antidegradable for $\\tfrac12\\le\\epsilon\\le1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-it-qudit-erasure-channel",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $A$ be a $d$-dimensional complex inner-product space with $d\\ge1$. Form the orthogonal direct sum $B:=A\\oplus\\mathbb C e$, where $e$ is a unit erasure vector orthogonal to every vector in $A$; the inner product on pairs is $\\langle(x,a),(y,b)\\rangle_B=\\langle x,y\\rangle_A+a\\overline b$. Thus $\\dim B=d+1$ by [[cor-dimension-of-a-direct-sum]]. Let $J:A\\to B$ be the isometric inclusion into the data summand.\n\nFor an erasure probability $\\epsilon\\in[0,1]$, the **$d$-dimensional quantum erasure channel** is the linear map\n\n$$\\mathcal E_{d,\\epsilon}(X):=(1-\\epsilon)JXJ^*+ \\epsilon\\operatorname{tr}(X)|e\\rangle\\langle e|,\\qquad X\\in\\mathcal L(A).$$\n\nFor a density operator $\\rho$, this outputs the data state with probability $1-\\epsilon$ and the orthogonal erasure flag with probability $\\epsilon$. The definition is on the full operator space, and its channel property is established in [[thm-it-qudit-erasure-channel-is-cptp]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-it-information-units-and-logarithm-base",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Write $\\ln$ for the natural logarithm $\\log$ of [[def-natural-logarithm]]. Unless a base is displayed, every information quantity on this page uses $\\ln$ and is measured in **nats**. For a base $b>1$ and $t>0$, define\n$$\\log_b t:=\\frac{\\ln t}{\\ln b}.$$\nThe denominator is positive: $b>1$, $\\ln$ is strictly increasing, and $\\ln 1=0$ by [[thm-natural-logarithm-laws]]. A base-$b$ entropy or divergence is obtained by replacing each $\\ln$ in the finite formulas below by $\\log_b$; use $0\\log_b0:=0$ and assign $+\\infty$ to a positive numerator over a zero denominator. The case $b=2$ measures **bits**; $b=e$ gives nats.",
          "uses": [
