          "quote": "Let $H$ and $K$ be finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces and write $\\mathcal L(H)$ and $\\mathcal L(K)$ for their vector spaces of complex-linear endomorphisms with pointwise operations ([[def-dimension]], [[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]], [[def-vector-space-of-linear-maps]], [[prop-linear-maps-form-a-vector-space]]). Let $\\Phi:\\mathcal L(H)\\to\\mathcal L(K)$ be complex-linear. For each integer $r\\ge1$, put $R=\\mathbb C^r$ with its standard ordered register basis ([[def-qc-register-and-computational-basis]]). Write an operator $X\\in\\mathcal L(H\\otimes R)$ in its unique $r\\times r$ block form $X=[X_{ij}]$ with $X_{ij}\\in\\mathcal L(H)$, and define $\\Phi^{(r)}(X)$ to have blocks $[\\Phi(X_{ij})]$ on $K\\otimes R$. Lemma [[lem-qc-finite-ancilla-amplification-is-block-map]] proves that this block rule defines a linear map on the full operator space for every finite $r$. For $r=1$, the extension is $\\Phi$ under the canonical unitary identifications $x\\mapsto x\\otimes e_0$ and $y\\mapsto y\\otimes e_0$ proved in that lemma.\n\nThe map $\\Phi$ is **completely positive** if, for every finite $r\\ge1$, $\\Phi^{(r)}$ sends every nonnegative operator on $H\\otimes R$ to a nonnegative operator on $K\\otimes R$ ([[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]]). The quantifier includes $r=1$; complete positivity therefore implies positivity. No Kraus, Choi, or Stinespring characterization is part of this definition.",
