          "claim": "The number $r$ is a primitive tenth root of unity, so its minimal\npolynomial is $\\Phi_{10}(x)=x^4-x^3+x^2-x+1$ and $[\\mathbb Q(r):\\mathbb Q]=4$.\nIts maximal real subfield is $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt5)$, and the nontrivial\nconjugate of $\\phi$ is $(1-\\sqrt5)/2=-\\phi^{-1}$; hence the field norm of\n$\\phi$ from $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt5)$ to $\\mathbb Q$ is $-1$. Therefore $\\phi$ is\nnot a square in that real subfield, since the norm of a square would be a\nnonnegative rational square. [given, A1, algebra]",
