    "thm-qc-tqc-toric-logical-algebra": {
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qc-tqc-toric-logical-loops",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "On the periodic torus, define four Pauli representatives\n\n$$\\bar Z_1=\\prod_{i\\in\\mathbb Z_L}Z_{h_{i,0}},\\qquad \\bar Z_2=\\prod_{j\\in\\mathbb Z_L}Z_{v_{0,j}},\\qquad \\bar X_1=\\prod_{j\\in\\mathbb Z_L}X_{h_{0,j}},\\qquad \\bar X_2=\\prod_{i\\in\\mathbb Z_L}X_{v_{i,0}}.$$\n\nThe $\\bar Z$ supports are the two fixed fundamental primal cycles. The\n$\\bar X$ supports are the dual cycles crossing them. These operators are\ncalled the toric logical loops. As verified below, each is a nontrivial\nlogical Pauli class in the periodic toric code.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-qc-pauli-commutator-is-symplectic-pairing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $a=(x,z)$ and $b=(x',z')$ in $V_n$ and any phases $k,k'$, \n\n$$P(k,x,z)P(k',x',z')=(-1)^{\\omega(a,b)}P(k',x',z')P(k,x,z).$$\n\nThus the Pauli operators commute exactly when their labels are symplectically orthogonal.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-qc-stabilizer-syndrome-and-logical-operators",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$ and let $S$ be a stabilizer subgroup generated by an ordered independent\nHermitian list $g_1,\\ldots,g_r$. For a phase-aware Pauli error $E$, its\n**syndrome** is the bit string $s(E)\\in\\mathbb F_2^r$ defined by\n\n$$E g_j=(-1)^{s_j(E)}g_jE,\\qquad s_j(E):=\\omega(\\lambda(E),\\lambda(g_j)).$$\n\nHere $\\lambda(P(k,x,z))=(x,z)$ is the binary Pauli label and\n$\\omega((x,z),(x',z'))=x\\cdot z'+z\\cdot x'$. The scalar center of\n$\\mathcal P_n$ is $Z_n=\\{i^kI:k\\in\\mathbb Z/4\\mathbb Z\\}$, and the\n**normalizer** is $N(S)=\\{P\\in\\mathcal P_n:Pg=gP\\text{ for every }g\\in S\\}$.\nThe **logical Pauli classes** form the quotient group\n\n$$N(S)/(Z_nS).$$\n\nA normalizer element is logically nontrivial exactly when it lies in\n$N(S)\\setminus Z_nS$. Multiplication by a scalar phase does not change a\nsyndrome or logical class.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-qc-tqc-toric-code-dimension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the periodic $L\\times L$ toric checks with $L\\ge3$, the common $+1$\neigenspace is a valid stabilizer code on $n=2L^2$ qubits. Its independent\ncheck rank is $r=2L^2-2$, so its dimension is\n$2^{n-r}=4=2^2$ and it encodes two logical qubits.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-qc-stabilizer-logical-pauli-quotient-dimension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$ and let $S$ be an $n$-qubit stabilizer group generated by $r$ independent\ncommuting Hermitian Pauli checks with $-I\\notin S$. Then the logical Pauli\nquotient $N(S)/(Z_nS)$ is a binary vector space of dimension $2(n-r)$.\nConsequently, it is nontrivial exactly when the stabilizer code encodes at\nleast one qubit.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-qc-tqc-toric-mutual-sign",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $P$ be a primal path and $Q$ a dual path whose edge supports share\nexactly one physical qubit. Then their string Paulis anticommute:\n$Z(P)X(Q)=-X(Q)Z(P)$. This is the algebraic mutual e/m string sign.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
