          "quote": "Let $S_+=\\mathbb C^2$, $S_-=(\\mathbb C^2)^*$, where the algebraic dual is defined in [[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]], and set $V=S_+\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}S_-$. By [[lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring]], $\\mathbb C$ is a commutative ring; use the scalar actions to regard $S_+$ as a right module and $S_-$ as a left module. Use their module tensor product from [[def-tensor-product-of-modules-by-generators-and-relations]]; its universal property is [[thm-universal-property-of-module-tensor-products]], and its unique $\\mathbb C$-module structure is given by [[thm-commutative-ring-module-structure-on-a-tensor-product]]. Thus $V$ is a complex vector space, every tensor is a finite sum of elementary tensors, and bilinear formulas on $S_+\\times S_-$ descend to linear maps on $V$.\n\nLet\n$$ \\mathfrak l=\\mathfrak{sl}(S_+)\\oplus\\mathfrak{sl}(\\mathbb C^2). $$\nUsing the standard basis of $S_+$, both summands are copies of $\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\mathbb C)$, the traceless complex matrix Lie algebra with commutator from [[def-classical-complex-matrix-lie-algebras]].\nUse the natural action of the first factor on $S_+$, the dual action $B\\cdot\\phi=-\\phi B$ of the second factor on $S_-$, and the tensor action on $V=S_+\\otimes S_-$:\n$$ (A,B)\\cdot(u\\otimes\\phi)=Au\\otimes\\phi-u\\otimes\\phi B. $$\nLet the even part be $\\mathfrak g_{\\bar0}=\\mathfrak l\\oplus V$, with bracket\n$$ [(L,v),(L',v')]=([L,L'],L\\cdot v'-L'\\cdot v), $$\nand let the odd part be $\\mathfrak g_{\\bar1}=S_+\\oplus S_-$. The even action on the odd part is $(L,v)\\cdot s=L\\cdot s$, so translations $V$ act trivially. Define the odd bracket by\n$$ [u,u']=0,\\qquad[\\phi,\\phi']=0,\\qquad[u,\\phi]=[\\phi,u]=u\\otimes\\phi $$\nfor $u,u'\\in S_+$ and $\\phi,\\phi'\\in S_-$. Extend all brackets bilinearly and by graded skew symmetry. This is the **complexified spinor-form bracket data** for the four-dimensional $N=1$ super-Poincare algebra. A separate theorem proves that t