          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-filtered-maurer-cartan-gauge-path",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $W$ be a complete filtered shifted L∞-algebra over a characteristic-zero field $k$. Put $P=F^1W$ with its induced filtration. This is closed and complete in each degree: it is the kernel of $W\\to W/F^1W$, and compatible cosets in $P/F^pW$ have their unique $W$-limit in that kernel. All $q_n$ preserve $P$.\n\nThe **completed polynomial coefficients** and **completed polynomial differential forms** mean\n\n$$P[t]^{\\wedge}=\\varprojlim_p (P/F^pW)\\otimes_k k[t],\\qquad P\\widehat\\otimes\\Omega=\\varprojlim_p (P/F^pW)\\otimes_k k[t,dt],\\quad |t|=0,\\quad |dt|=1.$$\n\nThese are compatible polynomials in each quotient; their degrees need not have a common bound. Evaluation at $0,1$, differentiation, and integration from $0$ to $t$ are defined in every quotient by polynomial formulas and commute with reduction; integration uses $\\int_0^t u^jdu=t^{j+1}/(j+1)$. Operations on these spaces are extended continuously, with each coefficient of a fixed quotient a finite sum.\n\nWrite $C(w)=\\sum_{n\\ge1}q_n(w^n)/n!$ and $q_1^w(a)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}q_{n+1}(w^n,a)/n!$. An **elementary gauge path** is a pair $w\\in P^0[t]^{\\wedge}$, $a\\in P^{-1}[t]^{\\wedge}$ such that $C(w)=0$ and $w'=q_1^w(a)$. Its endpoints are $w(0),w(1)$. **Gauge equivalence** is the equivalence relation generated by endpoints of these paths. A constant path has $a=0$; reversal is $(w(1-t),-a(1-t))$; finite chains give transitivity. Polynomial concatenation is not required.\n\nFor the polynomial-form interpretation, forms are placed on the right. For homogeneous $x_i\\in P$, $\\alpha_i\\in\\Omega$, the extension of $q_n$ has scalar sign $(-1)^{\\sum_{i<j}|\\alpha_i||x_j|}$; its unary part also contains $(-1)^{|x|}x\\otimes d\\alpha$. The degree-zero form $z=w-a\\,dt$ then satisfies $C_{\\Omega}(z)=C(w)+(w'-q_1^w(a))dt$. Thus these paths are precisely Maurer–Cartan elements over the completed polynomial interval. This fixes a gauge construction using the full operations, rather than choosing an arbitrary equivalence relation on a solution set.",
