          "quote": "Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space and let $n\\ge0$. Set\n$\\Lambda^0H:=\\mathbb C$. For $n\\ge1$, the **algebraic $n$-fold tensor\nproduct** $H^{\\otimes_{\\rm alg}n}$ is the complex vector space generated by\nsymbols $x_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes x_n$ subject to complex linearity in each\nslot. Equivalently, it is the quotient of the free complex vector space on\n$H^n$ by the span of the coordinate-wise additivity and scalar-linearity\nrelations. Give it the sesquilinear form determined on elementary tensors by\n\n$$\\langle x_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes x_n, y_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes y_n\\rangle =\\prod_{i=1}^n\\langle x_i,y_i\\rangle,$$\n\nwhich is well defined because the product is linear in each first-slot vector\nand conjugate-linear in each second-slot vector. Apply the choice-free sequential Hilbert completion constructed in proof steps 1.1–7.1 below to\n$H^{\\otimes_{\\rm alg}n}$ in the induced norm to obtain the Hilbert tensor\npower $H^{\\otimes n}$. Use the action convention\n$U_\\pi(x_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes x_n)=\nx_{\\pi^{-1}(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes x_{\\pi^{-1}(n)}$ for $\\pi\\in S_n$.\nThe **Hilbert exterior power** $\\Lambda^nH$ is the range of the orthogonal\nprojection\n\n$$A_n:=\\frac1{n!}\\sum_{\\pi\\in S_n}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\pi)U_\\pi.$$\n\nFor $x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in H$, write\n$x_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge x_n:=\\sqrt{n!}\\,A_n\n(x_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes x_n)$. Its inner product is\n\n$$\\langle x_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge x_n, y_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge y_n\\rangle =\\det[\\langle x_i,y_j\\rangle]_{i,j=1}^n.$$\n\nIf $T:H\\to H$ is bounded, its **induced operator** $\\Lambda^nT$ is the\nrestriction of $T^{\\otimes n}$ to $\\Lambda^nH$; equivalently\n$(\\Lambda^nT)(x_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge x_n)=Tx_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge Tx_n$.\nSet $\\Lambda^0T=I_{\\mathbb C}$. For every bound $C$ of $T$, $C^n$ is a\nbound for $\\Lambda^nT$, and\n$\\Lambda^n(ST)=\\Lambda^nS\\,\\Lambda^nT$ for bounded $S,T$.",
