          "source": "lem-pi-three-so-three-generated-by-the-quaternion-double-cover",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathbb H$ be the quaternions with conjugate $x\\mapsto\\bar x$ and norm $N$,\nlet $S^3=\\{q\\in\\mathbb H:N(q)=1\\}$ be the unit sphere, and let\n$\\operatorname{Im}\\mathbb H=\\mathbb Ri\\oplus\\mathbb Rj\\oplus\\mathbb Rk$ carry the\nrestricted Euclidean inner product, identified with $\\mathbb R^3$ through the\nbasis $(i,j,k)$. Let $\\operatorname{SO}(3)$ be the group of real $3\\times3$\nmatrices $R$ with $R^{\\mathsf T}R=I$ and $\\det R=1$, carrying the subspace\ntopology of the nine entries, and for $q\\in S^3$ let $\\rho(q)$ be the linear map\nof $\\operatorname{Im}\\mathbb H$ defined by\n$$\\rho(q)(v)=qvq^{-1},$$\nwritten in the basis $(i,j,k)$ as a real $3\\times3$ matrix. Then:\n\n1. $\\rho:S^3\\to\\operatorname{SO}(3)$ is a continuous surjective group\n   homomorphism with kernel $\\{\\pm1\\}$, and it is a two-sheeted covering map;\n   consequently $\\operatorname{SO}(3)$ is homeomorphic to the orbit space\n   $S^3/\\{\\pm1\\}$.\n2. For every covering $p:E\\to B$, every $e_0\\in E$ and every integer $n\\ge2$,\n   the induced homomorphism $p_*:\\pi_n(E,e_0)\\to\\pi_n(B,p(e_0))$ is an\n   isomorphism. In particular\n   $\\rho_*:\\pi_3(S^3,1)\\to\\pi_3(\\operatorname{SO}(3),I)$ is an isomorphism.\n3. $\\pi_3(S^3,1)\\cong\\mathbb Z$ by degree, and $\\rho_*$ carries the degree-one\n   generator of $\\pi_3(S^3,1)$ to the class $[\\rho]$; hence\n   $\\pi_3(\\operatorname{SO}(3),I)\\cong\\mathbb Z$ is generated by $[\\rho]$.\n4. The clutching construction over the equatorial $S^3$ with clutching map\n   $\\rho$ produces an oriented rank-three real vector bundle\n   $E_\\rho\\to S^4$ which is not trivial.",
