          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "We compute the actual component generators rather than infer them from the KO table. At $q=0$, real involutions are classified up to orthogonal conjugacy by their eigenspace dimensions; their fixed-rank orbits are real Grassmannians. At $q=1$ the mass parameter is $g\\in O(n)$ relative to $g_0$. At $q=2$ it is an orthogonal complex structure $A$, with orbit $O(2n)/U(n)$: an adapted basis consists of pairs $(v,Av)$, and its stabilizer is precisely the complex-linear isometry group. The latter is connected by complex plane rotations, whereas $O(2n)$ has its two determinant components. At $q=4$, the fixed $J_1,J_2$ give a quaternionic multiplicity space. Put $K=J_1J_2$; then $A=KS$ with $S=-KA$ a quaternionic-linear symmetric involution. Its eigenspace orbit is $Sp(k+m)/(Sp(k)\\times Sp(m))$. Hence the nonzero stable difference groups in these rows are the real rank difference, the determinant parity, the complex-orientation parity, and the quaternionic rank difference. Their elementary differences are respectively one opposite real line, one reflected real isometry, one reversed complex line, and one opposite quaternionic line.",
