          "quote": "Let $Q$ be a finite acyclic quiver and $k=\\mathbb F_q$. A representation is\nfinite-dimensional at every vertex, with one linear map for each arrow. The\ncategory $\\operatorname{Rep}_k(Q)$ is essentially small. Use a small skeleton\n$\\mathcal R_Q$, for example the one obtained by fixing the spaces $k^{d_i}$\nand retaining one arrow-matrix tuple from each orbit under vertexwise change\nof basis. This skeleton is a hereditary finitary abelian category in the sense of\n[[def-finite-field-hereditary-hall-category]]. Its simple objects are the\nvertex representations $S_i$, its Grothendieck group is freely generated by\n$[S_i]$ and is isomorphic to $\\mathbb Z^{Q_0}$, and\n$$ \\dim_k\\operatorname{YExt}^1(S_i, S_j)=c_{ij}, $$\nwhere $c_{ij}$ is the number of arrows $i\\to j$.\nFor any two representations $V,W$, its Euler form is explicitly\n$$ \\langle[V],[W]\\rangle =\\sum_{i\\in Q_0}\\dim V_i\\dim W_i -\\sum_{a:i\\to j}\\dim V_i\\dim W_j. $$",
