          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $E$ be a finite-dimensional real vector space with a positive definite symmetric bilinear form $(\\ ,\\ )$. A **finite reduced crystallographic root system** is a finite subset $\\Phi\\subseteq E\\setminus\\{0\\}$ spanning $E$, such that for every $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\Phi$ the integer $\\langle\\beta,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle=2(\\beta,\\alpha)/(\\alpha,\\alpha)$ is integral, the reflection $s_\\alpha(x)=x-\\langle x,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle\\alpha$ preserves $\\Phi$, and the only scalar multiples of $\\alpha$ in $\\Phi$ are $\\alpha,-\\alpha$. The **Weyl group** $W$ is the subgroup of linear isometries of $E$ generated by these reflections. A **root reflection** means one of the $s_\\alpha$. The empty root system on $E=0$ is allowed, with $W=\\{1\\}$.\n\nChoose $t\\in E$ such that $(t,\\alpha)\\ne0$ for every root. Put $\\Phi^+=\\{\\alpha:(t,\\alpha)>0\\}$ and $\\Phi^-=-\\Phi^+$. A positive root is **simple** if it is not the sum of two positive roots. The following positive-root lemma proves that the simple roots form a basis $\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_r$ and that the simple reflections $s_i=s_{\\alpha_i}$ generate $W$; no such assertion is assumed by this definition. Once proved, let $\\ell(w)$ be the least number of simple reflections in a word for $w$ and let $\\operatorname{Inv}(w)=\\{\\alpha\\in\\Phi^+:w\\alpha\\in\\Phi^-\\}$. A word is reduced if its length equals $\\ell(w)$.\n\nIdentify $E$ with its dual by the displayed form, so $\\alpha^\\vee=2\\alpha/(\\alpha,\\alpha)$. The root and coroot groups are $Q=\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\Phi}\\mathbb Z\\alpha$ and $Q^\\vee=\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\Phi}\\mathbb Z\\alpha^\\vee$. The **weight group** is $P=\\{\\lambda\\in E:(\\lambda,\\alpha^\\vee)\\in\\mathbb Z\\text{ for all }\\alpha\\in\\Phi\\}$; the positive-root lemma proves it is a lattice with basis the vectors dual to the simple coroots. Put $Q_+=\\sum_i\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\alpha_i$, and write $\\mu\\le\\lambda$ when $\\lambda-\\mu\\in Q_+$. The open and closed positive chambers are $\\mathcal C=\\{x:(x,\\alpha_i)>0\\text{ for all }i\\}$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal C}=\\{x:(x,\\alpha_i)\\ge0\\text{ for all }i\\}$. A weight is dominant if it belongs to $P\\cap\\overline{\\mathcal C}$. More generally chambers are the connected components of $E\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\alpha\\in\\Phi}\\alpha^\\perp$. The closed-chamber representative and stabilizer assertions are proved later, not included as axioms.",
