          "source": "thm-normalized-rational-vanishing-cycles-have-coherent-operations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC, work with rational analytic sheaves, and let $f:X\\to\\mathbb C$ be regular on a smooth separated finite-type complex variety. With $i:f^{-1}(0)\\hookrightarrow X$, define the **normalized rational vanishing cycles**\n$$V_f A=i^*R\\Gamma_{\\{\\operatorname{Re}f\\le0\\}}A.$$\nThey are viewed on $X$ by closed extension when necessary. Positive angular continuation specifies their monodromy. The standard constant-input coefficient $V_f\\mathbb Q_X$ is bounded algebraically constructible. It has $V_0\\mathbb Q_X=\\mathbb Q_X$ and\n$$H^k(V_f\\mathbb Q_X)_x=\\widetilde H^{k-1}(F_{f,x};\\mathbb Q),$$\nwhere $F_{f,x}$ is the local Milnor fibre. The following comparisons are natural maps of the ambient support construction. The asserted Thom–Sebastiani isomorphisms are for constant inputs and their constant shifts, external products and smooth pullbacks; normalized duality below supplies the required standard dual coefficients. Proper commutation has the displayed proper-image input, and proper images remain bounded constructible. No Thom–Sebastiani isomorphism for unrestricted proper-image inputs is asserted:\n\n1. smooth pullback commutes with $V$, with the positive complex orientation for exceptional pullback;\n2. for proper $p:E\\to X$ with $E$ smooth, $V_fRp_*\\mathbb Q_E\\simeq Rp_*V_{fp}\\mathbb Q_E$; applying this to the unit gives $V_f\\mathbb Q_X\\to Rp_*V_{fp}\\mathbb Q_E$;\n3. for $A=\\mathbb Q_X[a]$ and $B=\\mathbb Q_Y[b]$, including their external products and smooth pullbacks, the natural support-product/inclusion map is a Thom–Sebastiani isomorphism $V_fA\\boxtimes V_gB\\simeq k^*V_{f+g}(A\\boxtimes B)$ on the product zero fibre, associative, unital and compatible with the two tensor-product monodromies;\n4. normalized duality gives $D V_f\\mathbb Q_X\\simeq V_fD\\mathbb Q_X$, hence $D V_f\\mathbb Q_X\\simeq V_f\\mathbb Q_X[2\\dim X]$;\n5. complex affine fibrations give oriented homotopy-invariance isomorphisms.\n\nThe proper/smooth and external-product comparisons commute with composition, units and Cartesian pasting for these maps. This is a rational constructible statement; it asserts no mixed Hodge enrichment or arbitrary-stack formalism.",
