          "claim": "We first give an indexed shuffle identity, including an explicit way to expand its right side. Pfaffians with arbitrary ordered lists are interpreted alternatingly, and vanish on repeated indices. If $h\\ge h'$ are positive even integers, $1\\le s\\le h'$, $U=(u_1,\\ldots,u_{h+s})$, and $C=(c_1,\\ldots,c_{h'-s})$, put $\\epsilon(D,U\\setminus D)$ equal to the sign of the permutation listing first the positions $D$ and then their complement, both increasingly. Then [given, algebra]\n$$\nF(U,C)=\\sum_{D\\subseteq[h+s],\\ |D|=h}\\epsilon(D,U\\setminus D)P_{U_D}P_{(U_{D^c},C)}\\ \\in\\ (P_H:|H|\\ge h+2).\n$$\nHere is a polynomial identity proving this membership, rather than a set-theoretic rank argument. Use exterior variables $e_1,\\ldots,e_{h+s},f_1,\\ldots,f_{h'-s}$, in that order, and put $\\Omega=\\sum_{i<j}a_{u_i u_j}e_i e_j$, $\\Theta=\\sum_{i,j}a_{u_i c_j}e_i f_j$, and $\\Psi=\\sum_{i<j}a_{c_i c_j}f_i f_j$. The displayed sum is the coefficient of $e_1\\cdots e_{h+s}f_1\\cdots f_{h'-s}$ in\n$$\n\\operatorname{pr}_{h+s,h'-s}\\left(\\frac{\\Omega^{h/2}}{(h/2)!}\\frac{(\\Omega+\\Theta+\\Psi)^{h'/2}}{(h'/2)!}\\right)=\\sum_{2r+q=s,\\ q+2l=h'-s}\\frac{\\Omega^{h/2+r}\\Theta^q\\Psi^l}{(h/2)!r!q!l!}.\n$$\nHere $\\operatorname{pr}_{a,b}$ means projection to exterior bidegree $(a,b)$ in the $e$ and $f$ variables. For $r\\ge1$, expand the coefficient of $\\Omega^{h/2+r}/(h/2+r)!$: each coefficient is a Pfaffian of size $h+2r$. For $r=0$, $q=s\\ge1$; expand $\\Theta^q$ as a sum of products of $\\theta_j f_j$, where $\\theta_j=\\sum_i a_{u_i c_j}e_i$, and single out its first factor. Each coefficient of $\\Omega^{h/2}\\theta_j/(h/2)!$ is, with its exterior sign, $P_{(U_E,c_j)}$ for $|E|=h+1$: this follows by expanding the Pfaffian along $c_j$. Expanding the remaining exterior factors gives a completely specified finite sum of larger Pfaffians times polynomials. Thus the identity proves the asserted ideal membership. Repeated actual labels cause no difficulty because the identity is alternating in the formally labelled positions. Larger Pfaffians themselves belong to the ideal of size $h+2$ by repeated expansion along the first index.",
