          "claim": "Assuming the function known below $N$, switching crossing $c$ transports a proposed value $x$ to $T_c(x)=-x+tC_c(D)$. Repeating this switch is the identity because its two smoothings do not depend on the crossing sign. Two switches $c,j$ commute as transports: applying the lower-crossing skein relation at the remaining crossing in each smoothing gives $C_c(D)+C_c(S_jD)=t\\sum_{u\\in\\{E_c,e_c\\},v\\in\\{E_j,e_j\\}}L(uvD)=C_j(D)+C_j(S_cD)$. Consequently $T_jT_c(x)=x-tC_c(D)+tC_j(S_cD)=x-tC_j(D)+tC_c(S_jD)=T_cT_j(x)$. Thus an expansion along a fixed set of switches is independent of their order. Explicitly, for an ordered list $c_1,\\ldots,c_k$ and $D_j=S_{c_j}\\cdots S_{c_1}D$, its value obtained from a prescribed terminal value $u(D_k)$ is $(-1)^ku(D_k)+t\\sum_{j=1}^k(-1)^{j-1}C_{c_j}(D_{j-1})$. This identity follows successively from the two-term transport rule, so it uses only already-defined evaluations of diagrams with at most $N-1$ crossings.",
