          "claim": "For a directed point $p$ on a one-component diagram, switch exactly those crossings first encountered underneath when following the component from $p$. The terminal diagram $U_p$ is descending and is an unknot: lift its traversed part above the untraversed part and draw that part away successively until only framing curls remain. Give it terminal value $b^{w(U_p)}$, where $w$ is oriented writhe. Define $Q(D,p)$ by the switch expansion of step 2.1 with this terminal value, and define $L(D)=(Q(D,p)+Q(D,\\bar p))/2$, where $\\bar p$ reverses the direction at the same point. If the diagram is itself descending for some directed point, prescribe its value directly as $b^{w(D)}$. For a multi-component diagram having a component over all the others, prescribe $L(D)=hL(C)L(D-C)$, with $C$ considered alone. If no component is over all the others, for every component $C$ choose a point and either direction, switch its undercrossings with other components so that it lies over all the others, and use terminal value $hL(C)L(D-C)$. Define $L(D)$ as the average of these $2r$ switch expansions, where $r$ is the number of components. The terminal factors are already defined: single-component diagrams are defined first at level $N$, and deleting one component decreases component number if the remaining crossing number is still $N$. The expansion sums have fewer crossings.",
