        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "Choose a direction, order and initial point on every closed component as well as the standard directions and order of the boundary strands. A crossing is incorrect if first encountered underneath. The skein relation replaces an incorrect crossing by its reversal and two diagrams with one fewer crossing. Induct first on the total crossing number and, for the unchanged-crossing term, on incorrect crossings of this fixed traversal. Reversal changes no component or traversal and removes exactly that incorrect crossing. Smoothing terms use the outer induction, with newly chosen traversals if needed. Hence the quotient is spanned by descending diagrams. A descending component is unknotted; successively lifting its traversed portion above its remaining portion and pulling that initial arc away leaves only framing curls. Earlier components lie above later ones, so closed components can be separated and removed. Removing curls multiplies by powers of $\\lambda$, and removing circles by $\\delta$. The surviving diagrams are descending with no closed components and no self-crossings.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "induction"
