        {
          "id": "derive-4-1",
          "claim": "Close all boundary strands outside the rectangle without new crossings and evaluate by step 1.1, multiplying by $\\delta^{-n}$; call the resulting linear functional $\\epsilon$. Let $*$ reflect a diagram top to bottom. For any choice of diagrams $U_c$ with connector $c$, form $B=(\\epsilon(U_cU_d^*))_{c,d}$. Specialize $\\lambda=1,z_0=0$ to $\\mathbb Z[\\delta^{\\pm1}]$. Switching crossings and deleting curls then have no effect, so the entry becomes $\\delta^{k_c+k_d+\\ell(c,d)-n}$, where $k_c$ is the number of closed components of $U_c$ and $\\ell(c,d)$ counts the alternating cycles in the union of the two matching edge sets on the same $2n$ vertices. This is at most $n$, and equals $n$ precisely when every cycle has two vertices, that is, $c=d$. Factor the units $\\delta^{k_c}$ from row $c$ and $\\delta^{k_d}$ from column $d$. The remaining matrix has diagonal $1$ and off-diagonal terms of strictly negative $\\delta$-degree. The determinant of this remaining matrix has constant term $1$, because every nonidentity permutation uses an off-diagonal entry. Therefore the specialized determinant is nonzero. Therefore $\\det B\\ne0$ in the domain $\\Lambda_0$. A relation among the $U_c$, paired with every $U_d^*$, has zero coefficient vector over $\\operatorname{Frac}(\\Lambda_0)$ and hence over $\\Lambda_0$. This proves independence of any representatives with distinct connectors, in particular the $T_c$.",
