          "id": "derive-7-1",
          "claim": "We prove $W_{m+1}=W_m\\{1,g_m,e_m\\}W_m$, with braces meaning linear span. Induct on $m$; it is immediate at $m=1$ from the quadratic reduction in step 6.1. To prove closure at the next rank, use the lower-rank expression for the factor between two occurrences of $g_m$ or $e_m$. Its outer $W_{m-1}$ factors commute with these two generators, so only $\\chi\\eta\\psi$ need reduction, with $\\chi,\\psi\\in\\{g_m,e_m\\}$ and $\\eta\\in\\{1,g_{m-1},e_{m-1}\\}$. For $\\eta=1$, quadratic, loop and untwisting relations suffice. For $\\eta=g_{m-1}$ the four reductions are braid, $g_mg_{m-1}e_m=e_{m-1}e_m$, $e_mg_{m-1}g_m=e_me_{m-1}$, and $e_mg_{m-1}e_m=\\lambda e_m$. For $\\eta=e_{m-1}$ they are $g_me_{m-1}g_m=g_{m-1}^{-1}e_mg_{m-1}^{-1}$, $g_me_{m-1}e_m=g_{m-1}^{-1}e_m$, its word reversal, and $e_me_{m-1}e_m=e_m$. Every result contains at most one rank-$m$ generator between $W_m$ factors. This proves the asserted span.",
