          "claim": "Put $f_1=e_1$ and $f_k=(g_1\\cdots g_{2k-2})f_{k-1}e_{2k-1}(g_{2k-2}\\cdots g_1)$. Define $C_j=e_je_{j+2}\\cdots e_{2k-j}$ for $1\\le j\\le k$. Then $f_k=C_kC_{k-1}\\cdots C_2C_1C_2\\cdots C_k$. To verify this formula inductively, substitute the formula for $f_{k-1}$ into the recursion. Move $e_{2k-1}$ into the central odd-index product and move the other $e$-columns through the two $g$-strings using step 7.2. The remaining middle identity is $(g_1\\cdots g_{2j})(e_1e_3\\cdots e_{2j-1})=(e_2e_4\\cdots e_{2j})(e_1e_3\\cdots e_{2j-1})$: group its left side into $(g_1g_2e_1)(g_3g_4e_3)\\cdots(g_{2j-1}g_{2j}e_{2j-1})$, replace each by $e_{2i}e_{2i-1}$, and commute the even terms left. Apply also its word reversal. The resulting columns are exactly the displayed diamond expression. Hence $\\alpha(f_k)=r_{2k}(f_k)=f_k$, and its diagram $F_k=\\phi(f_k)$ is the crossing-free nested pairing $i\\leftrightarrow2k+1-i$ at top and bottom, by successively applying these cup slides.",
