        {
          "id": "derive-8-3",
          "claim": "The positive permutation braid $g_\\pi$ is well defined for $\\pi\\in S_n$: use a reduced adjacent-transposition expression. Any two strands in its positive braid cross at most once, and conversely a positive braid with that property has crossing count equal to the inversion number of its permutation. In our positive left-over-right convention, increasing top-vertex order makes them descending: a pair that crosses once has its initially left strand still on the left just before that crossing, so that strand crosses over. Their uniqueness as braid words can also be checked by a monotone sorting procedure. Bring the strand with the rightmost target endpoint to its canonical rightmost route, sliding its crossings past crossings of other strands; a disjoint slide is distant commutation, and a slide past a crossing sharing one strand is the three-strand braid move. Since two strands cross at most once, the partners to be passed are exactly the inversion partners prescribed by the permutation, so this removes the same list of crossings in any diagram. Delete that strand and repeat. Thus both braid words reduce by distant and braid relations to the same recursively sorted wiring, giving equality in $W_n$, without pulling an arbitrary tangle isotopy back through $\\phi$. If $\\pi$ is an $(a,b)$ shuffle, its first $a$ and last $b$ images increase. Every permutation factors uniquely as a shuffle followed by a block permutation, with lengths adding, by sorting its two blocks; hence its positive lift factors likewise. Finally if $\\pi^{-1}(i+1)=\\pi^{-1}(i)+1=j+1$, then $g_ig_\\pi=g_\\pi g_j$ and $e_ig_\\pi=g_\\pi e_j$. For the cup identity, induct on the length of $\\pi$. A leftmost letter distant from $i$ commutes with $e_i$; the other possibilities are $g_{i+1}g_i$ or $g_{i-1}g_i$, and the cup-slide equality in step 7.2 moves the cup past that pair. Removing it decreases length by two, completing the induction.",
