        {
          "id": "derive-9-1",
          "claim": "For $i<k$ one has $g_i^{\\pm1}f_k=g_{2k-i}^{\\pm1}f_k$ and $e_if_k=e_{2k-i}f_k$. For the $g$ identity use induction on $k$. At $k=2$, $g_1g_2e_1e_3=g_3g_2e_1e_3$ by step 7.2. For $2\\le i<k$, commute $g_i$ through the recursion to obtain $(g_1\\cdots g_{2k-2})g_{i-1}f_{k-1}e_{2k-1}(g_{2k-2}\\cdots g_1)$, replace $g_{i-1}$ by $g_{2k-i-1}$ using induction and commute back, obtaining $g_{2k-i}f_k$. For $i=1,k>2$, substitute the recursion once more for $f_{k-1}$; the braid relations rewrite the two initial strings as $(g_2\\cdots g_{2k-3})(g_1\\cdots g_{2k-2})$. The adjacent middle block $g_{2k-3}g_{2k-2}e_{2k-3}e_{2k-1}$ can be replaced by $g_{2k-1}g_{2k-2}e_{2k-3}e_{2k-1}$ using step 7.2. Move $g_{2k-1}$ left and the two $e$'s right, recovering $g_{2k-1}f_k$. Multiplying the resulting identity by the commuting inverses gives its inverse version. For the $e$ identity and $2\\le i<k$ the same recursion applies with the cup-slide identity. The remaining $i=1$ case is an explicit column cancellation: at $k=2$ it is $e_1e_2e_1e_3e_2=e_1e_3e_2=e_3f_2$. At $k\\ge3$, commute $e_1$ past $C_k\\cdots C_3$; then $e_1C_2C_1=(e_4e_6\\cdots e_{2k-2})C_1$, by $e_1e_2e_1=e_1$. Moreover $C_3(e_4e_6\\cdots e_{2k-2})C_1=C_1$: commute matching odd generators together and cancel successively $e_3e_4e_3$, then $e_5e_6e_5$, through the final pair. Thus $e_1f_k=C_k\\cdots C_4C_1C_2\\cdots C_k$, an expression fixed by index reflection. Reflecting gives $e_{2k-1}f_k=e_1f_k$. Word reversal and shifting give all right and shifted versions.",
