          "claim": "Suppose $\\phi$ is injective at ranks below $n$. If $n=2k$, choose preimages $u_c$ of the tangle basis at rank $k$. The elements $u_cf_ku_d$ span $I_n^{(0)}$ by step 11.1, and their images have distinct connectors: bending a $k$-connector along the nested rainbow gives a bijection with pairings of the $2k$ upper vertices, independently for the lower vertices. Thus their images are independent by step 4.1 and $\\phi$ is injective on $I_n^{(0)}$. For $n=2k+1$, use a basis at rank $k+1$ on either side of $S(f_k)$. Bending its $k$ paired positions leaves one free endpoint, so a $(k+1)$-connector corresponds bijectively to a pairing of $2k+1$ boundary vertices together with one free vertex: append that free vertex as the extra $(2k+2)$nd vertex to give the inverse bijection. Consequently the resulting upper and lower partial pairings give all distinct rank-one connectors. Again step 4.1 proves injectivity on $I_n^{(1)}$. For $n=0,1$, both algebras are the scalar ring, giving the induction bases.",
