          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R$, $\\delta$, $n$, $t$ and $W_{n,t}(R,\\delta)$ be as in [[def-temperley-lieb-link-state-standard-modules]]. For link states $x,y$, let $x^*$ denote reflection across a horizontal line. The diagram $y\\circ x^*:t\\to t$ is either $\\delta^c1_t$ for some $c\\ge0$ or has fewer than $t$ propagating components. Define $\\phi_{n,t}(x,y)=\\delta^c$ in the first case and $0$ in the second, and extend $R$-bilinearly. Then $\\phi_{n,t}$ is symmetric and satisfies\n\n$$\\phi_{n,t}(x\\cdot a,y)=\\phi_{n,t}(x,y\\cdot a^*)$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in W_{n,t}(R,\\delta)$ and $a\\in\\mathrm{TL}_n(R,\\delta)$. Here $a^*$ is reflection across a horizontal line. The matrix of $\\phi_{n,t}$ in the link-state basis is the Gram matrix obtained by reflecting and capping two link states, with one factor $\\delta$ for each closed loop.",
