          "claim": "For clarity give a basis proof of the induction sequence. In left-module notation put $h_r=d_rd_{r+1}\\cdots d_n$ for $1\\le r\\le n$, and $h_{n+1}=1$. A state $d$ is $r$-admissible when it has no adjacent arc at an index $i\\ge r$. The tensors $h_r\\otimes d$ with $d$ $r$-admissible, including all $1\\otimes d$, span $A_{n+1}\\otimes_{A_n}V_{n,p}$. First, a diagram word is a sum of $h_ru$ with $u\\in A_n$: reverse a spanning Jones normal form from [F1]. Its initial ascending block is either $h_r$ or contains no $d_n$, and every later block lies in $A_n$. The surjection from the presented algebra to the diagram algebra is supplied by the local relations, so this spanning assertion applies to diagram words. Next, if $d$ has its largest adjacent arc at $s\\ge r$, write $d=d_sf$. For $n\\ge3$ this can be done without division: move the endpoint immediately outside that adjacent arc through the arc, so applying $d_s$ restores $d$ and creates no loop. If $s=1$ use site $3$; otherwise use site $s-1$. The outside site is either a defect or an endpoint of another arc, and reconnecting its former partner supplies the inverse state $f$. Now the relations give $h_r\\otimes d=h_{s+2}\\otimes d_r\\cdots d_{s-1}d$. The latter state is $(s+2)$-admissible: the adjacent arc at $s$ has moved to $r$, all intervening endpoints shift by two, and the tail beyond $s+1$ still has no adjacent arc. Repetition, increasing $r$, proves spanning. At $n=1,2$ the same conclusion is checked on the at most two states, except $n=2,p=1,\\delta=0$, when the needed factorization $d=d_1f$ fails.",
