          "claim": "Compute $F_n$ on the state $z_p$ with adjacent arcs $(1,2),(3,4),\\ldots,(2p-1,2p)$ and all remaining sites defects. At a cup, expansion of the two adjacent crossings uses the identities $d_ig_{i+1}g_i=-d_id_{i+1}$ and $g_ig_{i+1}d_i=-d_{i+1}d_i$; for example the first is $qd_i-d_id_{i+1}-\\delta d_i+q^{-1}d_id_{i+1}d_i=-d_id_{i+1}$. The two minus signs on the two sides of each cup cancel. Thus each initial cup can be removed without changing the coefficient of $z_p$. For the $t=n-2p$ defect strands, expand the auxiliary strand successively from its first crossing. A smoothing connecting two defects vanishes in the standard quotient. There are two remaining continuations: following the auxiliary strand on its two sides gives respectively $q^t\\delta-q^{t-1}=q^{t+1}$ and $q^{-t}\\delta-q^{-t+1}=q^{-(t+1)}$. The factors arise by choosing the identity smoothing at each subsequent crossing until the final cap; that cap either closes a loop or chooses the second smoothing, giving the displayed two terms on each side. Hence the coefficient of $z_p$ is $f_{n,p}$. For generic $q$, [F3] makes the form nondegenerate. Capping $z$ against $y$ and then attaching $x$ gives the rank-one diagram operator $z\\mapsto\\phi(y,z)x$, because lower-propagating terms vanish. Thus every nonzero vector generates the standard, which is simple. Centrality now makes $F_n$ scalar on the entire standard: over $\\mathbb C$ a central operator has an eigenvalue, whose nonzero eigenspace is the simple module. Since the action matrices and $f_{n,p}$ are Laurent polynomial in $q$, this scalar identity extends to every $q\\ne0$.",
