          "claim": "Append an adjacent arc at sites $n+1,n+2$ to $d$, obtaining $\\bar d\\in V_{n+2,p+1}$. The map $\\Phi(u\\otimes d)=u\\bar d$ into $V_{n+2,p+1}\\downarrow$ is balanced and $A_{n+1}$-linear: elements of $A_n$ act only on the first $n$ sites. Its image of $h_r\\otimes d$ has its rightmost adjacent arc at $r$; deleting that arc recovers $d$. Conversely every $(n+2,p+1)$-state has a rightmost adjacent arc; delete it at position $r$, and the remaining state is $r$-admissible. These two operations are inverse, so the spanning tensors in step 1.2 map bijectively to the link-state basis. They are therefore a basis and $\\Phi$ is an isomorphism, except in the stated exceptional case. Applying the restriction sequence [F2] to $V_{n+2,p+1}$ now gives the asserted induction sequence. Applying step 4.1 at rank $n+2$ splits it whenever $(n,p)$ is noncritical, since $n+2-2(p+1)+1=n-2p+1$.",
