          "claim": "Suppose $(n,p)$ is critical, $q\\ne\\pm1$, and $z_p$ is as in step 3.1. In the induced basis the coefficient of $h_{2p+1}\\otimes z_p$ in $F_{n+1}(1\\otimes z_p)$ is $(-1)^n(\\delta^2-4)\\ne0.$ Here is the local expansion computing it. First take $p=0$. To leave $h_1=d_1\\cdots d_n$ after reduction, the first pair of crossings of the closed auxiliary strand has three contributing smoothings, with weights $-1,-1,\\delta q^{-1}$; the fourth, of weight $q$, closes off the needed through path. This gives $-2+\\delta q^{-1}$. At each of the next $n-1$ pairs exactly one continuation retains the required external matching, of weight $-1$: the other continuation either caps the auxiliary path prematurely or makes an additional bottom arc. At the final pair the corresponding three weights are $-1,-1,\\delta q$, giving $-2+\\delta q$. Their product is $(-1)^{n-1}(-2+\\delta q^{-1})(-2+\\delta q)=(-1)^n(\\delta^2-4)$. The required exterior matching of $h_1$ has top pair $(1,2)$, bottom pair $(n,n+1)$, and through paths from top $j+2$ to bottom $j$ for $1\\le j<n$. Follow the auxiliary strand subject to these connections: a premature cap at an intermediate row would create another exterior arc instead of the required through path, so its continuation is excluded. For $p>0$, remove the first $p$ cups using the two local identities in step 3.1, on both sides of the auxiliary path. This leaves the same computation on $n-2p$ sites; $n-2p>0$ by criticality and $\\kappa\\ge2$, and its parity equals that of $n$. The target basis tensor is $h_{2p+1}\\otimes z_p=h_1\\otimes z_p$, by successive $d_id_{i+1}d_i=d_i$ reductions. One may do the cup removal first for $\\delta\\ne0$ by inserting $\\delta^{-1}d_{2j-1}$ at each initial cup. The resulting coefficient identity extends to $\\delta=0$: the induced basis reduction in step 1.2 uses no division, so its matrices, and this coefficient, are Laurent polynomials in $q$. Finally $\\delta^2-4=(q-q^{-1})^2\\ne0$.",
