          "quote": "Let $q\\in\\mathbb C^\\times$, $\\delta=q+q^{-1}$, $\\kappa=\\operatorname{ord}(q^2)$, and abbreviate $V_{n,p}=W_{n,n-2p}(\\mathbb C,\\delta)$, $R_{n,p}=\\operatorname{rad}\\phi_{n,n-2p}$ and $L_{n,p}=V_{n,p}/R_{n,p}$. At every label except $\\delta=0,n=2p>0$, $L_{n,p}$ is the simple head, and all proper submodules of $V_{n,p}$ are $0$ or $R_{n,p}$. The nonzero $L_{n,p}$ give all pairwise nonisomorphic simple modules. For $\\kappa=1$ every standard is simple; for $\\kappa=\\infty$ every standard is simple. At finite $\\kappa\\ge2$, critical labels give $R_{n,p}=0$. At a noncritical label put $n-2p+1=k\\kappa+r$, $1\\le r<\\kappa$, and $a=p+r-\\kappa$. Then\n$$R_{n,p}\\cong\\begin{cases}L_{n,a},&a\\ge0,\\\\0,&a<0.\\end{cases}$$\nThus, except for the zero-loop perfect-matching exception, a present reflected label gives the nonsplit exact sequence\n$$0\\longrightarrow L_{n,a}\\longrightarrow V_{n,p}\\longrightarrow L_{n,p}\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand these are the two composition factors, each once. An absent reflected label gives a simple standard. Each critical label is a singleton linkage class; the other linkage classes are the chains of allowed labels obtained by successive adjacent reflection. Here linkage means the equivalence generated by sharing a simple composition factor among standard modules.\n\nIf $\\delta=0$ and $n=2p>0$, the ordinary form on $V_{2p,p}$ is zero, so $R_{2p,p}=V_{2p,p}$ and $L_{2p,p}=0$. The actual module is simple and isomorphic to $L_{2p,p-1}$. The simple labels in this case are $0\\le j<p$. In particular this branch does not identify the actual simple head with the zero form quotient.",
