      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The generic string lattice is stable under every divided power by [F2,F3], and $T_{c,t}v_j=\\begin{bmatrix}a-2j+c\\\\t\\end{bmatrix}_qv_j$ by the toral product. Hence it specializes to the stated full-weight module. More generally, if a full-weight module is supported on $[-a,a]$ and $w$ has weight $a$, the assignment $v_j\\mapsto F^{(j)}w$ defines its unique map from $\\Delta(a)$. Indeed the support kills every $E^{(r)}w$ for $r>0$ and every $F^{(j)}w$ for $j>a$. Divided-power straightening gives $E^{(r)}F^{(j)}w=\\begin{bmatrix}a-j+r\\\\r\\end{bmatrix}_qF^{(j-r)}w$, while $F^{(r)}F^{(j)}=\\begin{bmatrix}r+j\\\\r\\end{bmatrix}_qF^{(r+j)}$. These are Laurent identities, obtained by commuting $E$ past $F$ and applying Pascal before specialization. They verify all divided actions; the full toral characters verify the remaining generators.",
