          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Work integrally over $A=\\mathbb Z[v,v^{-1}]$ in the quantum plane $P=A\\langle x,y\\rangle/(yx-vxy)$. Let $x,y$ have weights $1,-1$, $Ex=0$, $Ey=x$, $Fx=y$, $Fy=0$. The coproduct of [F1] preserves the relation: $E(yx-vxy)=vx^2-vx^2=0$ and $F(yx-vxy)=vy^2-vy^2=0$. On $P_n$, with basis $X_j=x^{n-j}y^j$, direct induction gives $E^{(r)}X_j=\\begin{bmatrix}j\\\\r\\end{bmatrix}_vX_{j-r}$ and $F^{(r)}X_j=\\begin{bmatrix}n-j\\\\r\\end{bmatrix}_vX_{j+r}$. These matrices and the full toral characters are Laurent integral. This is the dual Weyl lattice: if $w_j=(v_{n-j})^*$ in $\\Delta(n)^\\vee$, then $Ew_j=-v^{n-2j+2}[j]w_{j-1}$ and $Fw_j=-v^{-n+2j}[n-j]w_{j+1}$. Rescale by $c_0=1$, $c_j/c_{j-1}=-v^{-n+2j-2}$; the resulting coefficients are $[j]$ and $[n-j]$. The same calculation for divided powers proves the asserted integral identification.",
