        {
          "id": "derivation-4.1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "For $a,b\\ge1$, multiplication and insertion of the invariant tensor $x\\otimes y-v^{-1}y\\otimes x$ give a sequence $0\\longrightarrow P_{a-1}\\otimes P_{b-1}\\xrightarrow{i}P_a\\otimes P_b\\xrightarrow{\\mu}P_{a+b}\\longrightarrow0,$ where $i(f\\otimes g)=fx\\otimes yg-v^{-1}fy\\otimes xg$. Both maps are module maps by the module-algebra identities of step 3.1; invariance for higher divided powers follows from their integral matrices, or directly from their weight shifts on the two-factor tensor. Also $\\mu i=0$ by $yx=vxy$. Here is exactness over $A$, before any root specialization. For $f_i=x^{a-1-i}y^i$ and $g_j=x^{b-1-j}y^j$, $i(f_i\\otimes g_j)=v^{i+b-1-j}X_i\\otimes Y_{j+1}-v^{-1}X_{i+1}\\otimes Y_j.$ For each fixed $i+j$ these are adjacent differences along the consecutive path of target basis pairs, with unit coefficients. Successively eliminating from its first vertex proves independence and a free rank-one quotient. Multiplication sends each target vertex to a unit multiple of the same monomial, so it identifies this quotient with that monomial line. This proves exactness and freeness in every degree. It therefore remains exact at every specialization. When $a=0$ or $b=0$, $P_0=A$ is the tensor unit and the assertion is immediate. Iterating gives the dual-Weyl factors $a+b,a+b-2,\\ldots,|a-b|$; dualizing reverses tensor order and gives the Weyl factors. Tensoring finite filtrations is exact over $\\mathbb C$, proving tensor closure of both filtration classes and hence of tiltings.",
