        {
          "id": "derivation-6.1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "Let $M$ be Weyl-filtered with maximal label $a$. Its support is $[-a,a]$. An occurrence of a quotient $\\Delta(a)$ can be moved to the bottom of the filtration: lift its weight-$a$ generator across the preceding submodule and apply step 1.1, splitting that extension. Repeating for all occurrences gives the injective evaluation map $\\Delta(a)\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}M_a\\to M$, whose image is the sum of all maps from $\\Delta(a)$ and whose quotient is Weyl-filtered with labels below $a$. It is injective because after these splittings it is precisely the direct sum of the top standard factors, each with a one-dimensional highest line. Any idempotent $e$ on $M$ preserves this image and acts there as $1\\otimes e_a$. Its image and kernel therefore have top submodules that are sums of $\\Delta(a)$'s, and their quotients are summands of the lower-label filtered quotient. Induction on the maximal label shows both are Weyl-filtered. Duality gives the corresponding assertion for dual-Weyl filtrations. Thus every summand of a tilting is tilting.",
