          "id": "derivation-7.1",
          "step": "7.1",
          "claim": "The module $V=\\Delta(1)$ is its own dual Weyl, as the rescaling of step 3.1 shows at $n=1$. Its powers are tilting by step 4.1. Recursively splitting a finite-dimensional module into nontrivial direct sums terminates by dimension, giving an indecomposable decomposition. Summand closure makes these tiltings. Exactly one summand of $V^{\\otimes a}$ has weight $a$, whose whole space is one-dimensional; denote it by $T(a)$. For a finite-dimensional indecomposable module, an endomorphism $h$ is either invertible or nilpotent: for large $N$, stabilized kernel and image give $M=\\ker h^N\\oplus\\operatorname{im}h^N$. Consequently an endomorphism nonzero on a preserved one-dimensional highest line is invertible.",
