          "source": "lem-temperley-lieb-rank-one-weyl-and-dual-weyl-filtrations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the full integral-weight divided-power category of [[def-temperley-lieb-rank-one-lusztig-tilting-category]], with $q=e^{\\pi i/\\ell}$ and every $\\ell\\ge3$. The displayed strings define $\\Delta(a)$, and $\\nabla(a)=\\Delta(a)^\\vee$. For all $a,b\\ge0$,\n\n$$\\dim\\operatorname{Hom}(\\Delta(a),\\nabla(b))=\\delta_{ab},\\qquad \\operatorname{Ext}^1(\\Delta(a),\\nabla(b))=0.$$\n\nHere Ext vanishing means that every short exact sequence in the finite-dimensional full-weight category with those end terms splits. The tensor product $\\Delta(a)\\otimes\\Delta(b)$ has one Weyl factor of each label $a+b,a+b-2,\\ldots,|a-b|$, and the analogous dual-Weyl assertion holds. Both filtration classes are closed under direct summands. Thus tiltings are closed under tensor products, duals and direct summands. For every $a\\ge0$ there is exactly one indecomposable tilting $T(a)$ of highest weight $a$; its weight-$a$ space is one-dimensional, and it occurs as a summand of $V^{\\otimes a}$, where $V=\\Delta(1)$. Every finite tilting is a finite direct sum of these $T(a)$, so the additive idempotent-complete tensor category generated by $V$ is the entire tilting category.",
