          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space and let $\\mathcal B(H)$ denote\nits bounded linear operators. Applying AC to a family indexed by the natural numbers\nsupplies Countable Choice, the hypothesis of\n[[thm-hilbert-adjoint-properties]], which provides adjoints on $\\mathcal B(H)$.\nA **concrete von Neumann algebra** on $H$ is a\nunital $*$-subalgebra $\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\mathcal B(H)$ that is closed in the\nweak operator topology (WOT). Here unital means $I_H\\in\\mathcal A$; the zero\nHilbert space is allowed, with $I_H=0$ and sole unital algebra $\\{0\\}$. This is\nthe only use of AC in this definition.\n\nFor any set $\\mathcal S\\subseteq\\mathcal B(H)$, its **commutant** and\n**double commutant** are\n$$\\mathcal S':=\\{T\\in\\mathcal B(H):TS=ST\\text{ for every }S\\in\\mathcal S\\},\\qquad \\mathcal S'':=(\\mathcal S')'.$$\nCommutants are taken inside $\\mathcal B(H)$. The **von Neumann algebra\ngenerated by $\\mathcal S$** is\n$$W^*(\\mathcal S):=\\overline{\\operatorname{Alg}^*(\\mathcal S\\cup\\{I_H\\})}^{\\,\\mathrm{WOT}},$$\nthe WOT closure in $\\mathcal B(H)$ of the unital $*$-algebra generated by\n$\\mathcal S$. No bicommutant theorem is part of these definitions.\n\nTwo elementary properties will be used. For fixed $S\\in\\mathcal B(H)$,\n$$\\langle TS\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\langle T(S\\xi),\\eta\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle ST\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\langle T\\xi,S^*\\eta\\rangle$$\nare WOT-continuous matrix coefficients as functions of $T$. Thus each equation\n$TS=ST$ defines a WOT-closed set, and so $\\mathcal S'$ is WOT-closed. If\n$\\mathcal S$ is self-adjoint, then $\\mathcal S'$ is a unital $*$-subalgebra:\nproducts preserve commutation with every $S$, and taking adjoints of $TS=ST$\ngives $ST^*=T^*S$. These facts do not identify $W^*(\\mathcal S)$ with\n$\\mathcal S''$.",
