          "source": "def-quiver-hecke-klr-algebra-for-a-symmetrizable-cartan-datum",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(I,A,D,P,P^{\\vee},q)$ be a symmetrizable Cartan datum with no imaginary simple roots, so $a_{ii}=2$, and put $b_{ij}:=d_i\\,a_{ij}=(\\alpha_i\\mid\\alpha_j)$, a symmetric matrix with $b_{ii}=2d_i>0$ ([[def-symmetrizable-cartan-datum-for-a-quantum-group]], [[def-kac-moody-root-lattice-height-and-positive-cone]]). Fix a field $k$. A **KLR datum** on it is a family $Q=(Q_{ij})_{i\\ne j}$ of polynomials $Q_{ij}\\in k[u,v]$ such that\n\n(Q1) $Q_{ij}\\ne0$ for $i\\ne j$ and $Q_{ii}:=0$;\n\n(Q2) $Q_{ij}(u,v)=Q_{ji}(v,u)$ for all $i\\ne j$;\n\n(Q3) $Q_{ij}$ is homogeneous of total degree $-2b_{ij}$ for the degree assignments $\\deg u:=2d_i$, $\\deg v:=2d_j$;\n\n(Q4) the two extremal coefficients of $Q_{ij}$, namely that of $u^{-a_{ij}}$ and that of $v^{-a_{ji}}$, are nonzero.\n\nFor a chosen finite quiver with a compatible automorphism having no arrow within a vertex orbit, write $i\\cdot j=b_{ij}$ and let $d_{ij}$ be the number of arrow orbits directed from vertex orbit $i$ to vertex orbit $j$; the specialization applies when its induced Cartan datum is the fixed one. Then one takes $Q_{ij}(u,v)=(-1)^{d_{ij}}(u^{l/(i\\cdot i)}-v^{l/(j\\cdot j)})^{-2(i\\cdot j)/l}$ with $l=\\mathrm{lcm}(i\\cdot i,j\\cdot j)$, and $Q_{ij}=1$ when $a_{ij}=0$; the cyclic affine $A_1^{(1)}$ double edge $a_{ij}=a_{ji}=-2$ is the instance $Q_{ij}(u,v)=-(u-v)^2$ up to the fixed normalization of the top coefficients. The simply-laced standard choice is $Q_{ij}(u,v)=u+v$ for $a_{ij}=-1$.\n\n**The algebra.** For $n\\ge0$ let $I^n$ be the set of sequences, let $e(\\nu)$ for $\\nu\\in I^n$ be idempotents, and let $x_a$ ($1\\le a\\le n$) and $\\tau_a$ ($1\\le a\\le n-1$) be generators. The **KLR algebra** $R_n(Q)$ is the unital associative $k$-algebra generated by the $e(\\nu),x_a,\\tau_a$ subject to the relations, for all $\\nu\\in I^n$ (with $s_a$ the adjacent transposition acting on positions $a,a+1$):\n\n(1) $e(\\nu)e(\\nu')=\\delta_{\\nu\\nu'}e(\\nu)$ and $\\sum_{\\nu\\in I^n}e(\\nu)=1$;\n\n(2) $x_ae(\\nu)=e(\\nu)x_a$ and $x_ax_b=x_bx_a$;\n\n(3) $\\tau_ae(\\nu)=e(s_a\\nu)\\tau_a$;\n\n(4) $\\tau_a^2e(\\nu)=Q_{\\nu_a\\nu_{a+1}}(x_a,x_{a+1})e(\\nu)$;\n\n(5) $\\tau_a\\tau_b=\\tau_b\\tau_a$ whenever $|a-b|>1$;\n\n(6) $(\\tau_ax_b-x_{s_a(b)}\\tau_a)e(\\nu)=0$ if $b\\notin\\{a,a+1\\}$ or $\\nu_a\\ne\\nu_{a+1}$, while if $\\nu_a=\\nu_{a+1}$ one has $(\\tau_ax_a-x_{a+1}\\tau_a)e(\\nu)=e(\\nu)$ and $(\\tau_ax_{a+1}-x_a\\tau_a)e(\\nu)=-e(\\nu)$ (the sign normalization of the divided difference $\\tau_a(f)=(f-s_af)/(x_a-x_{a+1})$; Rouquier's printed relation has the opposite global sign, the two conventions being exchanged by $\\tau_a\\mapsto-\\tau_a$);\n\n(7) $(\\tau_{a+1}\\tau_a\\tau_{a+1}-\\tau_a\\tau_{a+1}\\tau_a)e(\\nu)=0$ if $\\nu_a\\ne\\nu_{a+2}$, and $(\\tau_{a+1}\\tau_a\\tau_{a+1}-\\tau_a\\tau_{a+1}\\tau_a)e(\\nu)=\\dfrac{Q_{\\nu_a\\nu_{a+1}}(x_a,x_{a+1})-Q_{\\nu_a\\nu_{a+1}}(x_{a+2},x_{a+1})}{x_{a+2}-x_a}e(\\nu)$ if $\\nu_a=\\nu_{a+2}$, the numerator carrying the sign of the $\\tau$-normalization of (6) (Rouquier's printed numerator is its negative); the quotient is a polynomial in $x_a,x_{a+1},x_{a+2}$ because $Q_{ij}$ is a polynomial in its first variable and the numerator vanishes at $x_{a+2}=x_a$ ([[def-polynomial-ring-over-a-commutative-ring]], [[def-multivariate-polynomial-ring-by-iteration]]).\n\nFor $\\beta\\in Q_+$ put $I^{\\beta}:=\\{\\nu\\in I^{|\\beta|}:\\sum_a\\alpha_{\\nu_a}=\\beta\\}$, $e(\\beta):=\\sum_{\\nu\\in I^{\\beta}}e(\\nu)$ and $R(\\beta):=e(\\beta)R_{|\\beta|}(Q)e(\\beta)=\\bigoplus_{\\nu,\\eta\\in I^{\\beta}}e(\\nu)R_{|\\beta|}(Q)e(\\eta)$, and put $R:=\\bigoplus_{\\beta\\in Q_+}R(\\beta)$. The presentation is the sequence form of Rouquier's $H_n(Q)$ via $1_\\nu=e(\\nu)$, $x_{a,\\nu}=x_ae(\\nu)$, $\\tau_{a,\\nu}=\\tau_ae(\\nu)$; no sign or orientation beyond $Q$ is part of the datum, and $R(0)=k$.",
