          "claim": "Let $V\\in\\mathcal O_{\\chi_\\kappa}^{\\mathrm{exact}}$. By [F1] it has a finite composition series with factors $L(\\lambda)$. Since the central ideal $\\ker\\chi_\\kappa$ annihilates $V$, it annihilates every subquotient; thus each factor has central character $\\chi_\\kappa$. By [F2], this means $\\lambda+\\rho$ and $\\kappa$ have the same values on all Weyl-invariant polynomials. These invariants separate the finite $W$-orbits: for two disjoint finite orbits, polynomial interpolation separates their point sets, and averaging the separating polynomial over $W$ makes it invariant. Therefore $\\lambda+\\rho=w\\kappa$ for some $w\\in W$. Since $\\kappa$ is integral, $\\lambda$ is integral, and every weight of $L(\\lambda)$ differs from $\\lambda$ by an element of the root lattice. All weights of $V$ are therefore integral.",
