          "id": "d1_1",
          "claim": "Write $\\kappa=a+ib$ in the real root space. Choose a real number $t$ outside the following finite exceptional set: for each root, exclude a value making $\\langle a+tb,\\beta^\\vee\\rangle=0$ unless both pairings already vanish; for each Weyl element, exclude a value making $w(a+tb)=a+tb$ unless $wa=a$ and $wb=b$. Each nonidentical linear equation excludes at most one value. Then the real vector $c=a+tb$ has stabilizer $W_c=W_a\\cap W_b=W_\\kappa$, and its perpendicular roots are exactly $\\Phi_\\kappa$. The real closed-chamber stabilizer theorem [F1] therefore identifies $W_\\kappa$ with the group generated by these root reflections. The assumed simple system gives $W_\\kappa=W_J$. Reduced-word positivity in [F1] gives $m_j=\\langle\\mu_{j-1},\\alpha_j^\\vee\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}$: the inverse prefix sends $\\alpha_j$ to a positive root. For completeness, $s_i$ permutes the positive roots other than $\\alpha_i$, so $s_i\\rho=\\rho-\\alpha_i$ and $\\langle\\rho,\\alpha_i^\\vee\\rangle=1$. Each positive coroot is a nonzero nonnegative integral sum of simple coroots (apply the positive-root statement to the dual root system), giving the asserted positive integral pairing. The final $\\mu_N=w_J\\rho$ pairs strictly negatively with every positive coroot of $\\Phi_\\kappa$.",
