          "source": "thm-quantum-highest-weight-modules-deform-the-classical-weight-character",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every dominant label weight $\\lambda$, let $V_A=U_Av_\\lambda=U_A^-v_\\lambda$ be the integral highest lattice inside the simple integrable module $L(\\lambda)$, over $A=\\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$. Then each label-weight component of $V_A$ is finite free of rank $\\dim_{\\mathbb Q(q)}L(\\lambda)_\\mu$, not merely torsionfree, and $V_A$ spans the field module. For every characteristic-zero field $k$ specialized at $q=1$, the fibre has the same weight dimensions and is the classical simple integrable highest weight module of the explicitly regularized realization, transported back to the original label lattice; hence its character is the Weyl–Kac character, the finite Weyl character in finite type. The integral quotient $U_A^-/\\sum_i\\sum_{s>\\lambda(h_i)}U_A^-F_i^{(s)}$ is $V_A(\\lambda)$; its field extension is $L(\\lambda)$ in every symmetrizable type.",
