        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Define a unital algebra-map candidate $\\Delta$ and a counit candidate $\\varepsilon$ by the displayed formulas, with $\\Delta(1)=1\\otimes1$ and $\\varepsilon(1)=1$; define an anti-algebra-map candidate $S$ by the displayed formulas, with $S(1)=1$. For toral conjugation, direct multiplication gives $\\Delta(K)\\Delta(E)\\Delta(K^{-1})=\\zeta^2\\Delta(E)$ and $\\Delta(K)\\Delta(F)\\Delta(K^{-1})=\\zeta^{-2}\\Delta(F)$. For the mixed relation, expand the coproduct commutator: the two cross terms cancel since $EK=\\zeta^{-2}KE$ and $K^{-1}F=\\zeta^2FK^{-1}$, leaving $[E,F]\\otimes K^{-1}+K\\otimes[E,F]=H\\otimes K^{-1}+K\\otimes H=\\Delta(H)$. The counit assignments preserve each relation. For the antipode assignment, applying the anti-map to the two toral relations gives $-KE+\\zeta^2EK=0$ and $-FK^{-1}+\\zeta^{-2}K^{-1}F=0$. Applying it to $EF-FE-H$ gives $K^{-1}FEK-EF+H=FE-EF+H=0$. Thus $\\Delta,\\varepsilon,S$ descend to $D_\\zeta$.",
