        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "claim": "For $n\\ge1$, telescoping gives $E^nF-FE^n=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}E^j[E,F]E^{n-1-j}$; moving $K^{\\pm1}$ through $E^j$ by $E^jK=\\zeta^{-2j}KE^j$ and $E^jK^{-1}=\\zeta^{2j}K^{-1}E^j$ gives $E^nF-FE^n=\\frac{\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\zeta^{-2j}K-\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\zeta^{2j}K^{-1}}{\\zeta-\\zeta^{-1}}E^{n-1}$. At $n=\\ell$ both geometric sums vanish because for $t=\\zeta^{\\pm2}$ one has $t\\ne1$, $t^\\ell=1$, and $\\sum_{j=0}^{\\ell-1}t^j=(1-t^\\ell)/(1-t)=0$ by [F2]; hence $E^\\ell$ commutes with $F$ and it commutes with $E,K$ as well. Similarly, $F^nE-EF^n=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}F^j[F,E]F^{n-1-j}$; using $F^jK=\\zeta^{2j}KF^j$ and $F^jK^{-1}=\\zeta^{-2j}K^{-1}F^j$ gives $F^nE-EF^n=-\\frac{\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\zeta^{2j}K-\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\\zeta^{-2j}K^{-1}}{\\zeta-\\zeta^{-1}}F^{n-1}$, whose sums also vanish at $n=\\ell$, so $F^\\ell$ commutes with $E$ and already commutes with $F,K$. Finally $K^\\ell$ commutes with $E,F$ since $\\zeta^{2\\ell}=1$. Thus $E^\\ell,F^\\ell,K^\\ell-1$ are central and $J_\\ell$ is a two-sided ideal.",
