        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "On $K$ the two iterated coproducts are $K\\otimes K\\otimes K$. On $E$ they both equal $E\\otimes K^{-1}\\otimes K^{-1}+1\\otimes E\\otimes K^{-1}+1\\otimes1\\otimes E$, and on $F$ they both equal $F\\otimes1\\otimes1+K\\otimes F\\otimes1+K\\otimes K\\otimes F$; hence coassociativity holds on generators. The two counit composites give $K,E,F$ on each generator: for $E$ they are $0+E$ and $E+0$, and for $F$ they are $0+F$ and $F+0$, while $K$ is sent to $1\\cdot K$ and $K\\cdot1$. Both counit composites and both iterated coproducts are algebra maps, so these identities hold on all of $D_\\zeta$. For the antipode equations, on $K$ they are $K^{-1}K=1=KK^{-1}$; on $E$ they are $S(E)K^{-1}+E=0$ and $EK+S(E)=0$; on $F$ they are $S(F)+K^{-1}F=0$ and $F+KS(F)=0$. Since $S(1)=1$, both equations also hold on the unit. If both equations hold for $x$ and $y$, then anti-multiplicativity of $S$ and multiplicativity of $\\Delta$ give $\\sum S(x_{(1)}y_{(1)})x_{(2)}y_{(2)}=\\varepsilon(x)\\varepsilon(y)1$ and $\\sum x_{(1)}y_{(1)}S(x_{(2)}y_{(2)})=\\varepsilon(x)\\varepsilon(y)1$. Induction on word length proves the antipode equations on all of $D_\\zeta$, so it is a Hopf algebra by [F3].",
          "step": "2.2",
