        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $AB=tBA$, induction starts at $n=0$ with $(A+B)^0=1$ and $C_{0,0}=1$ and gives $(A+B)^n=\\sum_{r=0}^n C_{n,r}B^{n-r}A^r$, where $C_{n,0}=C_{n,n}=1$ and, for $0<r<n$, $C_{n,r}=\\prod_{j=1}^r(1-t^{n-r+j})/(1-t^j)$. Multiplying the expansion on the right by $A+B$ shows the coefficient recurrence $C_{n+1,r}=C_{n,r-1}+t^rC_{n,r}$. For the product formula, putting those two terms over $\\prod_{j=1}^r(1-t^j)$ leaves the common numerator $\\prod_{k=n-r+2}^n(1-t^k)$ times $(1-t^r)+t^r(1-t^{n-r+1})=1-t^{n+1}$, exactly the product formula for $C_{n+1,r}$. Thus the formula follows inductively whenever its denominators are nonzero. For $t=\\zeta^{2}$ or $t=\\zeta^{-2}$ and $0<r<\\ell$, the denominator is nonzero and the numerator contains $1-t^\\ell=0$, so all intermediate coefficients vanish. In $\\Delta(E)$ the summands $A=E\\otimes K^{-1}$ and $B=1\\otimes E$ satisfy $AB=\\zeta^{-2}BA$; in $\\Delta(F)$ the summands $A=F\\otimes1$ and $B=K\\otimes F$ satisfy $AB=\\zeta^{2}BA$. Applying the formula to these two pairs gives $\\Delta(E^\\ell)=E^\\ell\\otimes K^{-\\ell}+1\\otimes E^\\ell$ and $\\Delta(F^\\ell)=F^\\ell\\otimes1+K^\\ell\\otimes F^\\ell$. Also $\\Delta(K^\\ell-1)=(K^\\ell-1)\\otimes K^\\ell+1\\otimes(K^\\ell-1)$. The counit kills all three generators. Since $\\ell$ is odd, $(EK)^\\ell=\\zeta^{-\\ell(\\ell-1)}E^\\ell K^\\ell$ and $(K^{-1}F)^\\ell=\\zeta^{\\ell(\\ell-1)}F^\\ell K^{-\\ell}$, with $\\zeta^{\\ell(\\ell-1)}=1$, we have $S(E^\\ell)=-E^\\ell K^\\ell$ and $S(F^\\ell)=-K^{-\\ell}F^\\ell$, both in $J_\\ell$; also $S(K^\\ell-1)=K^{-\\ell}-1=-K^{-\\ell}(K^\\ell-1)\\in J_\\ell$. The sum $J_\\ell\\otimes D_\\zeta+D_\\zeta\\otimes J_\\ell$ is a two-sided ideal of $D_\\zeta\\otimes D_\\zeta$, so the coproduct inclusions for the three generators extend to all of $J_\\ell$ because $\\Delta$ is multiplicative. The counit kills the ideal because it kills its generators, and the anti-multiplicative antipode preserves the ideal because it sends each generator into $J_\\ell$. Therefore the quotient operations are well-defined and satisfy the Hopf identities inherited from $D_\\zeta$.",
