          "quote": "Assume the inherited Axiom of Choice. For a finite simply-laced root datum with full weight lattice and $A=\\mathbb Z[q,q^{-1}]$, the generic algebra has automorphisms $S_i$ with\n$$S_i(K_h)=K_{s_i h},\\qquad S_i(E_i)=-K_iF_i,\\qquad S_i(F_i)=-E_iK_i^{-1}.$$\nThey fix $E_j,F_j$ for $a_{ij}=0$, while for $a_{ij}=-1$,\n$$S_i(E_j)=E_jE_i-qE_iE_j,\\qquad S_i(F_j)=F_iF_j-q^{-1}F_jF_i.$$\nThey satisfy $S_iS_j=S_jS_i$ for nonneighbors and $S_iS_jS_i=S_jS_iS_j$ for neighbors. They and their inverses preserve the entire divided-power form $U_A$, including its toral binomials, and the ordinary-generator form over $A[(q-q^{-1})^{-1}]$. They consequently specialize to inverse automorphisms whenever the indicated denominators are invertible. For neighbors,\n$$S_i(E_j^{(n)})=\\sum_{r+s=n}(-1)^rq^rE_i^{(r)}E_j^{(n)}E_i^{(s)},\\qquad S_i(F_j^{(n)})=\\sum_{r+s=n}(-1)^rq^{-r}F_i^{(s)}F_j^{(n)}F_i^{(r)}.$$\nNo root-of-unity PBW or small-quantum-group theorem is assumed in this assertion.",
