          "source": "lem-root-datum-combinatorics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R=(X,\\Phi,X^\\vee,\\Phi^\\vee)$ be a reduced root datum ([[def-abstract-root-datum-and-its-weyl-group]]). Then: (a) $W(R)$ is finite and generated by the reflections $s_\\alpha$, $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$; for a base $\\Delta\\subseteq\\Phi$ it is generated by the simple reflections $s_\\alpha$, $\\alpha\\in\\Delta$; (b) the correspondences between bases, positive systems and Weyl chambers are bijective, and $W(R)$ acts simply transitively on the set of Weyl chambers, equivalently on the set of bases; (c) for a chosen positive system $\\Phi^+$ there is a unique longest element $w_0\\in W(R)$ with $w_0(\\Phi^+)=-\\Phi^+$, and for every $w\\in W(R)$ the integer $n(w)=|\\Phi^+\\cap w(\\Phi^-)|$ equals the length of $w$ with respect to the simple reflections, with $n(w^{-1})=n(w)$ and $n(w_0w)=|\\Phi^+|-n(w)$; (d) $\\Phi$ spans $V=X\\otimes\\mathbb Q$ iff the root datum is semisimple, and then $\\Phi$ is a reduced root system in $V$ in the Euclidean sense.",
