          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix with presented group $W$, length $\\ell$ and geometric representation $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ as in [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], [[def-hh-geometric-coxeter-representation-and-roots]] and [[lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign]], and let $J\\subseteq S$.\n\n1. **Support and reduction.** For every $w\\in W$ the set $S(w)$ of letters occurring in a reduced expression of $w$ is independent of the reduced expression, and every word in $S$ representing $w$ can be transformed into a reduced expression by repeatedly deleting two letters (Tits reduction, using only letters already present). Consequently\n$$W_J=\\langle J\\rangle=\\{w\\in W:S(w)\\subseteq J\\}.$$\n2. **Intrinsic parabolic presentation.** Let $W_J^\\ast$ be the group presented by the restricted Coxeter matrix $(J,m|_J)$ in the sense of [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]. The canonical homomorphism $W_J^\\ast\\to W_J$, $s\\mapsto s$, is an isomorphism. Hence $(W_J,J)$ is a Coxeter system, its intrinsic length function $\\ell_J$ agrees with the ambient length $\\ell$ on $W_J$, and $W_J\\cap S=J$.\n3. **Minimal coset representatives.** Every right coset $W_J a:=\\{ua:u\\in W_J\\}$ ($a\\in W$, [[def-coset]]) has a unique element $d$ of minimal length; it is characterized by $\\ell(sd)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$, and it satisfies\n$$\\ell(ud)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)\\qquad\\text{for all }u\\in W_J .$$\nEquivalently, every $w\\in W$ has a unique factorization $w=u\\,d$ with $u\\in W_J$ and $d$ the minimal representative of the right coset $W_J d$, and then $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$. By inversion ($w\\mapsto w^{-1}$ preserves lengths and interchanges the two coset families $\\{W_Ja\\}$ and $\\{aW_J\\}$), every left coset $aW_J:=\\{au:u\\in W_J\\}$ has a unique minimal element $d$, characterized by $\\ell(ds)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$ and satisfying $\\ell(du)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(u)$ for all $u\\in W_J$.\n4. **Type A.** Let $n\\ge2$, $S=\\{s_1,\\dots,s_{n-1}\\}$ and $m(s_i,s_j):=3$ if $|i-j|=1$, $m(s_i,s_j):=2$ if $|i-j|>1$ (a Coxeter matrix of type $A_{n-1}$). Then $s_i\\mapsto(i\\ i+1)$ extends to an isomorphism $W\\to S_n$ (the letters $1,\\dots,n$ carry the library's symmetric group by the order-preserving identification with $\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\}$, under which $(i\\ i+1)$ is the adjacent transposition $(i-1\\ i)$), and for every $w\\in W$,\n$$\\ell(w)=\\operatorname{inv}\\bigl(\\varphi(w)\\bigr),$$\nthe inversion number of the corresponding permutation ([[def-inversions-inversion-number-and-sign]]). In particular a word in the $s_i$ is reduced if and only if its length equals the inversion number of its value.",
