          "claim": "Fix one root color, and first work in a single $P/\\mathbb Z\\alpha_i$ coset. Take a finite-dimensional simple graded module for the rank-one algebra, and choose a nonzero vector $v$ at its highest integer coroot label $n$. It is killed by every positive divided power. Reordering shows that the cyclic module is spanned by the distinct weight vectors $F^{(r)}v$, $r\\ge0$, and simplicity makes this the whole module. If $n=-b<0$, put $r=2\\ell k$. The negative-binomial identity gives $\\begin{bmatrix}-b\\\\r\\end{bmatrix}_\\zeta=(-1)^r\\begin{bmatrix}b+r-1\\\\r\\end{bmatrix}_\\zeta$. To see this is nonzero for arbitrarily large $k$, write $b-1=a\\ell+s$, $0\\le s<\\ell$, and use $\\prod_{j=0}^{N-1}(1+\\zeta^{N-1-2j}z)=\\sum_j\\begin{bmatrix}N\\\\j\\end{bmatrix}_\\zeta z^j$ with $N=b+r-1$. Every block of length $\\ell$ equals $1+\\zeta^{\\ell(N-1)}z^\\ell$, because $\\zeta^{-2}$ has odd order $\\ell$; the residual block has degree $s<\\ell$. The coefficient of $z^{2\\ell k}$ is therefore $\\binom{a+2k}{2k}$: the sign from $\\zeta^{\\ell(N-1)}=\\pm1$ is raised to the even power $2k$. It is nonzero. But finite support makes $F^{(r)}v=0$ for large $r$, contradicting $E^{(r)}F^{(r)}v=\\begin{bmatrix}n\\\\r\\end{bmatrix}_\\zeta v$. Hence $n\\ge0$.",
