          "source": "lem-lusztig-divided-power-form-is-a-hopf-integral-form",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice inherited from the integral triangular-decomposition supplier. The full form $U_A$ of [[def-lusztig-divided-power-integral-form]], including every $T_{i,c,t}$, is stable under the library coproduct, counit, antipode and inverse antipode. Consequently every scalar specialization, including $U^L_\\zeta$, is a Hopf algebra with bijective antipode. In the following formulas $a,b,r,t\\ge0$, $c\\in\\mathbb Z$, and $q=q_i$, $K=K_i$ when the color is suppressed:\n\n$$\\Delta E_i^{(r)}=\\sum_{a+b=r}q_i^{ab}E_i^{(a)}\\otimes K_i^{-a}E_i^{(b)},\\qquad \\Delta F_i^{(r)}=\\sum_{a+b=r}q_i^{-ab}F_i^{(a)}K_i^b\\otimes F_i^{(b)}.$$\n\n$$\\Delta T_{i,c,t}=\\sum_{a+b=t}q_i^{-bc}T_{i,c,a}K_i^{-b}\\otimes K_i^aT_{i,0,b}.$$\n\n$$S(E_i^{(r)})=(-1)^rq_i^{r(r-1)}E_i^{(r)}K_i^r,\\qquad S(F_i^{(r)})=(-1)^rq_i^{-r(r-1)}K_i^{-r}F_i^{(r)}.$$\n\n$$S(T_{i,c,t})=(-1)^tT_{i,t-c-1,t},\\qquad \\varepsilon(T_{i,c,t})=\\begin{bmatrix}c\\\\t\\end{bmatrix}_{q_i}.$$\n\nThe generalized Gaussian for integral $c$ denotes its Laurent-polynomial value; for $c=-b<0$ it equals $(-1)^t\\begin{bmatrix}b+t-1\\\\t\\end{bmatrix}_{q_i}$.",
