          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Put $K=\\{x\\in P:\\chi(2x,\\eta)=1\\text{ for all }\\eta\\in P\\}$. Since $t$ is primitive of order $ld$, the condition is $2d(x,\\eta)\\in ld\\mathbb Z$ for all $\\eta$, or $x\\in(l/2)P^*$, where $P^*=Q$ for this simply-laced full weight lattice. Thus $K=P\\cap(l/2)Q$. It has finite index in $P$ because it contains $lQ$. The pairing $\\chi(2x,y)$ is a well-defined nondegenerate bicharacter on the finite abelian group $G=P/K$: its radical is exactly how $K$ was defined. The characters of $G$ obtained from the second argument therefore give all characters of $G$, because the induced injection from $G$ into its character group is between finite groups of equal order. Their independence follows directly by summing each character over $G$: a nontrivial character sum is zero by translation by a point where it is nontrivial, and the trivial character sum is $|G|$.",
