          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Classical Weyl characters span the $W$-invariant group algebra $\\mathbb C[P]^W$. Indeed $\\chi_\\nu$ has leading orbit sum at dominant weight $\\nu$ with coefficient one and only strictly smaller dominant orbit labels below it; induction on the finite downward set eliminates these smaller terms and writes every orbit sum as a finite linear combination of Weyl characters. Evaluation on $G$ maps these orbit sums to the averages under $W$ of the character basis from step 2.2. Those averages span all $W$-invariant functions on $G$, since averaging commutes with Fourier expansion. In particular they distinguish different $W$-orbits of $G$ by the characteristic functions of those orbits. Thus equality $c_\\nu(\\lambda)=c_\\nu(\\mu)$ for every dominant $\\nu$ forces $\\mu+\\rho=w(\\lambda+\\rho)+k$ for some $w\\in W$ and $k\\in K$. This finite Fourier argument requires neither an invertible modular $S$-matrix nor simplicity of root Weyl modules.",
