          "quote": "Fix a field $k$. A **bialgebra over $k$** is a $k$-vector space $H$ with a unital associative $k$-algebra structure $(m,u)$ and a counital coassociative $k$-coalgebra structure $(\\Delta,\\varepsilon)$ in the sense of [[def-hh-coalgebra-and-coalgebra-map]], where $m:H\\otimes H\\to H$, $u:k\\to H$, $\\Delta:H\\to H\\otimes H$, and $\\varepsilon:H\\to k$. Explicitly, the algebra equations are $m(m\\otimes\\mathrm{id})=m(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes m)$ and $m(u\\otimes\\mathrm{id})=\\mathrm{id}=m(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes u)$ under the canonical identifications; the coalgebra equations are those of [[def-hh-coalgebra-and-coalgebra-map]].\n\nRequire $\\Delta:H\\to H\\otimes H$ and $\\varepsilon:H\\to k$ to be unital algebra homomorphisms. The algebra on $H\\otimes H$ is the one with $(a\\otimes b)(c\\otimes d)=ac\\otimes bd$ and unit $1_H\\otimes1_H$; the algebra on $k$ is its field algebra. A **bialgebra map** $f:H\\to K$ is a $k$-linear map preserving multiplication, unit, comultiplication, and counit: $f m_H=m_K(f\\otimes f)$, $fu_H=u_K$, $\\Delta_Kf=(f\\otimes f)\\Delta_H$, and $\\varepsilon_Kf=\\varepsilon_H$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Multiplication is associative and unital. The coproduct is coassociative, and $(\\varepsilon\\otimes\\mathrm{id})\\Delta(e_i)=e_i=(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes\\varepsilon)\\Delta(e_i)$. Also $\\Delta(e_i)\\Delta(e_j)=\\delta_{ij}e_i\\otimes e_i=\\Delta(e_ie_j)$, so $\\Delta$ is multiplicative.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Put $E=\\Delta(1)=\\sum_i e_i\\otimes e_i$. Both iterated coproducts of $E$ equal $\\sum_i e_i\\otimes e_i\\otimes e_i$, and $(E\\otimes1)(1\\otimes E)=(1\\otimes E)(E\\otimes1)=\\sum_i e_i\\otimes e_i\\otimes e_i$ by orthogonality. For basis triples $e_i,e_j,e_k$, each weak-counit product equals $1$ exactly when $i=j=k$ and is $0$ otherwise; trilinearity proves both weak-counit identities. The counital-map formulas give $\\varepsilon_t(e_i)=e_i=\\varepsilon_s(e_i)$. Finally, $S(e_i)=e_i$ gives $e_iS(e_i)=\\varepsilon_t(e_i)$, $S(e_i)e_i=\\varepsilon_s(e_i)$, and $S(e_i)e_iS(e_i)=S(e_i)$. Thus all weak Hopf axioms hold by linearity.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The counital formulas also give $\\varepsilon_s'(e_i)=\\sum_j e_j\\varepsilon(e_je_i)=e_i$, hence $H_t=H_s=H$. Since $E$ has no off-diagonal terms whereas $1\\otimes1$ has them, $\\Delta(1)\\ne1\\otimes1$. Moreover $\\varepsilon(e_0e_1)=0$ but $\\varepsilon(e_0)\\varepsilon(e_1)=1$, so the structure is not an ordinary bialgebra.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Orthogonal idempotents summing to $1$ give direct sums $V=e_0V\\oplus e_1V$ and $W=e_0W\\oplus e_1W$ by the left module axioms. On each elementary summand $e_iV\\otimes e_jW$, $E$ acts as multiplication by $\\delta_{ij}$, so its image is $(e_0V\\otimes e_0W)\\oplus(e_1V\\otimes e_1W)$. Also $v\\triangleleft e_i=e_iv$. The balancing relation for $x=e_i$ kills $e_iV\\otimes e_jW$ when $i\\ne j$, while for $i=j$ the left and right actions agree. For $v_i=e_iv$ and $w_j=e_jw$, the relation for $x=a_0e_0+a_1e_1$ is $(a_i-a_j)v_i\\otimes w_j$. It vanishes when $i=j$; when $i\\ne j$, choosing $x=e_i$ generates the whole summand $e_iV\\otimes e_jW$. Thus the relation subspace is exactly the sum of the two off-diagonal summands.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "The quotient and image are both the direct sum of the diagonal summands by step 4.1, and the quotient map and its inverse are the identity on each such summand. On each diagonal summand $e_jV\\otimes e_jW$, both the image and quotient actions of $e_i$ are multiplication by $\\delta_{ij}$; hence the isomorphism is $H$-linear. If $f:V\\to V'$ and $g:W\\to W'$ are $H$-linear, then [F3] gives $f(e_i v)=e_i f(v)$ and $g(e_i w)=e_i g(w)$, so $f\\otimes g$ preserves each diagonal summand and the isomorphism is natural in $V,W$.",
          "step": "5.1",
