    "lem-complete-local-hecke-modular-systems-and-idempotent-lifting": {
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Put $B=A_{\\mathfrak p}$. It is a nonzero Noetherian local domain by [F1]. Its residue is $\\operatorname{Frac}(A/\\mathfrak p)$: modulo $\\mathfrak p$, the inverted elements are exactly the nonzero elements of $A/\\mathfrak p$. This identifies that field with $k_0\\subseteq k$. Localizing a domain is injective. The elementary prime correspondence used below follows directly from fractions: a prime disjoint from the denominators extends to a prime, and contraction recovers it because a fraction belongs to the extended ideal exactly when its numerator does.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "If $\\mathfrak p\\cap\\mathbb Z=0$, every nonzero integer is a unit in $B$, so fraction arithmetic identifies $B$ with a prime localization of $\\mathbb Q[v,v^{-1}]$. The latter is a PID: ideals in its localization are generated by the image of a generator of their contracted ideal in $\\mathbb Q[v]$. Thus $B$ is a field or has a nonzero principal maximal ideal $(a)$. In the second case [F2] and the chain $(0)\\subsetneq(a)$ give dimension one. The class of $a$ modulo $(a)^2$ is nonzero, since $a=a^2b$ in the domain would imply $1=ab$ with $a$ a nonunit. Hence its embedding dimension is one. In the field case both dimensions are zero. Thus $B$ is regular in both cases.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Otherwise $\\mathfrak p\\cap\\mathbb Z=(\\ell)$ for a prime integer $\\ell$. The prime $\\mathfrak p/(\\ell)$ in the PID $\\mathbb F_\\ell[v,v^{-1}]$ is zero or $(f)$ for an irreducible Laurent polynomial $f$. If it is zero, $\\mathfrak p=(\\ell)$ and the principal-maximal-ideal argument of step 2.1 proves regularity. Otherwise choose a Laurent lift $G$ of $f$; then the maximal ideal of $B$ is $\\mathfrak m=(\\ell,G)$. The chain $(0)\\subsetneq(\\ell)B\\subsetneq\\mathfrak m$ and [F2] give $\\dim B=2$. The quotient $B/(\\ell)$ is the domain $\\mathbb F_\\ell[v,v^{-1}]_{(f)}$. If $\\ell=r\\ell^2+s\\ell G+tG^2$ in $B$, reduction modulo $\\ell$ gives $\\bar t f^2=0$, hence $t=\\ell t'$. Cancellation of $\\ell$ then gives $1=r\\ell+sG+t'G^2\\in\\mathfrak m$, impossible. Thus $\\ell\\notin\\mathfrak m^2$. If $a\\ell+bG\\in\\mathfrak m^2$, reducing modulo $\\ell$ gives $\\bar b f\\in(f^2)$, so $b\\in\\mathfrak m$. It follows that $a\\ell\\in\\mathfrak m^2$, and $\\ell\\notin\\mathfrak m^2$ forces $a\\in\\mathfrak m$, since otherwise $a$ is a unit. The two displayed generators therefore give a basis of $\\mathfrak m/\\mathfrak m^2$; its dimension is two. Thus $B$ is regular in this final case.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "By [F3], $R=\\widehat B$ is complete separated Noetherian local with residue $k_0$, is regular, and is a domain. Faithful flatness makes $B\\to R$ injective: if $b$ maps to zero, flat tensoring the inclusion $bB\\hookrightarrow B$ gives an injection $bB\\otimes_B R\\hookrightarrow R$ whose image is zero; faithfulness forces $bB=0$, so $b=0$. Therefore $A\\hookrightarrow R$ induces $\\mathbb Q(v)\\hookrightarrow F$, with the required residue map. If $\\mathfrak p=0$, this also covers the field $B=R=\\mathbb Q(v)$ with zero maximal ideal.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "For a finite projective module $P$ over any local ring, lift a basis of $P/\\mathfrak mP$ to $r$ elements of $P$. Nakayama on the finite cokernel makes $R^r\\to P$ surjective. Projectivity splits it as $R^r=P\\oplus Q$, with $Q$ finite. Reduction of this direct sum shows $Q/\\mathfrak mQ=0$, so Nakayama gives $Q=0$. Hence $P$ is free of rank $r$. Over our domain this rank equals both $\\dim_F(F\\otimes_RP)$ and $\\dim_{k_0}(k_0\\otimes_RP)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 4.1",
            "choose",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-2",
          "step": "5.2",
          "claim": "Let $x_0\\in C$ lift an idempotent modulo $\\mathfrak m_RC$. Define $\\delta_i=x_i^2-x_i$ and $x_{i+1}=x_i+(1-2x_i)\\delta_i$. All factors here are polynomials in $x_i$, so integer-polynomial expansion gives $\\delta_{i+1}=\\delta_i^2(4\\delta_i-3)$. Thus $\\delta_i\\in\\mathfrak m_R^{2^i}C$ and $x_{i+1}-x_i\\in\\mathfrak m_R^{2^i}C$. The finite-module completeness in [F4] gives a limit $q\\equiv x_0$ modulo $\\mathfrak m_RC$. Multiplication is continuous, since these ideal powers are two-sided ideals, so separatedness gives $q^2=q$. This construction involves no division and works in every characteristic.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 4.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "For any right $C$-module $X$, the $R$-linear decomposition $X=Xq\\oplus X(1-q)$ shows that projection $X\\mapsto Xq$ commutes with every scalar extension. It is exact: under a surjection $X\\to Y$, lift $y\\in Y$ to $x$, and then $xq$ lifts $yq$; kernels and injections follow by restriction of the same projection. If $0\\to I_{i+1}\\to I_i\\to B_i\\to0$ is a filtration sequence, projection gives $0\\to I_{i+1}q\\to I_iq\\to B_iq\\to0$. When $B_i$ is finite free, $B_iq$ is finite projective and hence free by step 5.1; the sequence splits as $R$-modules. It therefore retains its exact layer after scalar extension, including reduction to $k_0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 5.1",
            "step 5.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "step": "7.1",
          "claim": "Finally let $J_R=\\bigoplus_E M_{d_E}(R)$ act on an $R$-finite-free module $X$. With matrix units $e^E_{ij}$, put $Q_E=e^E_{11}X$. It is a direct summand of $X$, hence finite free by step 5.1. The map $R^{d_E}\\otimes_RQ_E\\to e_EX$ sending $u_i\\otimes x$ to $e^E_{i1}x$ has inverse $y\\mapsto\\sum_i u_i\\otimes e^E_{1i}y$, by the matrix-unit identities. Therefore the generic and residue multiplicities of the $E$-standard simple are both $\\operatorname{rank}_RQ_E$. This proves the final assertion and completes the lemma.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 5.1",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
