 for every $k\\ge1$.\n   Hence $\\sigma_s\\sigma_t=A$ has order exactly $m$ when $m<\\infty$, and infinite order when $m=\\infty$; in particular $A^m=\\mathrm{id}_E$ and $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ for $0<k<m$ in the finite case.\n4. **The representation and the exact dihedral orders.** The assignment $s\\mapsto\\sigma_s$ respects every defining relator of $W$ and induces a unique homomorphism $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ with $\\sigma(s)=\\sigma_s$. Consequently, for any distinct $s,t\\in S$, one has $s\\ne t$ in $W$ and $st$ has order exactly $m(s,t)$ in $W$ (infinite when $m(s,t)=\\infty$).\n5. **The signed reflection action.** Let $T:=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}\\subseteq W$. For $s\\in S$ define $U_s:\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T\\to\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ by\n$$U_s(\\varepsilon,r):=\\bigl(\\varepsilon\\cdot(-1)^{\\delta(s,r)},\\ srs\\bigr),$$\nwhere $\\delta(s,r)=1$ if $r=s$ and $\\delta(s,r)=0$ otherwise. Then $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$ for every $s$, and the assignment $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot s:=U_s(\\varepsilon,r)$ extends to a well-defined **right action** of $W$ on $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ (so $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot1=(\\varepsilon,r)$ and $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot(wv)=((\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w)\\cdot v$). Indeed the only relations to check are $s^2=1$ and, for distinct $s,t$ with $m=m(s,t)<\\infty$, $(st)^m=1$: $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$ because for $r=s$ the two signs cancel while for $r\\ne s$ one has $srs\\ne s$ and $U_s(\\varepsilon,srs)=(\\varepsilon,r)$; and the alternating word $s\\,t\\,s\\,t\\cdots$ of length $2m$ acts trivially because its $2m$ prefix reflections $r_i:=w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}$ (with $w_j:=s_1\\cdots s_j$) satisfy $r_{i+m}=r_i$ for $1\\le i\\le m$ and $r_1,\\dots,r_m$ are pairwise distinct, so each reflection of the dihedral subgroup $\\langle s,t\\rangle$ occurs exactly twice and every accumulated sign is even while the conjugating coordinate returns to $r$ because $(st)^m=1$.\n6. **Prefix reflections, deletion and expression independence.** Let $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ be a word in $S$ with value $w=s_1\\cdots s_k$ and prefix reflections $r_i=s_1\\cdots s_{i-1}s_is_{i-1}\\cdots s_1$, and let $n(r):=\\#\\{i:r_i=r\\}$.\n   (a) If $r_i=r_j$ for some $i<j$, then $s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_j}\\cdots s_k=w$: the two letters $s_i,s_j$ can be deleted.\n   (b) $(-1)^{n(r)}=:\\eta(r,w)$ depends only on $w$ and $r$, and the right action satisfies $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w=(\\varepsilon\\,\\eta(r,w),\\,w^{-1}rw)$.\n   (c) If the word is reduced ($k=\\ell(w)$), then $n(r)\\in\\{0,1\\}$ for every $r$, the map $i\\mapsto r_i$ is injective, and the set $\\Phi(w):=\\{r_1,\\dots,r_k\\}=\\{r\\in T:\\eta(r,w)=-1\\}$ is independent of the reduced expression chosen, with $\\#\\Phi(w)=\\ell(w)$.\n7. **Ambient reducedness of dihedral words.** Let $s\\ne t$, $m:=m(s,t)$, and for $q\\ge1$ let $w_q$ be the value of the alternating word of length $q$ beginning with $s$. If $m<\\infty$ and $q\\le m$, or if $m=\\infty$ and $q\\ge1$, then $\\ell(w_q)=q$, every word in $S$ representing $w_q$ has length at least $q$, and $w_1,\\dots,w_q$ are pairwise distinct. Thus dihedral alternating words are reduced in the ambient group $W$, not merely inside $\\langle s,t\\rangle$.",
