    "lem-unequal-parameter-hecke-standard-basis-and-bar-triangularity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-arbitrary-rank-coxeter-system-and-bruhat-order",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be any set, possibly infinite, and let $m:S\\times S\\to\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ be a Coxeter matrix: $m(s,s)=1$, and for distinct $s,t$, $m(s,t)=m(t,s)\\in\\{2,3,\\ldots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$. Let $F(S)$ be the free group on $S$, let $N$ be the normal closure of\n$$\n\\{s^2:s\\in S\\}\\cup\\{(st)^{m(s,t)}:s\\ne t,\\ m(s,t)<\\infty\\},\n$$\nand define $W=F(S)/N$. The image of $s\\in S$ in $W$ is also denoted $s$. This is the Coxeter group presented by $(S,m)$.\n\nFor $w\\in W$, define its length by\n$$\n\\ell(w)=\\min\\{k\\in\\mathbb N: w=s_1\\cdots s_k\\text{ for some }s_1,\\ldots,s_k\\in S\\}.\n$$\nThe set is nonempty because every element of the quotient has a finite word in $S\\cup S^{-1}$ and the relators give $s^{-1}=s$; its minimum exists by the well-ordering principle. A word of length $\\ell(w)$ for $w$ is a reduced expression; the empty word is the reduced expression of $1$.\n\nDefine the reflection set $T=\\{x s x^{-1}:x\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}$. Write $u\\to v$ if $v=ut$ for a reflection $t\\in T$ and $\\ell(v)>\\ell(u)$. Define the reflection-chain Bruhat order by $u\\le v$ if there is a finite chain $u=u_0\\to u_1\\to\\cdots\\to u_k=v$; the empty chain is allowed. Length strictly increases on each nonempty chain, so this relation is a partial order. The exact reduced-subword description and finite-interval consequence needed below are established in [[thm-arbitrary-rank-finite-parabolics-and-bruhat-subword]].\n\nWhen $S$ is finite, the presentation and length agree with [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], and the reflection-chain order agrees with [[def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains]]. No exchange, standard-parabolic, reduced-subword, or interval-finiteness property is included in this definition; those properties are justified separately.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-unequal-parameter-weight-function-and-coefficient-order",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be as in [[def-arbitrary-rank-coxeter-system-and-bruhat-order]]. A **totally ordered abelian group** $(\\Gamma,+,0,<)$ is an abelian group with a total order invariant under translation. Its group algebra $A=\\mathbb Z[\\Gamma]$ consists of finite sums $\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\Gamma}a_\\gamma v^\\gamma$, where $a_\\gamma\\in\\mathbb Z$, with $v^\\gamma v^\\delta=v^{\\gamma+\\delta}$. Define the ring involution $\\overline{v^\\gamma}=v^{-\\gamma}$, and set\n$$\nA_{<0}=\\bigoplus_{\\gamma<0}\\mathbb Z v^\\gamma,\\qquad A_{\\le0}=\\bigoplus_{\\gamma\\le0}\\mathbb Z v^\\gamma.\n$$\n\nA **weight function** is a map $L:W\\to\\Gamma$ such that $L(xy)=L(x)+L(y)$ whenever $\\ell(xy)=\\ell(x)+\\ell(y)$. Its simple-generator parameters are $L(s)$ for $s\\in S$. The exact criterion for prescribing those values on the simple generators is proved in [[lem-simple-generator-weights-extend-to-a-weight-function]]. No sign restriction on $L(s)$ is part of this definition.\n\nThe coefficient ordering used on the weighted Kazhdan–Lusztig basis is the given order on $\\Gamma$, through $A_{<0}$. The group algebra elements $v^{L(s)}$ are units for every $s\\in S$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
