          "claim": "Reversing words gives the anti-involution $T_w\\mapsto T_{w^{-1}}$ of the Hecke presentation. It commutes with bar. Inversion preserves length and sends a reflection-chain step $u\\to ut$ to $u^{-1}\\to tu^{-1}=u^{-1}(utu^{-1})$, again a length-increasing right-reflection step; hence it preserves Bruhat order. Thus it preserves Bruhat triangularity, and KL uniqueness gives $C_w\\mapsto C_{w^{-1}}$. Hence $h_{x,y,z}=h_{y^{-1},x^{-1},z^{-1}}$, $a(z)=a(z^{-1})$, and $\\gamma_{x,y,z}=\\gamma_{y^{-1},x^{-1},z^{-1}}$. Comparing coefficients in the bar-invariant products $C_xC_y$ also gives $\\overline{h_{x,y,z}}=h_{x,y,z}$. P3 with $y=1$ forces $a(1)=0$ and $1\\in\\mathcal D$, since $C_1C_1=C_1$ makes the required nonzero coefficient possible only for $d=1$ and exponent zero. The assignment $T_s\\mapsto-T_s^{-1}$ preserves the quadratic relation and braid relations (the signs agree at equal braid length and inverse reversed braid words agree), is involutive and is $A$-linear. Thus $D_xD_y$ has the same constants $h$.",
