          "quote": "Let $(S,m)$, $W$, $R$, $H$, the standard basis $\\{T_w:w\\in W\\}$, and the trace $\\tau$ be as in [[def-hh-universal-coxeter-hecke-parameters-and-presentation]], [[thm-hh-generic-coxeter-hecke-standard-basis]], and [[def-hh-canonical-hecke-trace-and-symmetrizing-form]]. Assume $W$ is finite. On the finite free $R$-module $E=\\bigoplus_{w\\in W}Re_w$, define the coordinate pairing by $\\langle e_x,e_y\\rangle=\\delta_{x,y}$ and extend $R$-bilinearly. For an endomorphism $A$ with $A(e_x)=\\sum_y a_{y,x}e_y$, define its coordinate transpose by $A^t(e_y)=\\sum_xa_{y,x}e_x$, so $\\langle Au,v\\rangle=\\langle u,A^tv\\rangle$. Let $P_s$ be the left length operator of [[lem-hh-commuting-left-right-hecke-length-operators]].\n\nFor all $x,y\\in W$,\n$$\n\\tau(T_xT_y)=\\delta_{x^{-1},y}.\n$$\nConsequently $\\tau(ab)=\\tau(ba)$ for all $a,b\\in H$, and the map $H\\to\\operatorname{Hom}_R(H,R)$, $a\\mapsto[b\\mapsto\\tau(ab)]$, is an isomorphism. The basis dual to $\\{T_w:w\\in W\\}$ for this pairing is $\\{T_{w^{-1}}:w\\in W\\}$; it is the standard basis indexed by inverses, not the algebra-inverse family $\\{T_w^{-1}\\}$.\n\nFor every unital homomorphism of commutative rings $\\phi:R\\to R'$, the induced canonical trace on $R'\\otimes_RH$ has the same basis-pair formula and is again symmetric and perfect. No semisimplicity, field, or nondegeneracy hypothesis is required.",
