            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-smith-normal-form-existence-over-a-pid",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every rectangular matrix over a PID is equivalent to a Smith diagonal matrix. Equivalently, for every $A\\in M_{m\\times n}(R)$ there are invertible $P,Q$ such that\n\n$$PAQ=\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\ldots,d_r,0,\\ldots,0),\\qquad d_1\\mid\\cdots\\mid d_r,$$\n\nwith every $d_i\\ne0$. This is an existence theorem over a PID and does not assert a Euclidean row-reduction algorithm.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "First derive the residue and valuation consequences needed below from [F2]. If $0\\ne x\\in\\mathcal O$, its leading exponent $r$ is nonnegative because $|x|_3\\le1$; the expansion gives $x=a_0+\\pi y$ with $a_0\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$ and $y\\in\\mathcal O$, since the shifted tail is a limit of partial sums in the closed unit ball. Distinct such digits represent distinct residue classes, since a nonzero difference in $\\{\\pm1,\\pm2\\}$ has absolute value $1$, whereas every element of $\\pi\\mathcal O$ has absolute value at most $1/3$. Thus $\\mathcal O/\\pi\\mathcal O$ has exactly the three classes $0,1,2$ with the operations of $\\mathbb F_3$. For a nonzero $x\\in\\mathcal O$ with leading exponent $r$, the shifted expansion gives $x=\\pi^r u$ with $u=a_r+\\pi z$, $a_r\\in\\{1,2\\}$ and $z\\in\\mathcal O$. The leading digit gives $|u|_3=|u^{-1}|_3=1$, so [F1] gives $u,u^{-1}\\in\\mathcal O$ and $u$ is a unit. Every nonzero ideal of $\\mathcal O$ therefore contains $\\pi^r$ for the minimum valuation $r$ of its elements, and every element of that ideal is divisible by $\\pi^r$ in $\\mathcal O$. Thus every ideal is principal and $\\mathcal O$ is a PID. After scaling any lattice $M$ into $\\mathcal O^2$, [F3] gives an $\\mathcal O$-basis $f_1,f_2$ of $\\mathcal O^2$ with $M=\\mathcal O\\pi^a f_1+\\mathcal O\\pi^b f_2$, where $0\\le a\\le b$ after absorbing unit factors into the basis. Scaling to $a=0\\le b$ gives a primitive representative and an exponent $n=b-a$. This exponent is well defined on the homothety class: a primitive representative has minimum coordinate valuation zero, so any scalar multiple that is also primitive differs by an $\\mathcal O$-unit. For a basis matrix of the primitive representative, the determinant valuation is $n$; changing either $\\mathcal O$-basis multiplies the determinant by a unit. Neighbors of $[L]$ correspond to the four lines in $L/\\pi L\\cong\\mathbb F_3^2$, because the inverse image of a line is an intermediate lattice. Distinct lines give distinct lattices, and they cannot become homothetic: all four inverse images have the same index $3$ in $L$, so a scalar relating two of them has valuation zero and is a unit, which preserves each lattice.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Relative to the base vertex $[\\mathcal O^2]$, a vertex with normalized elementary divisors $(0,n)$ has a chain of length $n$ through $\\mathcal O f_1+\\pi^j\\mathcal O f_2$. If $n>0$, exactly one of its four neighbors has distance parameter $n-1$: in the basis $f_1,\\pi^n f_2$ of its representative, the line spanned by $\\pi^n f_2$ has inverse image $\\pi\\mathcal O f_1+\\pi^n\\mathcal O f_2$, whose homothety class normalizes to parameter $n-1$. Every other line has inverse image with a primitive vector $f_1+t\\pi^n f_2$ and second elementary divisor $n+1$, hence parameter $n+1$. At $n=0$ all neighbors have parameter $1$. Therefore the parameter changes by one at each step, and the displayed chain is a shortest path. A cycle would have a vertex of maximal parameter and two distinct adjacent vertices of parameter one less, contradicting uniqueness of the decreasing neighbor. Thus the graph is connected and has no cycle.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Replacing a lattice basis multiplies its determinant by an $\\mathcal O$-unit; homothety adds twice an integer to its determinant valuation. Hence type is well defined, and determinant-one matrices preserve it. For an edge with the endpoint of type $0$ first, choose a basis $f_1,f_2$ of its first lattice with second lattice $\\mathcal O f_1+\\pi\\mathcal O f_2$. Its determinant has even valuation. Rescale both vectors by a power of $\\pi$ to make that determinant a unit and then multiply $f_1$ by its inverse; neither operation changes the edge, and the resulting change-of-basis matrix has determinant one. This proves transitivity. A determinant-one matrix fixes $[L_0]$ exactly when it preserves $L_0$ (homothety would change determinant valuation unless its scalar is a unit). Within $\\operatorname{SL}_2(\\mathcal O)$, preservation of $L_1$ is exactly $c\\in\\pi\\mathcal O$, proving the stated stabilizer.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
