        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The unique shortest edge gallery from $E$ to any other edge changes endpoint types alternately: changing twice at the same endpoint would shorten it. For a gallery proceeding along $L_0,L_1,\\ldots,L_n$, the matrices $\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\\\pi^n b&1\\end{pmatrix}$ for $b\\in\\mathcal O$ fix every earlier vertex and act transitively on the three possible next edges. In the basis $e_1,\\pi^n e_2$, the backward edge corresponds to the line spanned by the second basis vector; the other lines are spanned by $e_1+t\\pi^n e_2$ for $t\\in\\mathbb F_3$, and the displayed matrices send $t$ to $t+b$. For the other initial direction the apartment chain is $L_1,L_0,L_{-1},\\ldots,L_{-n}$; use instead $\\begin{pmatrix}1&\\pi^n b\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ at $L_{-n}$, which fixes that earlier chain and is transitive on the three forward lines there. Inductively move each gallery onto the corresponding apartment segment while fixing its earlier segment. Hence $I$ is transitive on the edges with each alternating type word. There are three choices at each extension, so the orbit size is $3^n$. Different words have different type sequences or distances and cannot be in the same orbit. By step 2.2 edges identify with $G/I$, so these orbits are exactly the double cosets.",
