          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Average any inner product on $V=Y\\otimes\\mathbb R$ over the finite group $W_0$. Each reflection is then orthogonal, has normal line $\\mathbb R\\alpha^\\vee$, and fixes $\\ker\\alpha$, so $\\langle\\alpha,v\\rangle=2(v,\\alpha^\\vee)/(\\alpha^\\vee,\\alpha^\\vee)$. Put $a_\\alpha=2\\alpha^\\vee/(\\alpha^\\vee,\\alpha^\\vee)$. Its metric coroot is exactly $\\alpha^\\vee$, and $(v,a_\\alpha)=\\langle\\alpha,v\\rangle$. Thus [F2] identifies the $a_\\alpha$ as a reduced crystallographic root system on $E=\\mathbb R\\Phi^\\vee$, with the chosen positive system. The orthogonal complement $C=E^\\perp$ is exactly the common root kernel and is fixed by $W_0$. Its presence adds a Cartesian factor $C$ to every alcove and changes neither the reflecting walls nor their combinatorics on $E$. Apply [F3]–[F4] on $E$ to obtain the asserted Coxeter system, simple transitivity and length for $Q^\\vee\\rtimes W_0$ on $V=E\\oplus C$. The base alcove is equivalently $0<\\langle\\alpha,v\\rangle<1$ for all positive $\\alpha$: no integer wall cuts this region; every alcove in the positive chamber touching $0$ must satisfy these inequalities, and the supplied fundamental-alcove theorem identifies it with this region.",
