          "claim": "A backtrack crosses the same wall twice in opposite directions, so its signed product is $N_sN_s^{-1}=1$ or $N_s^{-1}N_s=1$. To check a braid move of length $m<\\infty$, examine the associated $2m$-sector dihedral residue. Project a point sufficiently far in $D$ to a plane transverse to its common codimension-two face. The $m$ wall lines through the origin separate that plane into the $2m$ sectors of [F1]. On traversing their cycle once, the signs are $m$ consecutive plus signs followed by $m$ consecutive minus signs, up to cyclic rotation: the roots defining the crossed sides run consecutively through a half-circle, and the sign of their pairing with the projected point changes exactly at the two bounding rays of a half-plane. Starting at the beginning of the plus block, the positive product is one alternating word of length $m$ and the negative product is the inverse of the other alternating word. The Hecke braid relation makes their product $1$. Every cyclic rotation also has product $1$, since every factor is invertible. Cutting the cycle into the two gallery arcs of the braid move proves equality of their signed products. This also proves the assertion for a residue translated away from the base alcove.",
