        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-hh-sl2-q3-lattice-tree-and-iwahori-orbits",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Put $F=\\mathbb Q_3$, $\\mathcal O=\\mathbb Z_3$, and $\\pi=3$. Vertices are homothety classes $[L]$ of rank-two $\\mathcal O$-lattices in $F^2$. Join $[L]$ and $[M]$ when representatives satisfy $\\pi L\\subsetneq M\\subsetneq L$. This graph is a tree of degree $4$. The type of $[g\\mathcal O^2]$ is $v_3(\\det g)\\bmod2$; it is well defined. The group $G=\\operatorname{SL}_2(F)$ preserves types and is transitive on edges with type-ordered endpoints. Write $L_j=\\mathcal O e_1+\\pi^j\\mathcal O e_2$ and $E=([L_0],[L_1])$. Its stabilizer is\n$$I=\\left\\{\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\operatorname{SL}_2(\\mathcal O):c\\in\\pi\\mathcal O\\right\\}.$$\nThe $I$-orbits on edges are indexed by finite alternating words in $s_0,s_1$ (including the empty word), where $s_1$ changes an edge through its type-$0$ endpoint and $s_0$ through its type-$1$ endpoint. An orbit of word length $n$ has $3^n$ edges. These are precisely the double cosets $IgI/I$.",
