      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The determinant equation makes $G$ a closed subspace of $\\mathbb Q_3^4$, hence it is LCH by [F4]; matrix operations and inversion are continuous. The subgroup $I$ is open and closed in the compact space $\\mathbb Z_3^4$, hence compact. By [F5] choose left Haar measure. Its value on $I$ is finite and positive: if it were zero, every compact support of a continuous test function would have a finite cover by translates of the open subgroup $I$, making every Haar integral zero. Rescale to $\\mu(I)=1$. This is the only use of arbitrary choice beyond the countable compactness input in [F4]. For $m\\ge1$, let $K_m=\\{g\\in\\operatorname{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z_3):g\\equiv I_2\\pmod{\\pi^m}\\}$. These are kernels of reduction homomorphisms, hence subgroups. Since $\\mathbb Z_3$ is compact open, $\\operatorname{SL}_2(\\mathbb Z_3)$ is compact open in $G$; the entrywise congruence conditions make each $K_m$ compact open, and the matrix topology shows that $(K_m)$ is a neighborhood basis at the identity. For a locally constant compactly supported integrand, each point of its compact support has a right $K_m$-coset on which it is constant; a finite subcover and the nested subgroup basis give a finite coset partition on which it is constant. Thus convolution integrals and their associativity calculation reduce to finite sums; the change of variables $y=zu$ uses left invariance of $\\mu$. Compact support and local constancy are preserved.",
