        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The displayed $s_1$ fixes $[L_0]$ and sends the base edge through that type-$0$ vertex; $s_0=D s_1D^{-1}$ with $D=\\operatorname{diag}(1,\\pi)$ fixes $[L_1]$ and changes the base edge through type $1$. Their squares are $-I\\in I$. The three right cosets in each simple double coset are explicitly $$Is_1I=\\coprod_{b\\in\\mathbb F_3}\\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}s_1I,\\qquad Is_0I=\\coprod_{b\\in\\mathbb F_3}\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\\\pi b&1\\end{pmatrix}s_0I.$$ For the first, reduce the action on the four lines at $[L_0]$: $I$ fixes the line $e_1$ and upper unipotents translate the other three. For the second, use the basis $e_1,\\pi e_2$ at $[L_1]$, where lower unipotents translate the three lines other than the backward one. These matrices have entries in $I$, their three images are distinct, and [F1] shows they exhaust the orbit.",
