        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Under $G/I$ identified with type-ordered edges, the operator given by $1_{Is_iI}$ sums over the three other edges at the endpoint of the indicated type. Two such moves at the same vertex return to the original edge in three ways and reach any specified other edge in two ways. Equivalently the finite right-coset formula for convolution gives $1_{Is_iI}*1_{Is_iI}=3e+2\\,1_{Is_iI}$. A product with alternating endpoint types follows the unique shortest gallery in the tree. There is exactly one intermediate gallery to any specified final edge of that type word, so the product of the simple characteristic functions is the characteristic function of its entire double coset, with coefficient one. By [F1] these exhaust all double cosets. The infinite dihedral group has no finite braid relation between the two generators, so the quadratic identities give an algebra map from [F2] onto the corner. The basis of [F3] maps bijectively to the distinct double-coset characteristic functions, proving injectivity as well as surjectivity. Rescaling its quadratic identities yields the displayed symmetric normalization.",
