    "lem-hh-affine-hecke-parabolic-weight-filtration": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-hh-affine-hecke-general-root-data-bernstein-pbw",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R=(X,\\Phi,Y,\\Phi^\\vee,\\Delta)$ be any reduced based root datum, and give its canonical extended affine Weyl group arbitrary positive affine Hecke parameters invariant under extended conjugation ([[def-hh-affine-hecke-algebra-from-root-datum]]). Write $v_\\alpha$ for the parameter of the finite simple reflection $s_\\alpha$. In the formal Bernstein algebra of [[def-hh-affine-hecke-bernstein-presentation]], attach its exceptional parameters to this affine system by requiring $v'_\\alpha=v(H_{\\alpha,1})$ for $\\alpha\\in\\Delta\\cap2X$, where $v(H)$ is the parameter of reflection in the wall $H$.\n\nFor $y\\in Y$, write $y=\\lambda-\\mu$ with $\\lambda,\\mu$ dominant lattice elements. The element\n$$\\Theta_y:=N_{t_\\lambda}N_{t_\\mu}^{-1}$$\nis independent of these choices and satisfies $\\Theta_y\\Theta_z=\\Theta_{y+z}$. Its values are linearly independent, and both\n$$\\{\\Theta_yN_w:y\\in Y,\\ w\\in W_0\\},\\qquad\\{N_w\\Theta_y:y\\in Y,\\ w\\in W_0\\}$$\nare complex vector-space bases of the Iwahori–Matsumoto algebra. The assignment $\\theta_y\\mapsto\\Theta_y$, $N_s\\mapsto N_s$ gives an isomorphism from the attached formal Bernstein algebra to the Iwahori–Matsumoto algebra. In particular both ordered families $\\theta_yN_w$ and $N_w\\theta_y$ are bases of the former. No restriction to an irreducible or semisimple datum, to equal parameters, or to parameters greater than one is imposed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-root-datum-combinatorics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R=(X,\\Phi,X^\\vee,\\Phi^\\vee)$ be a reduced root datum ([[def-abstract-root-datum-and-its-weyl-group]]). Then: (a) $W(R)$ is finite and generated by the reflections $s_\\alpha$, $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$; for a base $\\Delta\\subseteq\\Phi$ it is generated by the simple reflections $s_\\alpha$, $\\alpha\\in\\Delta$; (b) the correspondences between bases, positive systems and Weyl chambers are bijective, and $W(R)$ acts simply transitively on the set of Weyl chambers, equivalently on the set of bases; (c) for a chosen positive system $\\Phi^+$ there is a unique longest element $w_0\\in W(R)$ with $w_0(\\Phi^+)=-\\Phi^+$, and for every $w\\in W(R)$ the integer $n(w)=|\\Phi^+\\cap w(\\Phi^-)|$ equals the length of $w$ with respect to the simple reflections, with $n(w^{-1})=n(w)$ and $n(w_0w)=|\\Phi^+|-n(w)$; (d) $\\Phi$ spans $V=X\\otimes\\mathbb Q$ iff the root datum is semisimple, and then $\\Phi$ is a reduced root system in $V$ in the Euclidean sense.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-hh-affine-hecke-parabolic-subalgebras-and-central-characters",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be the complex affine Hecke algebra of a reduced based root datum with positive real parameters and Bernstein PBW as in [[thm-hh-affine-hecke-general-root-data-bernstein-pbw]]. To keep the page's dual convention explicit, put $L=Y$, $B=\\Delta^\\vee\\subset L$, and write $\\beta^\\vee=\\alpha\\in X$ when $\\beta=\\alpha^\\vee\\in B$. Thus $A=\\mathbb C[L]$ is the Bernstein subalgebra, $T=\\operatorname{Hom}(L,\\mathbb C^\\times)$, and $E=\\operatorname{Hom}(L,\\mathbb R)=X\\otimes\\mathbb R$. The Weyl group $W=W_0$ acts on all these objects. Index parabolic subsets by $P\\subseteq B$; they correspond bijectively to subsets of $\\Delta$ by $\\alpha\\leftrightarrow\\alpha^\\vee$.\n\nWrite $W_P=\\langle s_\\beta:\\beta\\in P\\rangle$, and let $H_P$ denote the subalgebra of $H$ generated by $A$ and $N_{s_\\beta}$ for $\\beta\\in P$. In particular $H_\\varnothing=A$ and $H_B=H$. The notation $H_P$ here always retains the full lattice $L$. For an $H_P$-module $V$, its **parabolic induction** is $I_P(V)=H\\otimes_{H_P}V$ with $H$ acting on the first factor.\n\nAll modules in the classification below are nonzero, unital, complex and finite dimensional. For an $A$-module $V$ and $t\\in T$, the **generalized weight space** is\n$$\nV_t=\\{v\\in V:(\\theta_\\ell-t(\\ell))^{\\dim V}v=0\\text{ for every }\\ell\\in L\\}.\n$$\nThe **weight set** is $\\operatorname{Wt}(V)=\\{t:V_t\\ne0\\}$. The logarithmic absolute value $\\lambda(t)\\in E$ is $\\lambda(t)(\\ell)=\\log|t(\\ell)|$. For an irreducible $H_P$-module, the **central weight character** $\\operatorname{cc}_P(V)$ is its $W_P$-orbit of $A$-weights; the fact that there is exactly one such orbit is proved in [[lem-hh-affine-hecke-parabolic-weight-filtration]]. This terminology specifies the character of $A^{W_P}$; no equality with a larger center is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-hh-affine-hecke-weight-temperedness-and-positive-position",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use $L,B,E,W,H_P,T$ from [[def-hh-affine-hecke-parabolic-subalgebras-and-central-characters]]. Fix a positive definite $W$-invariant inner product on $E$, obtained by averaging any positive definite form over the finite group $W$ ([[lem-root-datum-combinatorics]]). For $P\\subseteq B$ put\n$$\nE_P=\\operatorname{span}_{\\mathbb R}\\{\\beta^\\vee:\\beta\\in P\\},\\qquad E^P=E_P^\\perp=\\{\\nu:\\beta(\\nu)=0\\text{ for every }\\beta\\in P\\},\\qquad K_P=-\\sum_{\\beta\\in P}\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\beta^\\vee.\n$$\nIn particular $K_\\varnothing=\\{0\\}$. Set\n$$\nC=\\{\\nu\\in E:\\beta(\\nu)\\ge0\\text{ for all }\\beta\\in B\\},\\qquad C_P^{++}=\\{\\nu\\in E^P:\\beta(\\nu)>0\\text{ for all }\\beta\\in B\\setminus P\\}.\n$$\nA finite dimensional $H_P$-module is **tempered** if all its logarithmic weights belong to $K_P$. It is **essentially tempered with real exponent $\\nu$** if $\\nu\\in E^P$ and every logarithmic weight belongs to $\\nu+K_P$. For a nonzero module this exponent is unique because $E^P\\cap E_P=0$. An irreducible essentially tempered module is in **strictly positive position** if $\\nu\\in C_P^{++}$. A **Langlands datum** is a pair $(P,\\sigma)$ with $\\sigma$ such a module. Its **standard module** is $I_P(\\sigma)$.\n\nThese are the weight definitions of temperedness and essential temperedness; no analytic characterization is presumed. For the full algebra the tempered condition is equivalently $|t(\\ell)|\\le1$ for every weight $t$ and every $\\ell\\in L^+=\\{\\ell:\\langle\\ell,\\beta^\\vee\\rangle\\ge0\\ (\\beta\\in B)\\}$. The equivalence follows because this rational polyhedral cone is generated as a real cone by its lattice points, and its polar cone is $K_B$. For $H_P$ use $P$ in place of $B$. The lattice-point assertion follows by expressing the cone as its central linear space plus nonnegative rational fundamental-weight rays and clearing denominators. The polar assertion also follows directly by expanding in the basis of simple coroots and testing the dual fundamental weights and both signs of every central vector.\n\nThe definition includes the whole central real exponent: when $P=B$, strict positivity imposes no additional inequality and $C_B^{++}=E^B$. Unitary central characters remain part of $\\sigma$. Thus no uniqueness of a choice of unitary twist or of a semisimple-parabolic torus factorization is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Every right coset of $W_P$ has a minimal-length representative $w$; minimality gives $w(P)\\subset R_L^+$, since otherwise right multiplication by a corresponding simple reflection decreases its inversion number. Such a $w$ sends all positive subsystem roots to positive roots. Along a reduced word for $v\\in W_P$, inversion numbers therefore increase at each step of $wv$, giving $\\ell(wv)=\\ell(w)+\\ell(v)$. Conversely this equality shows $w$ is the unique minimum in its coset: if $u=wv$ were another minimum its length would be $\\ell(w)+\\ell(v)$, forcing $v=1$. Thus $W^P$ is exactly this set of representatives. Applying [F1] to finite products and the cross-relations shows $H_P$ has basis $N_v\\theta_\\ell$ for $v\\in W_P$, $\\ell\\in L$: these elements span the subalgebra because cross-relations and finite Hecke multiplication keep all finite Weyl indices in $W_P$, and are independent as a subset of the PBW basis of $H$. The length-additive factorization $N_{wv}=N_wN_v$ now proves $H=\\bigoplus_{w\\in W^P}N_wH_P$. Tensoring gives $I_P(V)=\\bigoplus_{w\\in W^P}N_w\\otimes V$, including the injected identity summand, which is $H_P$-stable.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
