    "ex-hh-affine-b2-unequal-parameters": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-abstract-root-datum-and-its-weyl-group",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **root datum** is a quadruple $(X,\\Phi,X^\\vee,\\Phi^\\vee)$ consisting of free $\\mathbb Z$-modules $X,X^\\vee$ of finite rank in perfect duality $\\langle\\ ,\\ \\rangle:X\\times X^\\vee\\to\\mathbb Z$, finite subsets $\\Phi\\subseteq X$ and $\\Phi^\\vee\\subseteq X^\\vee$, and a bijection $\\alpha\\mapsto\\alpha^\\vee$ from $\\Phi$ to $\\Phi^\\vee$, subject to (rd1) $\\langle\\alpha,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle=2$ for all $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$; (rd2) the reflections $s_\\alpha(x)=x-\\langle x,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle\\alpha$ and $s_{\\alpha^\\vee}(y)=y-\\langle\\alpha,y\\rangle\\alpha^\\vee$ satisfy $s_\\alpha(\\Phi)\\subseteq\\Phi$ and $s_{\\alpha^\\vee}(\\Phi^\\vee)\\subseteq\\Phi^\\vee$; (rd3) the group $W(R)$ generated by the $s_\\alpha$ acting on $X$ is finite. The root datum is **reduced** if $\\mathbb Q\\alpha\\cap\\Phi=\\{\\pm\\alpha\\}$ for every $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$. The group $W(R)$ is the **Weyl group**, $\\Phi$ the root system and $\\Phi^\\vee$ the coroot system; $W(R)$ acts dually on $X^\\vee$ by the $s_{\\alpha^\\vee}$, and $s_\\alpha$ fixes the hyperplane $\\langle\\ ,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle=0$. For $V_{\\mathbb R}=X\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb R$, the **Weyl chambers** are the connected components of $V_{\\mathbb R}\\smallsetminus\\bigcup_{\\alpha\\in\\Phi}\\{x:\\langle x,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle=0\\}$, and a **base** is a linearly independent subset $\\Delta\\subseteq\\Phi$ such that every root is a $\\mathbb Z$-combination of $\\Delta$ with all coefficients of one sign. A root datum is **semisimple** if $Z\\Phi$ has finite index in $X$.\n\nThe reflection $s_\\alpha$ depends only on the pair $(\\alpha,\\alpha^\\vee)$: it fixes the hyperplane $\\langle\\ ,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle=0$, sends $\\alpha$ to $-\\alpha$ by (rd1), and is determined by those two conditions on the rational direct sum $\\mathbb Q\\alpha\\oplus\\ker(\\langle\\ ,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle:X\\otimes\\mathbb Q\\to\\mathbb Q)$. The lattice form above is related to the Euclidean root-system theory of [[def-weyl-group-of-a-root-system]] and [[def-positive-system-and-base-of-simple-roots]] through a $W$-invariant form on $V_{\\mathbb R}$, but no choice of invariant inner product belongs to the definition. The perfect character–cocharacter pairing for a split torus is supplied by [[lem-character-and-cocharacter-lattices-of-a-split-torus]]; roots and coroots are additional data, not supplied by that lattice lemma.\n\nAn **isomorphism of root data** $R=(X,\\Phi,X^\\vee,\\Phi^\\vee)\\to R_1=(X_1,\\Phi_1,X_1^\\vee,\\Phi_1^\\vee)$ is a pair of $\\mathbb Z$-linear isomorphisms $f:X\\to X_1$ and $f^\\vee:X_1^\\vee\\to X^\\vee$ that are transpose to one another, $\\langle f(x),y_1\\rangle=\\langle x,f^\\vee(y_1)\\rangle$ for all $x\\in X$, $y_1\\in X_1^\\vee$, and that carry $\\Phi$ bijectively onto $\\Phi_1$ with $f(\\alpha)^\\vee=f^\\vee{} ^{-1}(\\alpha^\\vee)$ for every $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$. Composites of isomorphisms are isomorphisms, with the evident inverses, so root data form a category whose isomorphisms are these pairs; this is the notion of isomorphism used when separating the root data of SL_2 and PGL_2.",
