    "cex-rational-cherednik-category-o-not-semisimple-at-half": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-rational-cherednik-reflection-data-and-parameters",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathfrak h$ be a finite-dimensional complex vector space and let $W\\subseteq\\operatorname{GL}(\\mathfrak h)$ be a finite subgroup generated by **complex reflections**, meaning elements $s\\ne1$ for which $\\operatorname{rank}(1-s|\\mathfrak h)=1$. Let $S$ be the set of all such elements in $W$. The dual space is $\\mathfrak h^*=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathbb C}(\\mathfrak h,\\mathbb C)$, with the contragredient $W$-action $(w x)(y)=x(w^{-1}y)$.\n\nFor each $s\\in S$, choose a nonzero $\\alpha_s\\in\\mathfrak h^*$ spanning the one-dimensional image of $1-s$ on $\\mathfrak h^*$. Since that image has dimension one, there is a unique $\\alpha_s^\\vee\\in\\mathfrak h$ such that\n$$s x=x-\\alpha_s^\\vee(x)\\alpha_s\\qquad(x\\in\\mathfrak h^*).$$\nReplacing $\\alpha_s$ by $a\\alpha_s$ replaces $\\alpha_s^\\vee$ by $a^{-1}\\alpha_s^\\vee$, so the product $\\alpha_s(y)\\alpha_s^\\vee(x)$ is independent of this choice.\n\nPut $\\mathfrak V=\\mathfrak h\\oplus\\mathfrak h^*$. Define an alternating form $\\omega_s$ on $\\mathfrak V$ by\n$$\\omega_s(y+x,y'+x')=\\alpha_s(y)\\alpha_s^\\vee(x')-\\alpha_s(y')\\alpha_s^\\vee(x),$$\nfor $y,y'\\in\\mathfrak h$ and $x,x'\\in\\mathfrak h^*$. Its radical is $\\ker(1-s|\\mathfrak V)$, and its restriction to $\\operatorname{im}(1-s|\\mathfrak V)$ is nondegenerate; that image has dimension two. The form $\\omega(y+x,y'+x')=x'(y)-x(y')$ is the nondegenerate $W$-invariant alternating form on $\\mathfrak V$.\n\nA reflection parameter is a function $c:S\\to\\mathbb C$ constant on $W$-conjugacy classes. The pair $(W,\\mathfrak h)$ and such a function $c$ are the reflection data and parameters used below.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-rational-cherednik-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the data of [[def-rational-cherednik-reflection-data-and-parameters]]. The tensor algebra $T(\\mathfrak V)=\\bigoplus_{n\\ge0}\\mathfrak V^{\\otimes n}$ is the unital associative algebra generated by $\\mathfrak V$ with no relations. The smash product $T(\\mathfrak V)\\rtimes\\mathbb C W$ is the vector space $T(\\mathfrak V)\\otimes\\mathbb C W$ with multiplication determined by $(a w)(b v)=a\\,w(b)\\,(wv)$, for $a,b\\in T(\\mathfrak V)$ and $w,v\\in W$.\n\nFor $t\\in\\mathbb C$, define $H_{t,c}(W,\\mathfrak h)$ to be the quotient of this smash product by the two-sided ideal generated by\n$$[x,x']=0,\\qquad [y,y']=0,\\qquad [y,x]=t\\,x(y)1-\\sum_{s\\in S}c_s\\alpha_s(y)\\alpha_s^\\vee(x)s,$$\nfor all $x,x'\\in\\mathfrak h^*$ and $y,y'\\in\\mathfrak h$. Here $[a,b]=ab-ba$. The product $\\alpha_s(y)\\alpha_s^\\vee(x)$ is the normalization from the data item; the parameter $c_s$ is constant on conjugacy classes. This quotient is an associative unital complex algebra by construction. The case $t=1$ is denoted $H_{1,c}$ and is used for category O.\n\nThis definition makes no PBW or noncollapse assertion. Those are proved in [[thm-rational-cherednik-pbw-theorem]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-rational-cherednik-pbw-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(W,\\mathfrak h)$ and $c:S\\to\\mathbb C$ be as in [[def-rational-cherednik-reflection-data-and-parameters]], and let $t\\in\\mathbb C$. For $H_{t,c}(W,\\mathfrak h)$ of [[def-rational-cherednik-algebra]], choose any ordered basis $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ of $\\mathfrak h^*$ and its dual basis $y_1,\\ldots,y_n$ of $\\mathfrak h$. Then the elements\n$$x_1^{a_1}\\cdots x_n^{a_n}\\,w\\,y_1^{b_1}\\cdots y_n^{b_n}\\qquad(a_i,b_i\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\ w\\in W)$$\nform a complex-vector-space basis. Equivalently, multiplication induces an isomorphism of vector spaces\n$$\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak h^*]\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}\\mathbb C W\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak h]\\ \\longrightarrow\\ H_{t,c}(W,\\mathfrak h).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-rational-cherednik-standard-module",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite-dimensional representation $E$ of $W$. Regard $E$ as a module over the subalgebra $\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak h]\\rtimes\\mathbb C W$ of $H_{1,c}(W,\\mathfrak h)$ by letting $W$ act as given and every $y\\in\\mathfrak h$ act as zero. The **standard module** associated to $E$ is\n$$\\Delta_c(E)=H_{1,c}(W,\\mathfrak h)\\otimes_{\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak h]\\rtimes\\mathbb C W}E.$$\nBy the PBW theorem [[thm-rational-cherednik-pbw-theorem]], multiplication identifies its underlying vector space with $\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak h^*]\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}E$. This item defines the induced module and vector-space description; its membership in $\\mathcal O_c(W,\\mathfrak h)$ is proved in [[thm-rational-cherednik-standard-module-lies-in-category-o]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-rational-cherednik-category-o",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $W,\\mathfrak h,c$ as in [[def-rational-cherednik-algebra]] and consider $H_{1,c}(W,\\mathfrak h)$. By [[thm-rational-cherednik-pbw-theorem]], the subalgebra generated by $\\mathfrak h^*$ is the polynomial algebra $\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak h^*]=S(\\mathfrak h^*)$, and the subalgebra generated by $\\mathfrak h$ is $S(\\mathfrak h)$.\n\nThe **category O**, denoted $\\mathcal O_c(W,\\mathfrak h)$, is the full subcategory of left $H_{1,c}(W,\\mathfrak h)$-modules $M$ satisfying:\n\n1. $M$ is finitely generated as an $S(\\mathfrak h^*)$-module.\n2. The augmentation ideal $S(\\mathfrak h)_{>0}$ acts locally nilpotently: for every $m\\in M$, there is $N\\ge1$ such that every homogeneous polynomial in $S(\\mathfrak h)$ of degree at least $N$ kills $m$.\n\nSince $S(\\mathfrak h)$ is generated by a finite basis of $\\mathfrak h$ and its elements commute, the second condition is equivalent to each $y\\in\\mathfrak h$ acting locally nilpotently. Morphisms in $\\mathcal O_c(W,\\mathfrak h)$ are the $H_{1,c}(W,\\mathfrak h)$-module homomorphisms.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
