    "thm-cherednik-localized-monodromy-is-fully-faithful-with-subquotient-closed-image": {
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Local holomorphic horizontal frames exist for every integrable algebraic connection: on a small polydisc solve the coordinate linear systems successively with initial matrix $I$. Their Picard series converge normally on smaller polydiscs, because the length-$j$ iterated integral is bounded by $(C\\ell)^j/j!$. Integrability implies that after solving a new coordinate the previous horizontal equations still hold: their errors satisfy the homogeneous new-coordinate equation with zero initial values, hence vanish by uniqueness. Invertibility follows by solving the inverse equation. Thus an intertwiner of monodromy extends uniquely by parallel transport to a single-valued equivariant holomorphic horizontal map $F$; two paths give the same map because their monodromies are intertwined.",
