          "claim": "Write $R=\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak h]$. First, $R[\\delta^{-1}]$ is a UFD: factor a numerator in the UFD $R$ from [F7]; irreducible factors dividing $\\delta$ become units, and every other irreducible $p$ remains prime after localization because $p\\nmid\\delta$. Indeed, if $p$ divides a product in $R[\\delta^{-1}]$, clearing powers of $\\delta$ and using primality of $p$ in $R$ shows it divides one numerator. Existence and uniqueness of factorization in $R$ therefore give factorization and uniqueness in $R[\\delta^{-1}]$. The rational horizontal map constructed above is regular on all of $\\mathfrak h_{\\rm reg}$. Indeed use local algebraic bundle frames; its entries remain rational and are analytically holomorphic. A reduced denominator in this localized polynomial UFD cannot have a noninvertible irreducible factor: at a smooth point of its hypersurface away from the numerator's zero set it would give an analytic pole. Represent such a denominator factor, up to a unit, by an irreducible polynomial $p\\in R$ with $p\\nmid\\delta$. Choose an algebraic bundle-frame open meeting the generic point of $V(p)$; its defining factors are not divisible by $p$. After clearing powers of $\\delta$, combine the reduced numerator and these finitely many factors to avoid into a nonzero polynomial $q\\in R$ with $p\\nmid q$. Choose a variable $x$ in which $p$ has positive degree, put $S=\\mathbb C[\\text{the other variables}]$, and let $K=\\operatorname{Frac}(S)$. The polynomial $p$ is primitive over $S$ (otherwise its nonunit content would factor $p$ in $R$), so the Gauss-lemma argument in [F7] makes it irreducible in $K[x]$. Its derivative $\\partial_xp$ is nonzero in characteristic zero and has smaller $x$-degree, hence $p$ is coprime to $\\partial_xp$ in $K[x]$. By the choice of $q$, $p$ is also coprime to $q$, so it is coprime to $q\\partial_xp$. Bezout in that one-variable polynomial ring, followed by clearing denominators, gives $A p+B q\\partial_xp=D$ with a