          "source": "lem-rational-cherednik-dunkl-localization-and-flat-connection",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Set $P=\\mathbb C[\\mathfrak h^*]$, choose one nonzero linear form $\\alpha_H$ for each reflecting hyperplane, and put $\\delta=\\prod_H\\alpha_H$ and $\\mathfrak h_{\\rm reg}=\\{z\\in\\mathfrak h:\\delta(z)\\ne0\\}$. The assignment\n$$x\\mapsto m_x,\\qquad w\\mapsto w,\\qquad y\\mapsto T_y:=\\partial_y-\\sum_{s\\in S}c_s\\frac{\\alpha_s(y)}{\\alpha_s}(1-s)$$\ndefines an injective homomorphism $H_{1,c}(W,\\mathfrak h)\\to\\mathcal D(\\mathfrak h_{\\rm reg})\\rtimes W$, and it induces an isomorphism after inverting $\\delta$. Here $\\mathcal D(\\mathfrak h_{\\rm reg})$ is the algebra generated by regular functions and algebraic vector fields, and the crossed product uses the given action of $W$.\n\nFor every $M\\in\\mathcal O_c(W,\\mathfrak h)$, $M_{\\rm reg}=P[\\delta^{-1}]\\otimes_P M$ is finite locally free over $P[\\delta^{-1}]$ with an integrable connection from the $\\mathcal D(\\mathfrak h_{\\rm reg})$ action. The connection is $W$-equivariant. Localization and then taking a fiber with parallel transport is exact on short exact sequences in $\\mathcal O_c$.",
