    "lem-fusion-ring-character-orthogonality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-fusion-rules",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a multifusion category choose a finite supplied list $(S_i)_{i\\in I}$ of\nrepresentatives of its simple isomorphism classes. The **fusion rules** are the\nnonnegative integers $N_{ij}^k$ determined by\n\n$$[S_i][S_j]=\\sum_{k\\in I}N_{ij}^k[S_k]$$\n\nin $K_0(\\mathcal C)$. Jordan--Hölder makes the multiplicities independent of a\nchosen composition series.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-fusion-and-multifusion-category",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **multifusion category** is a finite semisimple multitensor category. A\n**fusion category** is a finite semisimple tensor category. In particular the\nunit of a fusion category is simple, whereas a multifusion unit may split.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For a basis element $y$, form $P_\\chi=\\sum_{x\\in I}\\chi(x)x^*$ in $A\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}\\mathbb C$. The coefficient of $z$ in $yP_\\chi$ is $\\sum_x\\chi(x)N_{y,x^*}^z=\\sum_u\\chi(u^*)N_{y^*,z}^{u}$ by [F1], after setting $u=x^*$. By multiplicativity and the anti-involution identity $(y^*z)^*=z^*y$, this equals $\\chi(z^*)\\chi(y)$, which is the coefficient of $z$ in $\\chi(y)P_\\chi$. Hence $yP_\\chi=\\chi(y)P_\\chi$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Applying $\\psi$ to the equality in step 1.1 gives $\\psi(y)\\psi(P_\\chi)=\\chi(y)\\psi(P_\\chi)$, where $\\psi(P_\\chi)=\\sum_x\\chi(x)\\psi(x^*)$. Since $\\chi\\ne\\psi$, some $y\\in A$ has $\\chi(y)\\ne\\psi(y)$; subtracting the two sides forces $\\psi(P_\\chi)=0$. This is the asserted sum.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
