    "def-weighted-lagrangian-ribbon-bordism": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-3-manifold-boundary-kernel-is-lagrangian",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $M$ be a compact oriented smooth 3-manifold as in [[def-oriented-smooth-cobordism]], with closed, possibly disconnected boundary $\\Sigma$, and let $i:\\Sigma\\hookrightarrow M$ be inclusion. Extend the direct-sum intersection form of [[def-intersection-form-on-the-homology-of-a-closed-oriented-surface]] to $H_1(\\Sigma;\\mathbb R)$. The boundary kernel\n\n$$\\lambda_M=\\ker(i_*:H_1(\\Sigma;\\mathbb R)\\to H_1(M;\\mathbb R))$$\n\nis Lagrangian. If $\\Sigma$ is empty, this means $\\lambda_M=0$ in the zero symplectic space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-lagrangian-relation-sends-lagrangian-to-lagrangian",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(V,\\omega_V)$ and $(W,\\omega_W)$ be finite-dimensional real symplectic vector spaces of dimensions $2n$ and $2m$. Give $\\overline V\\oplus W$ the form $-\\omega_V\\oplus\\omega_W$. If $\\Lambda\\subseteq\\overline V\\oplus W$ is Lagrangian and $L\\subseteq V$ is Lagrangian, then\n\n$$R_\\Lambda(L)=\\{y\\in W:\\text{there is }x\\in L\\text{ with }(x,y)\\in\\Lambda\\}$$\n\nis Lagrangian in $W$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-maslov-index-four-lagrangian-cocycle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(V,\\omega)$ be a finite-dimensional real symplectic vector space and let $L_1,L_2,L_3$ be Lagrangian subspaces. Put\n\n$$U_{123}=\\frac{L_1\\cap(L_2+L_3)}{(L_1\\cap L_2)+(L_1\\cap L_3)}.$$\n\nFor $a\\in L_1\\cap(L_2+L_3)$ write $a+b+c=0$ with $b\\in L_2,c\\in L_3$. The form\n\n$$\\psi_{123}([a],[a'])=\\omega(a,b')$$\n\nis well-defined, symmetric, and nondegenerate. Define $\\mu(L_1,L_2,L_3)=\\operatorname{sign}(\\psi_{123})$. For any four Lagrangians,\n\n$$\\mu(L_1,L_2,L_3)+\\mu(L_1,L_3,L_4)=\\mu(L_1,L_2,L_4)+\\mu(L_2,L_3,L_4).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-maslov-correction-composes-for-lagrangian-relations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the Wall-form Maslov convention of [[lem-maslov-index-four-lagrangian-cocycle]]. Let $B\\subseteq\\overline V\\oplus W$ be a finite-dimensional real Lagrangian relation, where the product symplectic form is $-\\omega_V\\oplus\\omega_W$. Write $B(L)=\\{w:\\exists v\\in L,(v,w)\\in B\\}$ and $B^t(Q)=\\{v:\\exists w\\in Q,(v,w)\\in B\\}$. For Lagrangians $L,P\\subseteq V$ and $Q,R\\subseteq W$,\n\n$$\\mu_V(L,P,B^t(Q))+\\mu_W(B(L),Q,R)=\\mu_W(B(P),Q,R)+\\mu_V(L,P,B^t(R)).$$\n\nConsequently, for composable Lagrangian relations $A:V_0\\to V_1$, $B:V_1\\to V_2$, $C:V_2\\to V_3$ and specified Lagrangians $\\lambda_i\\subseteq V_i$, the integer correction $m(B,A)=\\mu_{V_1}(A(\\lambda_0),\\lambda_1,B^t(\\lambda_2))$ satisfies\n\n$$m(B,A)+m(C,BA)=m(C,B)+m(CB,A).$$\n\nIt vanishes for either identity relation, is additive on direct sums, and for graphs of symplectic isomorphisms $f_1,f_2$ equals $\\mu(f_2f_1\\lambda_0,f_2\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)$. No injectivity, transversality or equal-dimension hypothesis on a general relation is imposed.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-mayer-vietoris-sequence-in-singular-homology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For an open cover $X=U\\cup V$, the sequence\n$$\\cdots\\to H_n(U\\cap V;G)\\to H_n(U;G)\\oplus H_n(V;G)\\to H_n(X;G)\\xrightarrow{\\delta}H_{n-1}(U\\cap V;G)\\to\\cdots$$\nis exact, with middle map $(u,v)\\mapsto u+v$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-collar-neighborhood-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Every smooth manifold with boundary has a smooth collar.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-homotopy-equivalences-induce-isomorphisms-on-singular-homology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f:X\\to Y$ is a homotopy equivalence, then for every $n\\geq 0$ and every\nabelian group $G$ the induced map\n$$H_n(f_\\#):H_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(X;G)\\to H_n^{\\mathrm{sing}}(Y;G)$$\nis an isomorphism.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The boundary inclusion kernel consists of pairs with $j_0x_0+j_1x_1=0$. It is Lagrangian by [F1] for $-\\omega_{\\Sigma_0}\\oplus\\omega_{\\Sigma_1}$. The linear map $(x_0,x_1)\\mapsto(x_0,-x_1)$ preserves that form and sends this kernel to $\\Lambda_M=\\{j_0x_0=j_1x_1\\}$. Thus each individual bordism defines a Lagrangian relation.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Transposing a Lagrangian relation reverses the sign of its symplectic form and preserves its Lagrangian property. Hence [F2] shows that $A(\\lambda_0)$ and $B^t(\\lambda_2)$ are Lagrangian; the third space is the specified $\\lambda_1$. The Wall form in [F3] therefore defines exactly the middle-surface integer in the Definition, without any transversality or injectivity assumption.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "Let $M=M_2\\circ M_1$. Choose an open cover by extending each piece a short distance into the other through its product collar. In the collar coordinate, pushing that short extension linearly back to the seam is a deformation retraction onto the original piece; their intersection is a surface times an interval and retracts onto the common surface. By [F5], Mayer–Vietoris therefore gives exactness of $H_1(\\Sigma_1)\\to H_1(M_1)\\oplus H_1(M_2)\\to H_1(M)$ at the middle term, with first arrow $y\\mapsto(j_1y,-j_1'y)$. If an incoming class $x_0$ and outgoing class $x_2$ become equal in $H_1(M)$, their difference $(j_0x_0,-j_2x_2)$ lies in the image of that arrow. Hence some $y$ satisfies $j_0x_0=j_1y$ inside $M_1$ and $j_1'y=j_2x_2$ inside $M_2$. Conversely these two equalities imply equality in the union. Thus $\\Lambda_M=BA$ as actual relations. Disconnected surfaces cause no exception: the proof uses exactness at the $H_1$ direct sum and makes no injectivity assertion involving $H_0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "For three composable bordisms, their underlying relations compose by step 2.2. Apply [F4] with the four specified object Lagrangians. Its identity $m(B,A)+m(C,BA)=m(C,B)+m(CB,A)$ says precisely that the total correction, and therefore the total integer weight, agrees in both bracketings. The underlying geometric gluings agree up to the collar-respecting diffeomorphism that merely changes the placement of the two seams. Thus weighted composition is associative on the stated diffeomorphism classes.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-4.1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "An identity cylinder has diagonal relation, so either one-sided correction has two equal Lagrangians and vanishes by [F4]. Its zero weight therefore gives the two identity laws. Surface homology, inclusion relations and Wall forms split as direct sums under disjoint union; [F4] makes corrections and weights add. The empty surface has zero homology and zero correction. Disjoint-union associators and symmetry cylinders permute these direct-sum data and have zero correction whenever their transported Lagrangians agree. Consequently the ordinary geometric symmetric-monoidal identifications respect all weights, units and composition laws.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-5.1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "For mapping cylinders the relations are graphs of $f_1,f_2$. The three middle Lagrangians are $f_1\\lambda_0,\\lambda_1,f_2^{-1}\\lambda_2$. Symplectic invariance in [F4] transports them by $f_2$ and gives exactly $\\mu(f_2f_1\\lambda_0,f_2\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)$. This proves the declared mapping-cylinder convention and finishes the category construction independently of the later surgery-defined RT maps.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
