          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Fix the standard oriented solid torus $D^2\\times S^1$, with $m$ the positive disk-boundary and $l$ the core direction. Its boundary orientation is $(m,l)$. The reference opposite-cap reflection acts on this basis by $r=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$. The positive Hopf gluing exchanges the meridian of one cap with the longitude of the other; its two disk-core orientations give the exchange matrix $\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]$. Removing the fixed opposite reflection gives the stated $s_+$, since $rs_+$ is that exchange matrix. This fixes which Hopf generator is meant and its linking orientation. In particular $m\\mapsto-l$, $l\\mapsto m$, rather than the inverse convention. In the capped matrix coefficient use the composition-dual test $y_j=r_j/d_j$ because $r_jb_j=d_j$. The positive Hopf graph with these two coupons evaluates to $s_{ji}/d_j$. Its closed empty-surgery normalization is $D^{-1}$ and its outgoing torus correction from [F3] is $d_j$, giving exactly $S_{ji}=s_{ji}/D$. The positive core twist is the actual solid-torus map $(z,e^{i\\phi})\\mapsto(e^{i\\phi}z,e^{i\\phi})$, so $m\\mapsto m$, $l\\mapsto l+m$ and its core normal disk makes one positive full rotation. Its ribbon operator is therefore $\\theta_i$ on the $i$ summand with no off-diagonal terms. The cylinder Gram inverse cancels as in [F3], leaving exactly $T$. When $\\lambda=\\alpha$, both terms of the correction $a(R;\\lambda,\\alpha)$ vanish by repeated arguments in [F4], so these really are the weight-zero matrices, not unspecified scalar multiples.",
