          "claim": "Normalization holds because every exponent vanishes if any argument is $0$. For bits, $b+c$ in the cocycle equations is reduced modulo $2$. The pentagon signs have exponents\n$abc+a(b+c)d+bcd=acd+bcd+abc+abd\\pmod2,$\nso its two sides agree. For the first hexagon, the ratio of the $r$-terms is $i^{-2abc}=(-1)^{abc}$, which is exactly the ratio of the $\\omega$-terms. For the second hexagon, the ratio of the $r$-terms is again $i^{-2abc}=(-1)^{abc}$, equal to $\\omega(a,b,c)\\omega(c,a,b)\\omega(a,c,b)^{-1}=(-1)^{abc}$. The twist equation is trivial if either bit is zero; at $(1,1)$ its right side is $r(1,1)^2q(1)^2=i^2i^2=1=q(0)$. Also $q(-g)=q(g)$. Thus all specified cocycle and twist identities hold.",
