      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The Wall form in [F1] is unchanged by a symplectic isomorphism: the isomorphism carries its numerator and denominator to the corresponding subspaces and preserves $\\omega(a,b')$. The cyclic map $[a]\\mapsto[b]$, with $a+b+c=0$, is a well-defined isomorphism between the cyclic Wall quotients: changing a decomposition adds an adjacent intersection, and its inverse is obtained by solving the same sum-zero equation. It preserves the form because $\\omega(b,c')=-\\omega(b,a')=\\omega(a',b)=\\omega(a,b')$. Hence $\\mu$ is cyclically invariant. If the first two Lagrangians agree, its numerator and denominator are both that Lagrangian, so the quotient is zero. Cyclic invariance therefore shows that $\\mu$ vanishes whenever any two arguments agree. Orthogonal direct sums give orthogonal direct sums of Wall quotients and forms, so their signatures add.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Set $K=\\{v:(v,0)\\in B\\}$ and $K'=\\{w:(0,w)\\in B\\}$. Both are isotropic. A vector $x$ annihilates $\\pi_VB$ exactly when $(x,0)$ annihilates $B$, hence belongs to $B=B^\\perp$. Thus $(\\pi_VB)^\\perp=K$ and $\\pi_VB=K^\\perp$; likewise $\\pi_WB=K'^\\perp$. A pair $(v,w)\\in B$ defines an isomorphism $\\phi:K^\\perp/K\\to K'^\\perp/K'$. Changing $w$ adds precisely $K'$, and changing $v$ adds $K$; the two projections are onto and these are their kernels. Isotropy of $B$ gives $\\omega_W(w,w')=\\omega_V(v,v')$, so $\\phi$ is symplectic.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "algebra",
            "construct"
