        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For a Lagrangian $L\\subseteq V$, put $L_K=((L\\cap K^\\perp)+K)/K$. It is Lagrangian in $K^\\perp/K$: the inverse image $P_L=(L\\cap K^\\perp)+K$ is isotropic, and $P_L^\\perp=(L+K)\\cap K^\\perp=(L\\cap K^\\perp)+K=P_L$. The last equality follows by writing $x=l+k$ and observing that $x,k\\in K^\\perp$ force $l\\in K^\\perp$. Here $(U\\cap T)^\\perp=U^\\perp+T^\\perp$ follows from inclusion and equal dimensions by [F3]. The relation normal form in step 1.2 gives $B(L)/K'=\\phi(L_K)$ and $B^t(Q)/K=\\phi^{-1}(Q_{K'})$. These are equalities of the actual subspaces containing $K'$ or $K$, not an assumption that relation images are injective.",
