        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Suppose $N_0,N\\subseteq V$ both contain $K$. We prove $\\mu_V(L,N_0,N)=\\mu_{K^\\perp/K}(L_K,N_0/K,N/K)$ by identifying their complete Wall forms. The left numerator $L\\cap(N_0+N)$ lies in $K^\\perp$. It projects onto the right numerator: if a representative $l+k$, with $l\\in L\\cap K^\\perp$, lies in the sum of the two reduced spaces, then $l\\in N_0+N$, since that sum contains $K$. The image of $(L\\cap N_0)+(L\\cap N)$ is precisely the right denominator: $l+k\\in N_0$ is equivalent to $l\\in N_0$, and likewise for $N$. The projection kernel $L\\cap K$ is already in this denominator. Hence the Wall quotient spaces are isomorphic. A decomposition $a+b+c=0$ projects to the corresponding decomposition, and $\\omega(a,b')$ descends to the quotient form because both entries lie in $K^\\perp$. The isomorphism is an isometry, proving equality of signatures. By step 1.1 the same reduction holds when any two arguments contain $K$.",
          "inputs": [
