          "claim": "**Regular-parameter intervals: a relative left-right trivialization proved by a vector field.** Suppose $s$ varies in a sufficiently short closed interval containing no exceptional event. The finitely many critical points have smooth disjoint branches $p_j(s)$ by the parameter implicit function theorem, and their distinct critical values $c_j(s)$ stay separated. Successive square completion, with $s$ a parameter, gives smooth local embeddings $\\psi_{j,s}$ with $F_s(\\psi_{j,s}(z))=c_j(s)+Q_j(z).$ Put $V_{j,s}=\\partial_s\\psi_{j,s}\\circ\\psi_{j,s}^{-1}$. Differentiating the last equation gives $\\partial_sF_s+dF_s(V_{j,s})=c_j'(s)$ near that critical branch. Choose a smooth scalar field $Z_s(t)$ on the value interval, supported away from $0,1$ and the fixed collar value ranges, equal to $c_j'(s)$ on disjoint neighborhoods of the values $c_j(s)$. Such a field is a finite sum of disjoint bumps following those values. Near a critical point the equation $dF_s(Y_s)=Z_s(F_s)-\\partial_sF_s$ is therefore solved by $Y_s=V_{j,s}$. Away from the critical branches it is solved by $Y_s=\\bigl(Z_s(F_s)-\\partial_sF_s\\bigr)\\frac{\\operatorname{grad}F_s}{|\\operatorname{grad}F_s|^2}.$ A partition of unity patches these solutions because the equation is linear in $Y_s$. Take $Y_s=0$ on the fixed collar: both sides vanish there. These vector fields have compact support in the interior of $W$, so their time-dependent flow $\\Phi_s$ exists throughout the short parameter interval, fixes the boundary collar, and is a diffeomorphism (use the ordinary flow theorem on the extended space with field $\\partial_s+Y_s$). Let $\\eta_s$ be the scalar flow of $Z_s$, initially the identity. Uniqueness for the scalar ODE gives $F_s\\circ\\Phi_s=\\eta_s\\circ F_{s_0}.$ Thus regular level surfaces, handle bands, and both incoming and outgoing identifications are carried by actual collar-fixed diffeomorphisms. The scalar flow is increasing and is a translation near each critical value, since $Z_s$ is locally constant in $t$ there; hence a transported Euclidean critical-point field retains its exact Morse model. There is no appeal to “structural stability” in this argument. A finite subdivision covers any compact parameter interval without events. If an attaching embedding at one slice is $S: S^{k-1}\\times D^{n-k}\\to F_{s_0}^{-1}(a)$, its transported embedding is $\\Phi_s\\circ S$, considered in the level $F_s^{-1}(\\eta_s(a))$. If its elementary outgoing identification was $d$, it becomes $\\Phi_s|_{\\mathrm{out}}\\circ d\\circ(\\Phi_s|_{\\mathrm{in}})^{-1}$, with the handle part transported by the same local coordinates. These formulas are necessary: writing only that the attaching sphere is isotopic loses the terminal boundary parametrization.",
