          "claim": "Represent a standard cap by a ball containing one coupon and its marked strands, with one attached solid cylinder for each handle; the two bases of its handle arc are adjacent on the coupon. Reverse the coupon for the opposite raw cap. For a cylinder take two such disjoint standard handlebody neighborhoods in $S^3$ and, for each handle, a zero-framed surgery annulus surrounding its two opposed handle arcs. Choose mutually disjoint products $D_r\\times[-1,1]$; inside each, the two handle arcs occupy small cylinders $B_r^-\\times[-1,1]$, $B_r^+\\times[-1,1]$ in an interior disk $D'_r\\subset D_r$. Its surgery annulus is $D_r\\setminus\\operatorname{Int}D'_r$. Zero-framed surgery replaces its thickening by $D^2\\times S^1$, attaching so that each $\\partial D'_r\\times\\{t\\}$ bounds a disk. That disk joined to the pair-of-pants $D'_r\\setminus\\operatorname{Int}(B_r^-\\cup B_r^+)$ is an annulus between the two handle boundaries. As $t$ varies these are an annulus product, and the complementary portion of the filling solid torus leaves the ordinary cylinder between the punctured sphere boundaries. The product structures use the same $t$ coordinate on their collars, so they glue to $\\Sigma_t\\times I$ with its original parametrizations. This is a local coordinate proof of this particular cylinder presentation, not a completeness assertion. Its $g$ surgery annuli are zero-framed and mutually unlinked, so $m=g$, $\\sigma(B)=0$.",
