          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-absolute-hurewicz-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge2$ and assume the Axiom of Choice. If a CW complex $X$ is $(n-1)$-connected, then for every $x\\in X$\n$$\\widetilde H_i(X;\\mathbb Z)=0\\quad(0\\le i<n),\\qquad h:\\pi_n(X,x)\\xrightarrow{\\cong}H_n(X;\\mathbb Z).$$\nThe same conclusion holds for an $(n-1)$-connected space already known to be homotopy equivalent to a CW complex, using an actual homotopy equivalence.\n\nSeparately, for $n=1$ and any nonempty path-connected space $X$, the map $h:\\pi_1(X,x)\\to H_1(X;\\mathbb Z)$ is the abelianization map: it is surjective with kernel the commutator subgroup, and $\\widetilde H_0(X;\\mathbb Z)=0$. This degree-one assertion is choice-free and requires no CW-type assumption. A bare weak CW approximation is not the hypothesis used for the CW-type transfer above.",
