          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every weak homotopy equivalence $f:X\\to Y$ of topological spaces induces isomorphisms $H_k(X;\\mathbb Z)\\to H_k(Y;\\mathbb Z)$ for all $k\\ge0$, without any choice principle. More generally, if $A\\subseteq X$, $B\\subseteq Y$, $f(A)\\subseteq B$, and both $f$ and $f|_A:A\\to B$ are weak homotopy equivalences, then the induced maps $H_k(X,A;\\mathbb Z)\\to H_k(Y,B;\\mathbb Z)$ are isomorphisms. No separation or CW hypothesis is imposed on these spaces.",
