        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Let $T_q$ be the Thom space of the universal oriented $q$-plane bundle. Its Thom cells are shifted up by $q$. Their integral cellular boundary is the base cellular boundary shifted by $q$: over an adjacent pair of base characteristic disks the fiber orientation is preserved, so the incidence degree is the base incidence degree multiplied by the fixed oriented fiber degree one. With the fiber-first convention this also identifies evaluation of $w_4\\bar u_q$ on a shifted homology class with evaluation of $w_4$ on the base class. Hence $H_{q+j}(T_q;\\mathbb Z)=H_j(BSO(q);\\mathbb Z)$, compatibly with mod-two reduction and evaluation. In particular it is $(q-1)$-connected with $H_q(T_q;\\mathbb Z)=\\mathbb Z$ on the normalized fiber generator. The stabilization $BSO(q)\\to BSO(q+1)$ is $q$-connected by the sphere fibration in [F5] and its homotopy sequence. A relative CW model therefore has no new cells of dimension at most $q$. Over each relative characteristic disk trivialize the pulled-back $(q+1)$-plane bundle; its Thom cell has dimension the disk dimension plus $q+1$. This proves that $\\Sigma T_q\\to T_{q+1}$ has no relative cells below $2q+2$, hence is an isomorphism on homotopy through $2q$. The construction uses a mapping-cylinder relative model and bundle homotopy transport, so it does not assume that particular Grassmannian Schubert structures form a literal relative cell inclusion.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
