          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $N$ be a connected nonempty compact smooth oriented three-manifold, and let $W$ be a finite four-dimensional two-handle trace from $N$, with its full external boundary collar fixed. Every smooth framed circle in $\\operatorname{int}W$ avoiding that protected collar is ambiently isotopic, with compact support outside it, to a circle in a pushed-in copy of $N$. The target circle may avoid the old finite attaching link. Its prescribed oriented rank-three normal framing is represented, up to homotopy and isotopy of its small tubular attaching embedding, by an integral rank-two framing in $N$ plus the level-normal line. No condition on $\\pi_1(N)$ or on the circle's class is imposed. More generally, freely homotopic embedded circles in a smooth manifold of dimension $d\\ge4$ are isotopic whenever their homotopy lies in a prescribed relatively compact open set; the isotopy may be supported in that set. Every loop class in a three-manifold has an embedded representative avoiding a prescribed finite link.",
