          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix a dual pair $X,X^\\vee$ with evaluation $X\\otimes X^\\vee\\to\\mathbf1$ and coevaluation $\\mathbf1\\to X^\\vee\\otimes X$. Define $r_{n,X}:R(n\\odot X)\\to Rn\\odot X$ by the following natural bijections in $m$: $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal M}(m,R(n\\odot X))\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal N}(Fm,n\\odot X)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal N}(F(m\\odot X^\\vee),n)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal M}(m\\odot X^\\vee,Rn)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal M}(m,Rn\\odot X)$. The middle identification uses $f_{m,X^\\vee}$ and the other identifications use the adjunction or the displayed duality. Every arrow is invertible, so Yoneda gives an invertible module comparison. To verify its pentagon, apply $\\operatorname{Hom}(m,-)$ to the two paths for $X,Y$. Both paths move $Y^\\vee$ and then $X^\\vee$ across Hom, apply $f$ to the resulting action, then move them back. The equality of these composites is exactly the module pentagon for $f$, since evaluation of the dual of $X\\otimes Y$ is the consecutive evaluation of $Y$ and $X$ with the associators. For $X=\\mathbf1$ the same comparison reduces to the module unit equation for $f$. This proves all module-functor axioms for $R$.",
