        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "For $(x,a)$ put $s_{(x,a)}=T(a)\\sigma_x\\eta_x:x\\to Tx$. Its composite with $a$ is $aT(a)\\sigma_x\\eta_x=a\\mu_x\\sigma_x\\eta_x=a\\eta_x=1$. It is an algebra map to $(Tx,\\mu_x)$: indeed $s a=T(a)\\sigma_x\\eta_xa=T(a)\\sigma_xT(a)\\eta_{Tx}=T(a)T^2(a)\\sigma_{Tx}\\eta_{Tx}=T(a)T(\\mu_x)\\sigma_{Tx}\\eta_{Tx}=T(a)\\sigma_x\\mu_x\\eta_{Tx}=T(a)\\sigma_x$, while $\\mu_xT(s)=\\mu_xT^2(a)T(\\sigma_x)T(\\eta_x)=T(a)\\mu_{Tx}T(\\sigma_x)T(\\eta_x)=T(a)\\sigma_x\\mu_xT(\\eta_x)=T(a)\\sigma_x$. Here the middle equalities use, in order, naturality, the algebra equation, the two bimodule equations for $\\sigma$, and the unit equations. Naturality of $s$ in algebra maps follows by the same naturality of $\\sigma,\\eta$ and $ha=bT(h)$. Thus every algebra is a natural retract of its free algebra.",
