          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Under the bijections of step 2.1, the image of the adjunction counit $\\epsilon_n:FRn\\to n$ is its tensor translate, so the equality $(\\epsilon_n\\odot1_X)f_{Rn,X}F(r_{n,X})=\\epsilon_{n\\odot X}$ holds. This is precisely module-naturality of $\\epsilon$. For the unit, apply the adjunction bijection to its two proposed module-naturality composites; both correspond to the identity of $F(m\\odot X)$ after $f$ and its inverse cancel. Faithfulness of the bijection proves their equality. Hence both triangles from step 1.1 are identities of module-natural transformations. For $L$, use the dual pair with evaluation $X^\\flat\\otimes X\\to\\mathbf1$ and coevaluation $\\mathbf1\\to X\\otimes X^\\flat$ in the identical argument with the Hom adjunction reversed: $\\operatorname{Hom}(L(n\\odot X),m)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}(n,Fm\\odot X^\\flat)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}(Ln,m\\odot X^\\flat)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}(Ln\\odot X,m)$. Its middle comparison is that of $F$. Evaluation of tensor products gives the module pentagon, the unit pair gives its unit equation, and applying this bijection proves module-naturality of its unit and counit.",
