          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "For each $x$, $L_T(x)$ is projective, since $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal M^T}(L_Tx,-)=\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal M}(x,U-)$ is exact. Hence all objects are projective by step 2.2. Let $g$ be the sum of the finitely many simples of $\\mathcal M$. Every object of $\\mathcal M^T$ is a quotient of a finite sum of copies of $L_Tg$: first its underlying object is a summand of a finite sum of copies of $g$, and then its action is an epimorphism from its free object. Also $\\operatorname{End}(L_Tg)$ is finite dimensional, being a subspace of an underlying Hom space in $\\mathcal M$. Every object has finite length, since a strict chain of subobjects remains a strict chain after the faithful exact functor $U$ and $U(x,a)=x$ has finite length. With all objects projective, all short exact sequences split, so each object is a finite sum of simples. Each simple is a summand of $L_Tg$ because its quotient from a finite sum of $L_Tg$ splits; the finite length of $L_Tg$ bounds the number of such simples. Thus $\\mathcal M^T$ is finite semisimple with finite dimensional Hom spaces.",
