          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Define $\\tau_n(t)=R_{A^{\\otimes(n-1)},A}(t)(q\\otimes\\mathrm{id})$ on $\\operatorname{Hom}(A^{\\otimes n},\\mathbf1)$, and set $t_3=b(m\\otimes\\mathrm{id})=b(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes m)$. Using the first expression and naturality in the first argument gives $\\tau_3(t_3)=b_R(q\\otimes m)=b(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes m)=t_3$. Thus $\\tau_3^2(t_3)=t_3$. On the other hand, the two-factor rotation rule gives $\\tau_3^2(t_3)=R_{A,A\\otimes A}(t_3)(q\\otimes q\\otimes\\mathrm{id})$. Using $t_3=b(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes m)$ and naturality in the last argument makes this $b_R(F(m)(q\\otimes q)\\otimes\\mathrm{id})$. The nondegenerate pairing makes $h\\mapsto b_R(h\\otimes\\mathrm{id})$ injective on $\\operatorname{Hom}(A\\otimes A,F(A))$, and $t_3=b_R(qm\\otimes\\mathrm{id})$. Therefore $qm=F(m)(q\\otimes q)$. Similarly rotating $b(\\mathrm{id}\\otimes\\eta)=\\epsilon=b(\\eta\\otimes\\mathrm{id})$ gives $b_R(F(\\eta)\\otimes\\mathrm{id})=b_R(q\\eta\\otimes\\mathrm{id})$, so $q\\eta=F(\\eta)$. Thus $q$ is an algebra isomorphism. Every expression above is typed: both inputs to $F(m)$ lie in $F(A)$, and no product of an $A$ input and an $F(A)$ input is used.",
