          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Apply this construction to $K\\in\\mathcal D$ and its Frobenius pairing. The twice-right-dual of $\\rho_V$ is a map $(F(V)\\boxtimes\\mathbf1)K^{\\vee\\vee}\\to(\\mathbf1\\boxtimes F^{-1}(V))K^{\\vee\\vee}$, because right duality in the reversed second factor is left duality in $\\mathcal C$. It is right $K^{\\vee\\vee}$-linear by twice dualizing the action square. Transport through the algebra isomorphism $q:K\\to K^{\\vee\\vee}$ gives a right $K$-linear map $\\widetilde\\rho_V:(F(V)\\boxtimes\\mathbf1)K\\to(\\mathbf1\\boxtimes F^{-1}(V))K$. Compose with $\\rho_{F^{-1}(V)}^{-1}$. Full faithfulness of $J$ gives a unique isomorphism $\\delta_V:F(V)\\to F^{-1}(V)$ whose image is this composite. The twice-dual sliding equation, transported through $q$, is the sliding equation for $\\widetilde\\rho$: internal $q^{-1},q$ pairs cancel, and the multiplication and unit vertices agree by step 2.2. Combining it with the inverse sliding equation for $\\rho$ proves the tensor equation for $\\delta$; full faithfulness reflects it in $\\mathcal C$. Thus $g_V=\\delta_{F(V)}^{-1}:V\\to F^2(V)$ is a monoidal natural isomorphism.",
