          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Monoidal natural isomorphisms of rigid tensor functors preserve duality. Here this follows directly by applying naturality and the monoidal equation for $g$ to evaluation and coevaluation: the resulting two equations say that $g_{X^\\vee}$ is the inverse dual of $g_X$, under the canonical dual constraints. Uniqueness of a dual mate follows from the snake identities, so $g_{X^\\vee}=(g_X^\\vee)^{-1}$. Applying this twice gives $g_{F(X)}=F(g_X)$. Define $\\widetilde{\\mathcal C}$ to have objects $(V,f)$ with $f:V\\to F(V)$ invertible and $F(f)f=g_V$, and arrows $h$ with $F(h)f=f'h$. Tensor using $f\\otimes f'$ and the tensor constraint of $F$; monoidality of $g$ proves closure. The inverse-dual identity just proved gives dual objects and their evaluation and coevaluation. The tautological map $f:(V,f)\\to(F(V),F(f))$ is an arrow, with its target an object by $F(g_V)=g_{F(V)}$, and it is natural and monoidal. Hence it is a pivotal structure.",
