        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The object $B_T=\\underline{\\operatorname{End}}_{\\mathcal Q}(T)$ represents $W\\mapsto\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal E}(W\\cdot T,T)$: explicitly, sum each simple object of $\\mathcal Q$ with the multiplicity of the corresponding finite-dimensional Hom space. Composition of evaluations makes $B_T$ an algebra, and $\\rho_T$ corresponds to an algebra map $A\\to B_T$. In particular $B_T$ is an $A$-bimodule. Internal-Hom adjunction and duality give isomorphisms $$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal Q}(A^{\\otimes n},B_T)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal E}(A^{\\otimes n}\\cdot T,T)\\cong C_T^n.$$ For the last map, the summand indexed by $(i_1,\\ldots,i_n)$ is the adjunction $$\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal E}(N_{i_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes i_n}TM_{(i_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes i_n)^*},T)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\mathcal E}(N_{i_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes i_n}T,TM_{i_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes i_n}).$$ The module associators identify tensor-word actions with the iterated actions in the statement. Values on simple tuples extend uniquely to natural transformations because every object is a finite direct sum of simples. Thus these maps are isomorphisms, not just injections into the cochain spaces.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Under these isomorphisms the two outer Hochschild faces compose with $\\rho_T$ on the two sides; taking their adjoints gives precisely the zeroth and last cofaces displayed in the statement. For an interior face, the component $\\sum_a p_a^r\\boxtimes(q_a^r)^*$ of multiplication inserts $\\sum_{r,a}q_a^rp_a^r=\\mathrm{id}$ in the adjacent tensor factors; its adjoint is the merge coface, transported by the module associators. The unit of $A$ is the image of the unit summand of $K$, so the normalized conditions also agree. Consequently the maps in the preceding step identify the stated normalized complex with the normalized Hochschild complex of $A$ with coefficients $B_T$. This also proves $d^2=0$ for the stated differential.",
