    "ex-real-two-dimensional-spin-factor": {
      "risk": "medium",
      "kind": "example",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-real-spin-factor",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(V,\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle)$ be a real inner-product space with the\npositive-definite symmetric form of [[def-inner-product-space]]. On\n$S(V):=V\\oplus\\mathbb R$ define\n\n$$ (v,\\alpha)\\circ(w,\\beta):=(\\alpha w+\\beta v,\\langle v,w\\rangle+\\alpha\\beta). $$\n\nThis is the **real spin-factor product**; [[thm-real-spin-factor-is-jordan]]\nproves that it is a unital Jordan algebra.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-real-spin-factor-is-jordan",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real inner-product space $V$, the product on $S(V)$ in\n[[def-real-spin-factor]] is commutative and bilinear, has unit $(0,1)$, and\nsatisfies $x\\circ(y\\circ x^2)=(x\\circ y)\\circ x^2$. Hence it is a unital real\nJordan algebra.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a real or complex inner-product space with induced length $\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle}$. Then $\\|v\\|\\ge0$ with $\\|v\\|=0$ exactly for $v=0$, $\\|\\lambda v\\|=|\\lambda|\\,\\|v\\|$ for every scalar $\\lambda$, and $\\|u+v\\|\\le\\|u\\|+\\|v\\|$; hence $\\|\\cdot\\|$ is a norm on $V$, read over $\\mathbb R$ by [[def-norm-and-normed-space]] and over $\\mathbb C$ by [[def-complex-norm-and-complex-normed-space]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-of-square-roots",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then every\n$a \\in F$ with $a \\ge 0$ has a **unique** $s \\in F$ with $s \\ge 0$ and $s^2 = a$;\nwe write $s = \\sqrt{a}$. Consequently the positive elements of $F$ are exactly the\nnonzero squares: $x > 0$ if and only if $x = y^2$ for some $y \\neq 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Substituting $u=(0,1)$, $e_1=((1,0),0)$, and $e_2=((0,1),0)$ into [F1] gives $u\\circ e_i=e_i$, $e_1^2=e_2^2=u$, and $e_1\\circ e_2=0$, and verifies that $u$ is the unit.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F1] and the standard dot product, $(v,\\alpha)^2=(2\\alpha v,x^2+y^2+\\alpha^2)$ for $v=(x,y)$. Expanding in the basis products of step 1.1 gives $(ae_1+be_2+cu)^2=2ac e_1+2bc e_2+(a^2+b^2+c^2)u$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $(w,t)=(v,\\alpha)^2$, step 2.1 gives $w=2\\alpha v$ and $t=\\|v\\|^2+\\alpha^2$. By norm homogeneity in [F3], $\\|w\\|=2|\\alpha|\\,\\|v\\|$, and $(|\\alpha|-\\|v\\|)^2\\geq0$ gives $\\|w\\|\\leq t$. Thus every square lies in the stated cone.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "Conversely, suppose $t\\geq\\|w\\|$. If $t=0$, norm separation in [F3] gives $w=0$ and $(w,t)=(0,0)^2$. If $t>0$, then $t^2-\\|w\\|^2=(t-\\|w\\|)(t+\\|w\\|)\\geq0$, so both $\\sqrt{t^2-\\|w\\|^2}$ and, after defining $s=(t+\\sqrt{t^2-\\|w\\|^2})/2>0$, $\\alpha=\\sqrt{s}$ exist by [F4]. Set $v=w/(2\\alpha)$. The quadratic equation $s^2-ts+\\|w\\|^2/4=0$ gives $s+\\|w\\|^2/(4s)=t$ and $2\\alpha v=w$, so step 2.1 gives $(v,\\alpha)^2=(w,t)$.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The equality locus of the square cone is $t=\\|w\\|$, equivalently $t\\geq0$ and $t^2-\\|w\\|^2=0$; for $w=(x,y)$ and $t=\\alpha$, this is $\\alpha\\geq0$ and $\\alpha^2=x^2+y^2$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 3.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "The function $g(w,t)=t-\\|w\\|$ is continuous in the product topology [F5], since the triangle inequality in [F3] gives $|\\|w\\|-\\|w'\\||\\leq\\|w-w'\\|$. Continuity makes $\\{g>0\\}$ and $\\{g<0\\}$ open; the first lies in the cone and the second in its complement. If $g(w,t)=0$, then $(w,t+\\varepsilon)$ is in the cone and $(w,t-\\varepsilon)$ is outside it for every $\\varepsilon>0$; both approach $(w,t)$ as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$. Hence exactly the equality locus in step 4.1 is the boundary. The algebra is Jordan by [F2].",
