          "claim": "We justify formal use by faithful bending. Suppose $V$ has the dual $V^\\vee$, evaluation $b_V:V\\otimes V^\\vee\\to\\mathbf1$ and coevaluation $d_{V^\\vee}:\\mathbf1\\to V^\\vee\\otimes V$, with both snakes proved from Frobenius and units as in this lemma step 1.1. For $f:U\\to V$ define $D(f):=b_V(f\\otimes1_{V^\\vee}):U\\otimes V^\\vee\\to\\mathbf1.$ For $h:U\\otimes V^\\vee\\to\\mathbf1$ define $J(h):=\\lambda_V(h\\otimes1_V) \\alpha^{-1}_{U,V^\\vee,V} (1_U\\otimes d_{V^\\vee})\\rho^{-1}_U:U\\to V.$ Naturality of associators/unitors and the snake on $V$ give $J(D(f))=f$. In detail the composite is the map obtained by tensoring $f$ with $d_{V^\\vee}$ and then evaluating $b_V$ on its first two factors; the snake is $\\lambda_V(b_V\\otimes1_V)\\alpha^{-1}_{V,V^\\vee,V} (1_V\\otimes d_{V^\\vee})\\rho^{-1}_V=1_V.$ Precomposing this map by $f$ proves the identity. Thus D is injective on Hom sets with no vector-space assumption. For tensor outputs use $V=V_1\\otimes V_2$, dual $V_2^\\vee\\otimes V_1^\\vee$, evaluation exactly the reversed-factor P-composite in step 3.1, and coevaluation formed by inserting $d_{V_2^\\vee}$ between the two factors of $d_{V_1^\\vee}$ (and reassociating). The two constituent snakes cancel successively, so the tensor version of D is also injective. Every pairing calculation below is an equality of maps with external tensor factors of the indicated types. For example the two sides of the module equation in the later module calculation are maps $C\\otimes A_{aa}\\to C$; applying D adds one external C factor and gives the three-composite equality displayed there. The product-transpose in step 3.1 uses the external factors $A_{ab},A_{bc},A_{ca}$; after inserting the coproduct expression from step 2.2, its contraction is precisely the second snake composed with indexed associativity. The tensor-output tests in the later transport, coalgebra and window calculations use P with both external dual factors, so injectivity of D applies. In all such displays, input letters name these external factors, juxtaposition is the displayed indexed product, and evaluation is the displayed pairing. The equalities use only the defining maps, their tensor products and structural equations; they do not assume scalar-valued points separate morphisms in a general category. Thus the displayed identities hold in the formal presentation. In the vector-space specialization, pure-tensor evaluations give the same formulas, and the additional finite-dimensional nondegeneracy assertion follows from step 1.1.",
