          "quote": "The normal word is first a **formal composite** of the ten typed surface generators, with $C$ the circle symbol and $A_{ab}$ the interval symbol $I_{ab}$. Tensor, associators, unitors and flips are those of their symmetric monoidal presentation with the elementary sewing relations. The formulas below also specify its evaluation in $\\mathrm{Vect}_{\\mathrm{fd}}(k)$ after choosing knowledgeable data. Equality of such vector-space evaluations is not used to infer equality of formal words or surfaces; that distinction is essential before the normal-form theorem.\n\nLet $M$ be a connected morphism in $\\mathsf{Cob}^{\\mathrm{oc}}_2(S)$, with the marked incoming and outgoing black boundary parameterizations of [[def-oriented-open-closed-surface-category]]. The **marked data** of $M$ are:\n\n1. the partition of its marked black boundary components into connected components of $M$;\n2. the genus $g\\ge0$ of each connected component;\n3. for each color $s\\in S$, the number $w_s\\ge0$ of free-boundary circles (windows) of color $s$ on that component;\n4. the cyclically ordered open-boundary cycles, each recorded as the ordered list of its marked interval slots together with the full endpoint-color sequence along those intervals and free edges.\n\nAn open-boundary cycle is obtained by following the oriented boundary of $M$ through alternating black interval collars and the intervening free-boundary interval faces. Its successive black slots are distinct within the cycle and each slot appears in exactly one cycle. If the cycle visits slots $j_1,\\ldots,j_q$ in boundary order, write their oriented endpoint labels after bending the black boundary as\n$$\nI_{a_0a_1}, I_{a_1a_2},\\ldots,I_{a_{q-1}a_q},\\qquad a_q=a_0.\n$$\nThe color at each join agrees because the free face has a single label. Distinct cycles may have different endpoint-color sequences even when their lengths agree.\n\nUse the snakes of [[lem-frobenius-sewing-derived-moves]] to bend every outgoing interval to an incoming interval of reversed endpoint order and every incoming circle to an outgoing circle. Thus a connected component is reduced to a typed word with $m$ incoming intervals, $n$ outgoing circles, and the same marked open-boundary cycles. Let $c_1,\\ldots,c_r$ be those cycles, ordered by their least marked interval slot; within each cycle start at its least slot. For cycle $c_j$, let its slots be $j_{j,1},\\ldots,j_{j,q_j}$ and its cyclic endpoint colors be $a_{j,0},\\ldots,a_{j,q_j}=a_{j,0}$. Define\n$$\nT_j:=i_{a_{j,0}}^*\\circ\\mu^{(q_j)}_{a_{j,0},a_{j,1},\\ldots,a_{j,q_j}}: \\bigotimes_{\\ell=1}^{q_j} A_{a_{j,\\ell-1}a_{j,\\ell}}\\longrightarrow C.\n$$\nLet $P$ be the symmetry isomorphism that permutes the $m$ interval factors into this listed cycle order, preserving each factor and its endpoint colors. Set\n$$\nT_M:=(T_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes T_r)\\circ P.\n$$\nFor $r=0$, set $T_M=1_k:k\\to k$, the identity on the empty tensor. Let $\\mu_C^{[r]}:C^{\\otimes r}\\to C$ be the iterated closed multiplication for $r\\ge2$, with $\\mu_C^{[1]}=1_C$ and $\\mu_C^{[0]}=\\eta_C$. Let $\\Delta_C^{[n]}:C\\to C^{\\otimes n}$ be the iterated closed comultiplication for $n\\ge2$, with $\\Delta_C^{[1]}=1_C$ and $\\Delta_C^{[0]}=\\epsilon_C$. The ordered product of window operators is\n$$\nW_M:=\\mathop{\\prod}_{s\\in S,\\;w_s>0} W_s^{\\,w_s}:C\\to C, \\qquad W_s=i_s^*i_s,\n$$\nwhere the product is a finite composite: compactness gives only finitely many windows on $M$. Its order is immaterial by the $C$-linearity of the $W_s$; if there are no windows, $W_M=1_C$. Let $H=\\mu_C\\Delta_C$ and define the **candidate normal word**\n$$\nN(M):=\\Delta_C^{[n]}\\circ H^g\\circ W_M\\circ\\mu_C^{[r]}\\circ T_M,\n$$\nwith the canonical associators, unitors, and symmetry maps inserted. The identity on each marked closed output slot is retained by the final output permutation. If $n=0$, the final map is $\\epsilon_C$; if $r=0$, the initial closed input is $\\eta_C$. In