        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-boundary-relative-two-dimensional-surface-normal-form",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be any set and let $M,N$ be compact oriented $S$-colored surfaces in $\\mathsf{Cob}^{\\mathrm{oc}}_2(S)$ with the same marked source and target words. Then $M$ and $N$ represent the same morphism if and only if they have the same partition of marked black boundary slots into components, the same genus and colored-window multiplicities on every component meeting a marked slot, and the same cyclic open-boundary slot cycles with their complete endpoint-color sequences, together with the same multiset of genus/window data for components meeting no marked black slot. For each connected component, the common morphism is equal to the candidate word $N(M)$ of [[def-relative-open-closed-surface-normal-form]]. For disconnected surfaces the word is the tensor product of the component words, with the marked slot permutations restored.\n\nThe claim includes components with no black boundary: their data are genus and the finite multiplicities of colored free-boundary circles, and their word is $\\epsilon_C H^g W_M\\eta_C$. No color sequence may be discarded, and the theorem makes no semisimplicity or characteristic assumption.",
          "uses": [
